window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"f":"must-know.html","t":"Must Know Quick Reference","secs":[{"s":"Must Know Quick Reference","a":"","k":["Exam Prep","Everything the instructor explicitly said would be tested or heavily hinted. A ★ means it was said out loud."]},{"s":"Week 1▾","a":"week-1","k":["Week 1▾","Topic Name","★ A short, quick-reference bullet (about 20 words).","An unstarred bullet keeps its attribution: she hinted this would show up.","Not Tested","Record every \"you don't need to memorize this\" the moment it's said."]}]},{"f":"week1-comprehensive-study-guide.html","t":"Comprehensive Study Guide","secs":[{"s":"Comprehensive Study Guide","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 1","Part 1 — Cardiovascular Labs & Diagnostics","Part 2 — CAD & Acute Coronary Syndrome","Part 3 — Pericarditis","Part 4 — Infective Endocarditis","Part 5 — Valvular Heart Disease","Self-Test Flashcards","Everything covered in Week 1, in one long page, in the order it was taught: Part 1 cardiovascular labs and diagnostics, Part 2 CAD and acute coronary syndrome, Part 3 pericarditis, Part 4 infective endocarditis, and Part 5 valvular heart di…"]},{"s":"Part 1 — Cardiovascular Labs & Diagnostics▾","a":"part-1-cardiovascular-labs-diagnostics","k":["Part 1 — Cardiovascular Labs & Diagnostics▾","From Cardiovascular Labs & Diagnostics. Built from the two Week 1 labs-and-diagnostics voiceovers: serum labs and non-invasive tests (part 1), then the invasive procedures (part 2). The instructor says you will not need to memorize the nume…","Serum Cardiac Markers","When a patient has a myocardial infarction (MI), there is heart cell death, and the markers below are what the blood draw shows.","Troponin — The most sensitive indicator of myocardial damage. Helps differentiate cardiac from non-cardiac chest pain (for example, cardiac versus GI substernal pressure). — Elevates 2 to 3 hours after the MI; returns to baseline in 10 to 1…","CK-MB — Muscle damage, but not specific to the heart. — Elevated 3 hours after an MI; stays elevated 24 hours to 2 days. — Historically important and used before troponin existed; \"NCLEX still likes to talk about it.\" Not as specific as tro…","Myoglobin — Another non-specific marker of myocardial damage (less specific than troponin). — Not given. — Mentioned only because it shows up on some standardized tests. The instructor says you don't need to know myoglobin.","Inflammation Markers: CRP & ESR","CRP (C-reactive protein)","Indicates inflammation in the body; non-specific (it doesn't say where).","More sensitive than the ESR.","Peaks within 18 to 72 hours after an MI; failure to normalize might indicate ongoing damage to the heart muscle.","With an MI there is inflammation in addition to heart cell death.","Monitors more closely for the acute picture.","ESR (sed rate)","Also a non-specific indicator of inflammation; could possibly be infection.","More useful for monitoring chronic inflammation.","CRP and ESR often go hand in hand.","Neither tells you where in the body the inflammation is (it could be an arthritis flare, for instance).","BNP & Pro-BNP","Brain natriuretic peptide","Despite the word \"brain,\" the main source of BNP is the ventricles of the heart.","Helps distinguish cardiac versus respiratory causes of dyspnea: a short-of-breath patient in the ED, where the provider wants to know if it is heart failure or respiratory failure.","An elevated BNP indicates left ventricular dysfunction.","Pro-BNP is what the instructor sees most often at UK. Plasma concentrations of BNP and pro-BNP are similar, but with left ventricular dysfunction (left-sided heart failure) pro-BNP rises much more significantly than BNP.","No ranges given: you don't need to memorize numbers for this, and NCLEX won't expect you to.","Rule of thumb from the lecture: BNP or pro-BNP elevated, think heart failure. Troponin elevated, think myocardial infarction.","Cholesterol & Lipoproteins","Total cholesterol","The main lipid associated with cardiovascular disease.","We eat it and we also manufacture it; high cholesterol can be inherited (familial).","Done as part of a lipid profile; not a totally accurate predictor of heart disease by itself.","Diurnal variation: a morning reading can differ from an afternoon reading.","Affected by malnutrition: if it is very low, also look at the serum albumin to see how malnourished the patient is.","HDL and LDL","An accurate predictor of heart disease.","HDL = the \"good\" cholesterol; removes cholesterol in the body.","LDL = the \"bad\" cholesterol; deposits cholesterol in the peripheral tissue.","We like high HDLs and low LDLs.","The values help determine whether the patient is low, moderate, or high risk for CAD.","The one number to know: total cholesterol less than 200. The instructor does not ask you to memorize the LDL, HDL, or triglyceride numbers; a test question would speak in general terms.","Non-Invasive Procedures","12-lead EKG (ECG) — Looks at the heart's conduction system from 12 different perspectives simultaneously. A snapshot in time (takes 2 to 3 minutes at the bedside). — The provider can diagnose an MI with a 12-lead. Nurses and nursing student…","Telemetry (\"tele\") — Ongoing, continuous monitoring of the cardiac rhythm (telebox around the neck, chest electrodes, or bedside monitoring; may be watched at a central monitoring area). — Only a one-way perspective of the heart's rhythm. I…","Ambulatory EKG (Holter monitor) — Like a 24-hour EKG, worn at home to capture heart activity, for example with syncope of unknown origin that could be a dysrhythmia. — The patient keeps a diary (exercise, anything that feels funny in the he…","Stress test — Evaluates the heart's response to exercise (treadmill or exercise bike), stressing the heart on purpose. — For chest pain with exertion but not at rest. CAD may show minimal symptoms and a normal resting EKG; exercise unmasks …","Echocardiogram (\"echo\") — Uses sound waves to create a moving picture of the heart. — Evaluates the valves, chamber size, and time in diastole and systole; measures ejection fraction. No radiation, no prep, painless. Bedside or outpatient; …","12-Lead EKG vs Telemetry","12-lead EKG","12 perspectives at once.","A snapshot in time.","Can diagnose an MI.","Telemetry","A one-way perspective.","Continuous and ongoing.","Monitors only; cannot diagnose.","Ejection Fraction","Definition and values","The amount of blood pumped out of the left ventricle with each contraction; an indication of how effectively the heart is pumping.","Measured with an echocardiogram, and (per the lecture) also with a right-sided heart cath.","Normal: 55% to 70% of the blood sitting in the ventricle at the end of diastole is ejected (about half or a little more).","Low values, such as 40%, are seen in heart failure. A really, really low ejection fraction can mean the patient might need a heart transplant.","Very important information for a patient who might have heart failure.","Invasive Procedures","TEE (transesophageal echocardiogram) — A probe is inserted into the esophagus. The plain echo shows the anterior heart; the TEE shows the posterior heart and can give a lot more information. Done in the vascular lab. — Contrast dye may be u…","EP study (electrophysiology study) — Evaluates the electrical health of the heart, usually for dysrhythmias not responding to standard treatment. Dysrhythmias are induced on purpose in a controlled environment to confirm the root of the ele…","Cardiac catheterization with angiography (\"heart cath\"; some older patients call it a \"dye test\") — Dye plus pictures. Gives information about the heart muscle, the coronary arteries, and the valves. Diagnostic: confirms the location and ex…","Left vs Right Heart Cath","Left heart cath","Enter an artery (femoral, radial, or brachial).","The catheter goes retrograde up into the ascending aorta / arch of the aorta.","The goal is always to get to the coronary arteries; this is the absolute only way to reach them.","Used when a patient has chest pain or angina and the question is coronary occlusion.","Places examined: the aorta, right coronary artery, left anterior descending, circumflex, and left main coronary artery; the left ventricle sits at the apex.","Right heart cath","Enter the venous system.","Can measure ejection fraction and look at the pressures of the right side of the heart.","Cannot look at the coronary arteries.","Sometimes only one side is done; a lot of times both are done.","Before the Heart Cath","Pre-procedure","NPO; meds with sips of water may be allowed (provider decision).","Signed consent (definitely invasive); the patient and family need to understand the procedure and what to expect.","Very little pain, because blood vessels don't have nerve fibers.","Warn the patient about the warm flush / hot flash when the contrast dye is injected; it is an expected finding, so they don't think something is wrong.","Pre-procedure checklist; monitor BUN and creatinine (dye can be nephrotoxic); empty the bladder before going.","Local anesthesia and likely IV sedation to keep them from being anxious.","After the Heart Cath","Recovery in the cath lab observation area before returning to the room — Standard recovery location.","Monitor clotting time closely before the sheath is pulled — Anticoagulants are used during the procedure and DC'd afterward; arteries are high-pressure vessels, so bleeding is possible (especially after a heparin drip).","Sheath removed, then pressure held up to about 20 minutes — A long time compared to a regular IV, because the vessel needs to seal.","Bed rest at least 8 hours if the femoral (groin) site was used; affected leg straight; head of bed below 30 degrees — So the vessel doesn't bleed.","Frequent assessment of circulation distal to the puncture: pulse, color, sensation — Make sure there is good perfusion on the other side of where the vessel was penetrated.","Potential Complications of a Heart Cath","Bleeding (number one) — An IV heparin drip is used during the procedure to decrease clot risk; monitor the puncture site closely for hematomas.","Emboli — Monitor for signs and symptoms of stroke.","Infection — A listed risk.","Dysrhythmias — A listed risk.","Damage to the heart — If the pericardium is accidentally poked, the patient may develop cardiac tamponade.","Allergy to the dye — Monitor for it.","Contrast nephrotoxicity — Risk is higher in kidney failure; see below.","Contrast Nephrotoxicity","Acute kidney injury after dye","An acute kidney injury (AKI) that happens after a dye injection; a major cause of AKI in the hospitalized patient.","Increased risk with existing renal problems, diabetes, or fluid volume deficit.","Acetylcysteine, and sometimes sodium bicarbonate, are given before the procedure to offset the elevated BUN and creatinine.","Encourage the patient to force fluids afterward to dilute the dye going through the kidneys."]},{"s":"Part 2 — CAD & Acute Coronary Syndrome▾","a":"part-2-cad-acute-coronary-syndrome","k":["Part 2 — CAD & Acute Coronary Syndrome▾","From CAD & Acute Coronary Syndrome. Built from the three Week 1 coronary voiceovers: coronary heart disease and chronic stable angina (part 1), acute coronary syndrome and MI (part 2), and caring for the patient with ACS, including PCI, thr…","Coronary Arteries & Terminology","The basics","Coronary heart disease, coronary artery disease (CAD), and ischemic heart disease are used interchangeably.","The coronary arteries perfuse the heart, getting oxygen to the heart muscle. We want patent coronary arteries.","You can predict what area of the heart an artery perfuses from the anatomy. The instructor is not going to ask you to memorize the arteries, but the providers look at a few main ones: the left main, the left anterior descending (LAD), the c…","The left main and LAD perfuse the left ventricle, the powerhouse chamber. Complete blockage there is sometimes called the \"widow maker\", because those patients often never make it to the hospital.","Atherosclerosis (\"hardening of the arteries\") is responsible for almost all cases of CAD, regardless of type.","The CAD Continuum","Think of coronary artery disease as a continuum, from chronic ischemic heart disease to acute coronary syndrome (ACS). Once it starts looking like unstable angina, it is collectively called ACS, because time is of the essence if it advances…","Stable angina — Unstable angina — NSTEMI — STEMI","Chronic ischemic heart disease; the patient lives with it. — ACS. No heart damage. — ACS. Heart damage. — ACS. More extensive heart damage.","\"A STEMI is worse than a non-STEMI, a non-STEMI is worse than unstable angina, and unstable angina is worse than stable angina.\"","Risk Factors for CAD","Non-modifiable","Age: the older someone gets, the more likely CAD is.","Gender: males are at greater risk than females.","Family history: especially relatives who developed CAD younger.","The four most firmly established modifiable","Elevated serum lipids (cholesterol, triglycerides); we want high HDLs and low LDLs.","Hypertension: untreated, the increased arterial pressure causes damage over time (the hardening). It can be silent and asymptomatic, so people don't perceive it as a big deal. Patients with CKD or diabetes have an even lower BP goal.","Tobacco use: risk is proportional to the number of cigarettes smoked.","Physical inactivity.","Other modifiable risk factors","Obesity: apple-shaped people (rotund in the belly) are at higher risk than pear-shaped people (hips wider than the abdomen).","Diabetes mellitus: poor glucose control aggravates coronary heart disease.","Depression or prolonged stress.","Teaching for Each Modifiable Risk Factor","Elevated serum lipids — Monitor lipids regularly; diet control (low-fat diet); statin medications.","Hypertension — Check BP regularly; antihypertensive medications; DASH diet (low-salt, low-fat).","Tobacco — Don't start; if they smoke, encourage stopping. Switching to lower-nicotine or filtered cigarettes does not impact the risk. The benefits of cessation are dramatic and start almost immediately, even after 20 years of smoking.","Physical inactivity — Middle-aged patients or those with a history of medical problems should see the provider before starting. Exercise sessions at least 40 minutes long with a 10-minute warm-up and cool-down, moderately 3 to 4 times a wee…","Obesity — Avoid fad diets; restrict saturated fats, sweets, and high-cholesterol foods; increase physical activity.","Diabetes — Tight glucose control: glycemic index diet, carb counting, diabetes education, hemoglobin A1C checked regularly.","Stress — Incorporate stress-reduction activities.","Chronic Stable Angina","Angina means chest pain from a temporary loss of oxygenation to the heart muscle, more often than not from an occluded coronary artery (but you can't know without the tests).","Where the pain can be","Substernal, possibly radiating up to the neck.","The classic: substernal, radiating down the left arm. It can radiate down both arms.","Epigastric (troponins help rule cardiac in or out; sometimes it is just gastric reflux).","Mid-scapular. The instructor once had a patient with pain in the elbows: it is not exactly predictable.","What makes it \"stable\"","Occurs with moderate to prolonged exertion, in a pattern familiar to the patient.","Frequency, duration, and intensity stay pretty much stable.","Only slight limitations in activity; regulated on medication.","Usually associated with fixed atherosclerotic plaque.","Relieved by rest and/or nitroglycerin.","Managed with drug therapy; rarely requires aggressive therapy.","Diagnosing Stable Angina","Workup","Physical exam and complete history; EKG.","Exercise stress test, or a pharmacological stress test if they can't use the treadmill or bike: a drug with positive inotropic and chronotropic effects (increased contractility and heart rate) to stress the heart on purpose.","Low and intermediate risk results: manage symptoms without an intervention.","High risk results: more testing, such as cardiac catheterization followed by revascularization (PCI or CABG), and/or an echo or TEE.","Goals and Medications for Stable Angina","Goal: decrease the workload on the heart and increase the oxygen supply, targeting the modifiable risk factors as appropriate.","Aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid), antiplatelet — 81 mg (the chewable) or 325 mg (the adult form); evidence doesn't show one works better. Anyone at risk for CAD needs to be taking an aspirin a day.","Lipid-lowering agents — Already covered in patho.","Beta blockers (\"-olol\") — The only classification proven to prevent re-infarction and improve survival for MI patients. Long-acting beta blockers are sometimes used.","Calcium channel blockers — Usually given in conjunction with the beta blocker.","Nitrates — Typically sublingual; first-line treatment for acute anginal symptoms. Long-acting nitrates also exist.","Sodium current inhibitors (Ranexa) — The newer therapy.","Nitroglycerin","Current guideline: if chest pain has not improved, or has worsened, 5 minutes after the first nitro, the patient calls 911 and chews an aspirin immediately (antiplatelet effects begin within an hour and continue for several days). The old t…","Sublingual (most common) — Rapid acting. Under the tongue; don't swallow. Repeat every 5 minutes times 3 if needed. At home, if one doesn't work, activate EMS. In the hospital, take vital signs before each dose; you might give it every 5 mi…","Translingual — Rapid acting; on the tongue.","IV — Rapid acting; dosed in micrograms per minute (med math). Comes in a glass bottle with special vented tubing.","Skin patch and ointment — Use gloves to apply and remove (it can vasodilate you and give you a horrendous headache). Never defibrillate over the patch.","Isosorbide — For preventing angina attacks; taken daily. Tolerance builds up over time.","Teaching about sublingual nitro","Take it at the beginning of chest pain, or prophylactically before activity known to cause it (walking, bike riding, sexual activity).","Works in less than 3 minutes; keep it accessible (in your pocket).","Protect it from light (brown bottle); replace the bottle every 6 months.","It should tingle as it dissolves; no tingle may mean it has expired. Continued pain or no tingle: activate EMS.","Never combine with erectile dysfunction drugs; this can be fatal.","Side effects (all from vasodilation)","Headache (not a reason to skip the nitro).","Lowered BP: fainting and postural hypotension.","Flushing.","Always know the blood pressure before giving nitroglycerin, because it is going to lower it. A patient with a low baseline (for example 90/60) can plummet. If the BP is too low, no more nitro is given; something different, like IV morphine,…","Acute Coronary Syndrome: Unstable Angina, NSTEMI & STEMI","ACS is what happens when the slippery slope begins and the patient has more signs and symptoms than we want. It is divided into unstable angina and acute MI.","Unstable angina — No changes (or ST depression / deep T-wave inversion) — Not elevated — None, but a sign to be vigilant about slipping into an NSTEMI or STEMI","NSTEMI (non-ST-elevation MI) — No ST elevation (ST depression / deep T-wave inversion, or no changes) — Elevated — Yes","STEMI (ST-elevation MI) — ST elevation — Elevated — Yes, more extensive","Unstable Angina","How it differs from stable angina","Often occurs at rest, unannounced and unprecipitated; usually lasts more than 20 minutes.","New onset that markedly limits physical activity (stops the person in their tracks).","Increasing angina: more frequent, longer, with less exertion than the patient's previous stable angina.","Poorly relieved by rest or nitroglycerin.","Unpredictable and unstable. Angina is an emergency because unstable angina precedes an MI.","Atypical Presentations","Women and people with diabetes","Symptoms are sometimes atypical: no classic radiation down the left arm.","Fatigue is a more prominent manifestation (a very big symptom); shortness of air; indigestion; anxiety.","Older patients may also present atypically.","A silent MI is one the person didn't know they had. Most MIs are not silent.","Angina Pain vs MI Pain","Angina pain","Precipitated by exertion or stress.","Relieved by rest and/or sublingual nitroglycerin.","Lasts less than 15 minutes.","MI pain","Occurs without a cause; sometimes wakes people from early-morning sleep (the instructor has read 4 to 6 a.m. is peak, possibly tied to the normal morning cortisol surge).","Relieved only by opioids (morphine).","Lasts 20 minutes or longer.","Often with nausea, diaphoresis, shortness of breath, and marked anxiety; can be associated with dysrhythmias.","Severe and immobilizing; not relieved by rest, position change, or nitrates.","Described as heaviness, pressure, tightness, burning, constriction, crushing (\"an elephant sitting on my chest\").","Ask about pressure, not just pain. A cardiac patient may say no to \"pain in your chest\" and yes to \"pressure in your chest.\" Choose words the patient understands.","How an MI Evolves","Time is muscle","Cells can stand about 20 minutes of ischemia before cell death starts.","The subendocardial layer is affected first; it takes about 4 to 6 hours for the entire thickness of the heart to necrose. (Chest pain at noon means full-thickness damage around 4 to 6 p.m.)","That is why ACS patients need to activate EMS and get to the hospital: waiting costs heart muscle.","The location of the MI correlates with the blocked coronary artery (an LAD blockage damages the apex, where the left ventricle is).","MIs are named by location: anterior, lateral, inferior, septal, posterior (for example, an inferior or inferolateral MI). You don't need to know this for the test; it is lingo you may hear.","Complications of an MI","Dysrhythmia — The most common complication of an MI and the most common cause of pre-hospital death (usually a ventricular dysrhythmia). Get a suspected MI on telemetry ASAP.","Heart failure — Muscle death, particularly at the apex and ventricle, loses the heart's pumping action. Keep MI and heart failure separate: they are different pathologies with different signs and symptoms, though one can impact the other.","Cardiogenic shock — Shock: loss of blood pressure and perfusion, and the body shuts down. Cardiogenic shock is when the heart is the problem, from severe left ventricular failure. High mortality.","Papillary muscle dysfunction — May produce a new murmur (listen to S1 and S2 for adventitious sounds).","Pericarditis — Occurs a couple of days after an MI, from the inflammatory response; shows up as a pericardial friction rub.","Diagnosing ACS: EKG Changes","The ST segment","We look for changes in the QRS, the ST segment, and the T wave (more in the dysrhythmia content).","At rest the ST segment should sit on the isoelectric line.","ST elevation = above the isoelectric line; with a positive troponin, that is a STEMI. Points to infarction (cell death, permanent).","ST depression = below the isoelectric line; sometimes happens with ischemia, sometimes infarction. Points to ischemia (temporary).","A STEMI shows ST elevation in two anatomically contiguous leads (contiguous = sharing a common border, touching each other).","A STEMI cannot be diagnosed by telemetry; it takes a 12-lead EKG.","Serial EKGs may be ordered because ischemia and infarction can change over a few hours.","STEMI vs NSTEMI","ST segment — Elevated — Depressed or normal","QRS — Often wide (\"wide and ugly\") — Normal","T wave — Peaked, then inverted — Inverted","Troponin — Elevated — Elevated","Occlusion — More extensive — More of an incomplete occlusion or transient thrombosis (shared with unstable angina)","Outcome — Larger infarct; people don't do as well — Better than a STEMI","Initial Care of the ACS Patient","Assess and stabilize","Consider ACS with chest discomfort, shortness of air, or other suggestive symptoms; remember atypical presentations in women, older patients, and patients with diabetes.","12-lead EKG within 10 minutes of arrival; repeat every 10 to 15 minutes if not definitive and MI is still suspected.","Airway, breathing, circulation. Sit the patient upright to support ventilation; supplemental oxygen per nasal cannula.","Baseline vital signs ASAP, including O2 saturation.","Telemetry ASAP to monitor for dysrhythmias (the number one complication) and ST changes. If you notice ST elevation on tele, the next best nursing action is a 12-lead EKG (cardiac nurses usually have a standing order).","IV access, for emergency or code medications.","Aspirin to chew and swallow (antiplatelet).","Assess pain with PQRST.","Pain Relief in ACS","Nitroglycerin first","0.4 mg sublingual is pretty standard, one at a time, spaced 5 minutes apart for persistent chest pain.","Check BP before each dose; it is likely to drop (a potent coronary vasodilator lowers peripheral vascular resistance).","If BP gets too low, switch to morphine.","Morphine","Eases the workload on the heart.","Decreases preload and afterload.","Helps with anxiety.","Patients often tolerate IV morphine better than nitroglycerin.","PQRST Pain Assessment","P — What started (precipitated) the pain?","Q — What does it feel like? Describe it for me.","R — Where is it, and does it radiate?","S — On a scale of 0 to 10, with 10 the worst imaginable, how bad is it right now?","T — When did it begin? (Timing helps decide the course of action.)","Labs and Ongoing Assessment","What to monitor","Cardiac markers: troponin is the most specific to cardiac muscle (some providers still order CK-MB or myoglobin).","Electrolytes, especially potassium, calcium, and magnesium: imbalances can be the root cause of dysrhythmias.","Hemoglobin and hematocrit; coagulation status (bleeding risk).","Heart sounds: listen for a murmur, gallop, or rub.","Lung sounds: an infarction in the left ventricle can lead to heart failure, and crackles could point to left-sided heart failure.","Treating Blockages: PCI, Thrombolytics & CABG","The three things the health care system can do for blockages found on a heart cath.","PCI (percutaneous coronary intervention) — STEMI and NSTEMI. The first line of treatment for a confirmed MI; what the patient gets more times than not. — Within 90 minutes of ED arrival.","Thrombolytic (fibrinolytic) therapy — A STEMI patient may also be a candidate. — Within 30 minutes of ED arrival; most effective within 6 hours of the initial coronary event.","CABG (coronary artery bypass graft, \"open-heart surgery\") — Patients with diabetes, multi-vessel disease (3 or more vessels), or a blockage the catheter can't reach. — Emergent, or semi-elective (cath today, CABG the day after tomorrow).","PCI (Angioplasty, the \"Balloon Procedure\")","What happens","The heart cath shows whether blockages exist, where, how bad, and whether there is collateral circulation (little vessels the body grew around the blockage to perfuse the other side). It also evaluates left ventricular function.","It is a left heart cath (through the brachial or femoral artery) to reach the coronary arteries.","A guide wire and balloon catheter go to the occlusion; the balloon inflates and smashes the plaque back against the wall, then deflates and is removed.","Sometimes a stent is placed to reestablish patency.","Advantages over open-heart surgery","Percutaneous, through the vascular system; no cracking the chest.","Local anesthesia, not general.","Ambulatory shortly after.","Significantly shorter hospital stay; back to work and life sooner.","More PCIs are performed than CABGs.","PCI Nursing Care","What the CAD lecture adds for PCI","Monitor closely for MI progression: chest pain, EKG changes, new or different dysrhythmias, hemodynamic instability (unstable BP and heart rate).","Dual antiplatelet therapy: aspirin plus a drug like clopidogrel (Plavix). Possibly IV heparin during the procedure. It is a delicate balance; we don't want the patient to bleed out.","The sheath is usually removed 4 to 6 hours after the procedure; an arterial puncture needs manual pressure for a good 20 minutes.","Assess distal to the puncture: for a left groin site, compare the left foot's DP and PT pulses and cap refill to the right foot.","Watch for bleeding externally (the dressing) and internally: a heart rate creeping up can be the body compensating for lost blood volume.","Thrombolytic Therapy (\"Clot Busters\")","The drugs","Fibrinolytics: they lyse fibrin to dissolve thrombi in the coronary arteries and restore blood flow.","Examples: tPA and reteplase.","Per the lecture, given during the cardiac catheterization.","PCI is more commonly performed than thrombolytic therapy.","Monitor closely for bleeding after administration.","Who should NOT get them","History of a brain bleed or stroke, particularly hemorrhagic stroke.","Recent abdominal surgery (bleeding risk).","Active bleeding.","Coagulation studies that are out of range.","CABG (Coronary Artery Bypass Graft)","The procedure","The surgeon sews (anastomoses) a graft proximal and distal to the blockage, literally bypassing it. Up to six grafts (for example \"CABG times one,\" \"CABG times three\").","The patient's own vessels are used, such as the internal mammary artery or the saphenous vein (the long vein from the groin down the medial leg to the ankle). Veins have valves, so the graft is turned in favor of blood flow. Which vessel is…","Valve replacement is sometimes done at the same time.","Stroke and MI — Listed risks after open-heart surgery.","Infection — Sternal wound or the leg where the vein was harvested; poor healing is likelier with atherosclerosis or peripheral arterial/vascular disease (poor perfusion to the periphery) and can lengthen the stay.","Dysrhythmias — ICU for a while, then telemetry for the rest of the stay.","Pleural effusion — Fluid in the pleural cavity; third spacing.","Pericardial effusion and cardiac tamponade — Excess fluid in the pericardial sac (pericardial effusion is to the heart as pleural effusion is to the lungs). A lot of fluid quickly squeezes the heart and it can't maintain cardiac output: car…","Renal failure — Monitor creatinine and urine output very closely.","Ongoing Care & Recovery","After the procedure","Continuous telemetry; rest, comfort, activity restriction for a while; good pain control.","Expect anxiety; teach at the level where the patient is, since everyone is in a different place. The nurse can be a game changer.","Timing matters for emotional and behavioral reactions. Example: resuming sexual activity, especially if that is what brought them in. Have those conversations before discharge so they understand their limitations.","Needs at home span physiological, psychological, mental, spiritual, economic, and vocational areas."]},{"s":"Part 3 — Pericarditis▾","a":"part-3-pericarditis","k":["Part 3 — Pericarditis▾","From Pericarditis. The first of three voiceovers on inflammatory, infective, and valvular heart disease. The friction-rub details from the CAD lecture (where pericarditis came up as an MI complication) are gathered here too, so the rub live…","The Pericardium","Layers and the potential space","The pericardium covers the heart. \"-itis\" means inflammation or infection: with pericarditis think inflammation (endocarditis is more about infection).","The fibrous pericardium is the outside; it circles back around into the visceral and parietal pericardium, with the pericardial space in between.","The pericardial space is a potential space holding usually 5 to 10 mL of fluid, just enough to keep the heart lubricated and smooth while it pumps.","Beneath are the myocardium and the endocardium.","Causes","Idiopathic — Often we really don't know why it happens.","Infective organisms — A variety sometimes precede pericarditis.","After an MI — A complication of an MI; usually shows up a few days after, from the MI's inflammation.","Connective tissue diseases — The pericardium is connective tissue. Examples: rheumatoid arthritis (RA) and lupus.","Pericarditis Pain","How it presents","Substernal / precordial (behind the breastbone), radiating to the left side of the neck, shoulder, and back.","A grating, oppressive feel (oppressive: hard to catch your breath).","Aggravated by breathing (mainly inspiration), coughing, and swallowing.","Worse lying down; better sitting up and leaning forward (leaning forward minimizes the stretch on the pericardium). A positional thing.","Pericarditis Pain vs MI Pain","After an MI you have to tell the two apart, since pericarditis is an MI complication.","Pericarditis pain","Onset usually more sudden.","Substernal, left precordial; may radiate.","Sharp and stabbing, or aching and dull; oppressive.","Worse on inspiration.","Body movement can increase it; leaning forward makes it better.","Can wax and wane.","Sublingual nitroglycerin won't help: nitro is a coronary vasodilator, and pericarditis has nothing to do with coronary vasodilation.","MI pain","Position is not going to matter.","Breathing isn't going to impact the pain.","Ischemia is what nitroglycerin addresses.","The Pericardial Friction Rub","The hallmark finding of pericarditis is the pericardial friction rub.","What it sounds like and why","A \"squeaky heart\"; a grating, continuous grinding sound, like two pieces of sandpaper rubbing together.","The visceral and parietal pericardium normally have a little fluid between them. Inflammation takes away that fluidity, and the two layers rub (CAD lecture).","Same idea as a pleural friction rub, when the visceral and parietal pleura are inflamed (for example, pleurisy).","How to listen","Stethoscope at the lower left sternal border.","Patient leaning forward (brings the heart closer to the chest wall).","Ask the patient to hold their breath to eliminate breath sounds and focus on the heart.","From the CAD lecture: if the grating disappears when the patient holds their breath, it is not cardiac in origin (it is respiratory).","Complications: Pericardial Effusion & Tamponade","Pericardial Effusion","Fluid in the pericardial space","Pericardial effusion is to the pericardium what pleural effusion is to the pleural space: third spacing, a collection of fluid that serves absolutely no purpose.","Symptoms come from the fluid squeezing the heart.","Cues: cough, tachypnea, difficulty breathing, and heart sounds that aren't crisp (fluid between the stethoscope and the heart).","The pericardial space doesn't expand much. As it fills, the heart gets squished, can't contract and release like it should, and cardiac output suffers.","Seen on an echocardiogram (or TEE), which delineates the heart's structures.","Cardiac Tamponade","Tamponade is time-sensitive and can be life-threatening. It develops when a pericardial effusion increases in volume.","Dyspnea, angina, restlessness, anxiety — Listed manifestations.","Decreased cardiac output (changes in vital signs) — Blood pressure is an indirect measure of cardiac output.","Marked jugular venous distension — Backup of fluid from the tamponade.","Very muffled heart sounds — Fluid around the heart.","Narrowed pulse pressure — See below.","Pulsus paradoxus (a classic) — See below.","Narrowed pulse pressure","Pulse pressure = systolic minus diastolic.","120/80 gives a pulse pressure of 40: pretty average.","In tamponade it narrows, such as 110/90: a pulse pressure of 20.","Pulsus paradoxus","BP normally drops a little with breathing, but not significantly. Significant = greater than 10 mmHg.","Systolic BP is 10 mmHg or more higher on expiration than on inspiration. (Lewis says it one way, UpToDate the other: 10 mmHg lower on inspiration. Same thing.)","For the skilled practitioner; the instructor has assessed it when the patient has an arterial line (systolic waxes and wanes with breathing). You won't get checked off on it.","1. Inflate the cuff beyond a palpable BP — You are palpating, not auscultating as in a normal BP.","2. Deflate gradually; note when sounds are first audible on expiration only — First number.","3. Note when sounds are audible on both inspiration and expiration — Second number.","4. A difference greater than 10 indicates tamponade — Lewis has a table, and there is a video on Canvas.","Diagnostics","EKG — Diffuse, widespread ST elevation in almost all leads. ST elevation in only two contiguous leads points to an acute MI instead.","Echocardiogram — Determines whether pericardial effusion or cardiac tamponade has occurred.","WBC, sed rate, CRP — May be elevated (it's an \"-itis\"). Sed rate and CRP are non-specific inflammation markers: sed rate more chronic, CRP more acute, and neither tells where the inflammation is.","Treatment","Bed rest, head of bed at 45 degrees — Plus measures to decrease anxiety.","Antibiotics — Only if bacteria is the causative agent; otherwise not indicated.","NSAIDs (the hallmark treatment) — Good old-fashioned NSAIDs: naproxen or high-dose ibuprofen (both over the counter). No morphine or opioids, because the problem is inflammation. Diagnosing it matters, because treatment differs from, say, p…","Colchicine — For recurrent pericarditis (covered again with arthritis and gout).","Prednisone taper — Only if NSAIDs are ineffective.","Pericardiocentesis (emergency) — Pericardiocentesis is to the pericardium as thoracentesis is to the pleural space: a needle removes the fluid. Per the lecture, removing about 5 to 10 mL can increase stroke volume by as much as 50%, letting…"]},{"s":"Part 4 — Infective Endocarditis▾","a":"part-4-infective-endocarditis","k":["Part 4 — Infective Endocarditis▾","From Infective Endocarditis. With pericarditis think inflammation; with endocarditis think infection. \"Infective endocarditis\" is the longer name, but it is usually just called endocarditis.","What It Is","The endocardium and the valves","The endocardium is the inside of the heart, which is a cue to where the problem is.","The valves are a very movable part of the heart, and a lot of the manifestations of endocarditis relate to the valves.","Endocarditis occurs when blood flow within the heart lets the organism infect previously damaged valves or another endothelial surface.","Subacute vs Acute","Subacute","Affects those with pre-existing heart problems.","Typically milder in presentation.","Can be more chronic.","Acute","Affects healthy valves.","Manifests itself quicker.","Therapy needs to be prompt and vigorous.","Who Gets It & What Causes It","Two things have to happen","A history of damaged epithelium, such as a valve replacement or a defect.","A port of entry for infection to reach the endocardium: a sore, a dental procedure, IV drug use.","Causative organisms","Most common: Staphylococcus aureus and Streptococcus viridans (bacterial).","Other possible pathogens: fungi and viruses, so not all endocarditis is bacterial.","Vegetations & Emboli","Why vegetations matter","Infection on the moving valves develops vegetations, for example mitral valve vegetation between the left atrium and left ventricle. Bacterial endocarditis of the mitral valve covers it with large, irregular vegetations.","The valves are constantly opening and closing, so vegetations can slough off and become emboli, dispersed into the vascular system.","Left-sided vegetation","Leaves the heart into the organs.","Can cause a limb infarction or an infarction in any organ of the body.","Right-sided vegetation","Blood from the right side goes to the lungs.","Like a pulmonary embolus.","Manifestations","Very non-specific at first","Timing: the transcript says it typically occurs \"within 10 two weeks\" of the infectious organism (unclear wording in the transcript, so no firm number is given here).","Low-grade fever, chills, malaise, fatigue, anorexia, joint pain, muscle pain. If you didn't know better, you'd think it was the flu.","Clubbing of the fingers is the odd one out, and it comes later in the disease process, not early.","Findings Unique to Endocarditis","The instructor says to commit these to memory: \"you don't hear about these in any other condition,\" and they \"would be great NCLEX questions.\"","Splinter hemorrhages — A longitudinal streak in the nail bed. — Vascular manifestation: vegetations leaving the left side of the heart and going to the periphery.","Petechiae — Little red hemorrhagic spots; smooth, flat (a macule), not raised, not painful. — Micro-emboli of vegetation. Fingers, eye, mouth, ankles, antecubital and popliteal areas.","Osler nodes — Tender papules on the fingertips and toes; look like they have a little pus in them. — Relatively uncommon, but highly suggestive of (specific to) infective endocarditis.","Janeway lesions — Flat, macular, painless red spots on the palms and soles. — Relatively uncommon, but highly suggestive.","Roth spots — Little hemorrhagic areas in the back of the retina. — Relatively uncommon, but highly suggestive.","Telling the two apart: Osler nodes are tender (fingertips and toes); Janeway lesions are painless and flat (palms and soles).","Clinical Manifestations","New-onset murmur — A murmur is about the opening and closing of the valve. You should hear S1 and S2 crisply; damaged valves produce a murmur, an adventitious heart sound graded from 1 to 6.","Heart failure — The most common complication of infective endocarditis. Damaged valves leak blood back instead of letting it go one direction, the blood backs up, and the patient develops left- and right-sided heart failure.","History & Diagnostic Tests","History — Recent dental, urological, or gynecologic procedure; IV drug use.","Blood cultures — Three cultures drawn over an hour apart from three different sites; positive results are indicative of endocarditis.","Echocardiogram — Evidence of endocardial enlargement.","Prophylactic Antibiotics","Who gets prophylaxis","A mechanical heart valve.","A history of endocarditis.","A history of congenital heart disease.","A heart transplant.","Before which procedures","Any dental visit.","A biopsy of any kind.","Tonsil and adenoid removal.","A wound infection or urinary tract infection.","Treatment & Nursing Care","Treatment","Typically hospitalized initially; medications usually given IV.","Complete eradication may take weeks upon weeks; relapses are common.","Home care may include going home with a PICC line for IV antibiotics.","Nursing care and teaching","Identify and educate high-risk individuals so they can avoid infections.","Good rest; good oral hygiene (the mouth is where bacteria can enter).","Let the provider know before any dental procedure.","Refer IV drug users to drug rehab.","Monitor for the non-specific signs and symptoms that indicate a recurrence."]},{"s":"Part 5 — Valvular Heart Disease▾","a":"part-5-valvular-heart-disease","k":["Part 5 — Valvular Heart Disease▾","From Valvular Heart Disease. The last of the three inflammatory, infective, and valvular voiceovers. The instructor says you really need to know your valves: where they are and what they do.","Valve Review","What the valves do","Valves let blood leave one chamber and go into the next. When the next chamber contracts, the valve slams shut, keeping blood moving in a unidirectional path. Stenosis, prolapse, or regurgitation messes with that and causes backflow.","AV valves: tricuspid and mitral. Semilunar valves: pulmonic and aortic.","The mitral lies between the left atrium and left ventricle; the aortic lies after the left ventricle, before systemic circulation.","Left heart valves take more of a beating, because the left side is the high-pressure system. A repaired or replaced valve is, more times than not, the mitral or the aortic.","Auscultation Landmarks","Aortic — Right sternal border, 2nd ICS — S2 heard best (the base of the heart).","Pulmonic — Left sternal border, 2nd ICS","Erb's point — Left sternal border, 3rd ICS — S1 and S2 both heard best simultaneously in one place.","Tricuspid — 4th ICS","Mitral — 5th ICS, left midclavicular line — S1 heard best (the apex; closure of the mitral valve).","Types of Valve Problems","Stenosis — The door that won't open all the way. — Constriction or narrowing; blood backs up. — Often a history of rheumatic heart disease.","Regurgitation — The door that won't close all the way. — An incompetent valve; blood backs up into the previous chamber when it shouldn't. — Older age, or infective endocarditis.","Prolapse — The leaflets buckle. — Etiology unknown. The most common valve problem in the US; often seen in younger people and may improve with age.","Manifestations","They start to look like heart failure","Lewis has a long list; you don't need to memorize all of it. Stick with the classic ones below.","Regardless of the cause, blood backs up, and the patient shows left and right heart failure.","Backup from the right side goes to the periphery; backup from the left side shows up in the lungs.","Classic manifestations: exertional dyspnea, weakness, fatigue, possibly chest pain, maybe a murmur.","Conservative Management","Initially we try not to do surgery; treatment escalates if the patient becomes symptomatic enough.","Prophylactic antibiotics as necessary — To prevent rheumatic fever, rheumatic heart disease, and infective endocarditis.","Sodium restriction — \"Salt sucks and water follows salt\": keep excess fluid off board.","Heart failure medications — To treat and control the heart failure that has resulted from the valve problem.","Anticoagulants and/or antidysrhythmics — May be ordered.","Repairing the Valve","If surgery is needed, we would rather repair the patient's own valve than replace it. Percutaneous options include a commissurotomy, a transluminal balloon valvuloplasty, or a percutaneous valve replacement (such as an aortic valve replacem…","Commissurotomy — \"-otomy\" means opening: the provider opens up a stenosed valve.","Balloon valvuloplasty — For a stiff valve: a balloon is inflated to open it up. Like a commissurotomy, the goal is to open a valve that isn't as open as it should be, without replacing it.","Annuloplasty — Surgery to repair and reshape the valve, using annuloplasty rings so it works better.","Replacing the Valve: Mechanical vs Biologic","Mechanical valve","More durable; lasts longer.","Needs lifetime anticoagulation: the body sees the mechanical pieces as foreign, and clots might form. That is the disadvantage.","For someone who will be compliant with lifetime anticoagulation.","You will hear a click (it is metal). Some say you can hear it from the bedside without a stethoscope.","Biologic valve (usually porcine, a pig valve)","Less durable; doesn't last as long (less than 8 to 10 years).","No need for anticoagulation.","For someone older with a shorter life expectancy, or someone unwilling or unable to adhere to anticoagulation.","No click; you hear only the closure of the valve.","Adventitious heart sounds so far: a rub, a murmur, a gallop, and now a click. Who has a click? Someone with a mechanical valve.","After Valve Replacement","Recovery and anticoagulation","Recovery is similar to a CABG; sometimes valves are replaced at the same time as the bypass (\"killing two birds with one stone\").","A prosthetic valve means anticoagulation. On warfarin (Coumadin), the INR is monitored for a lifetime.","Per the lecture: normal INR 0.8 to 1.1; goal INR on warfarin 2.5 to 3.5. Thin the blood, but not too thin; any higher and they could have issues.","Antidote for bleeding: vitamin K.","Eat vitamin K foods consistently (kale greens, for example); don't binge — Vitamin K messes with the clotting factors and reverses the effect of warfarin.","Electric razor instead of a straight-edge razor — Take measures not to bleed.","Monitor for any signs of excessive bleeding — Anticoagulated.","Avoid an MRI — The valve has metal.","Wear a medical alert bracelet or card — Always."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Mixed across all five Week 1 topics, built around the look-alikes that are easy to mix up. Click a card to flip it."]}]},{"f":"week1-cv-labs-diagnostics.html","t":"Cardiovascular Labs & Diagnostics","secs":[{"s":"Cardiovascular Labs & Diagnostics","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 2","Serum Cardiac Markers","Inflammation Markers: CRP & ESR","BNP & Pro-BNP","Cholesterol & Lipoproteins","Non-Invasive Procedures","Invasive Procedures","Self-Test Flashcards","Built from the two Week 1 labs-and-diagnostics voiceovers: serum labs and non-invasive tests (part 1), then the invasive procedures (part 2). The instructor says you will not need to memorize the numeric ranges for most of these labs; the e…"]},{"s":"Serum Cardiac Markers▾","a":"serum-cardiac-markers","k":["Serum Cardiac Markers▾","When a patient has a myocardial infarction (MI), there is heart cell death, and the markers below are what the blood draw shows.","Troponin — The most sensitive indicator of myocardial damage. Helps differentiate cardiac from non-cardiac chest pain (for example, cardiac versus GI substernal pressure). — Elevates 2 to 3 hours after the MI; returns to baseline in 10 to 1…","CK-MB — Muscle damage, but not specific to the heart. — Elevated 3 hours after an MI; stays elevated 24 hours to 2 days. — Historically important and used before troponin existed; \"NCLEX still likes to talk about it.\" Not as specific as tro…","Myoglobin — Another non-specific marker of myocardial damage (less specific than troponin). — Not given. — Mentioned only because it shows up on some standardized tests. The instructor says you don't need to know myoglobin."]},{"s":"Inflammation Markers: CRP & ESR▾","a":"inflammation-markers-crp-esr","k":["Inflammation Markers: CRP & ESR▾","CRP (C-reactive protein)","Indicates inflammation in the body; non-specific (it doesn't say where).","More sensitive than the ESR.","Peaks within 18 to 72 hours after an MI; failure to normalize might indicate ongoing damage to the heart muscle.","With an MI there is inflammation in addition to heart cell death.","Monitors more closely for the acute picture.","ESR (sed rate)","Also a non-specific indicator of inflammation; could possibly be infection.","More useful for monitoring chronic inflammation.","CRP and ESR often go hand in hand.","Neither tells you where in the body the inflammation is (it could be an arthritis flare, for instance)."]},{"s":"BNP & Pro-BNP▾","a":"bnp-pro-bnp","k":["BNP & Pro-BNP▾","Brain natriuretic peptide","Despite the word \"brain,\" the main source of BNP is the ventricles of the heart.","Helps distinguish cardiac versus respiratory causes of dyspnea: a short-of-breath patient in the ED, where the provider wants to know if it is heart failure or respiratory failure.","An elevated BNP indicates left ventricular dysfunction.","Pro-BNP is what the instructor sees most often at UK. Plasma concentrations of BNP and pro-BNP are similar, but with left ventricular dysfunction (left-sided heart failure) pro-BNP rises much more significantly than BNP.","No ranges given: you don't need to memorize numbers for this, and NCLEX won't expect you to.","Rule of thumb from the lecture: BNP or pro-BNP elevated, think heart failure. Troponin elevated, think myocardial infarction."]},{"s":"Cholesterol & Lipoproteins▾","a":"cholesterol-lipoproteins","k":["Cholesterol & Lipoproteins▾","Total cholesterol","The main lipid associated with cardiovascular disease.","We eat it and we also manufacture it; high cholesterol can be inherited (familial).","Done as part of a lipid profile; not a totally accurate predictor of heart disease by itself.","Diurnal variation: a morning reading can differ from an afternoon reading.","Affected by malnutrition: if it is very low, also look at the serum albumin to see how malnourished the patient is.","HDL and LDL","An accurate predictor of heart disease.","HDL = the \"good\" cholesterol; removes cholesterol in the body.","LDL = the \"bad\" cholesterol; deposits cholesterol in the peripheral tissue.","We like high HDLs and low LDLs.","The values help determine whether the patient is low, moderate, or high risk for CAD.","The one number to know: total cholesterol less than 200. The instructor does not ask you to memorize the LDL, HDL, or triglyceride numbers; a test question would speak in general terms."]},{"s":"Non-Invasive Procedures▾","a":"non-invasive-procedures","k":["Non-Invasive Procedures▾","12-lead EKG (ECG) — Looks at the heart's conduction system from 12 different perspectives simultaneously. A snapshot in time (takes 2 to 3 minutes at the bedside). — The provider can diagnose an MI with a 12-lead. Nurses and nursing student…","Telemetry (\"tele\") — Ongoing, continuous monitoring of the cardiac rhythm (telebox around the neck, chest electrodes, or bedside monitoring; may be watched at a central monitoring area). — Only a one-way perspective of the heart's rhythm. I…","Ambulatory EKG (Holter monitor) — Like a 24-hour EKG, worn at home to capture heart activity, for example with syncope of unknown origin that could be a dysrhythmia. — The patient keeps a diary (exercise, anything that feels funny in the he…","Stress test — Evaluates the heart's response to exercise (treadmill or exercise bike), stressing the heart on purpose. — For chest pain with exertion but not at rest. CAD may show minimal symptoms and a normal resting EKG; exercise unmasks …","Echocardiogram (\"echo\") — Uses sound waves to create a moving picture of the heart. — Evaluates the valves, chamber size, and time in diastole and systole; measures ejection fraction. No radiation, no prep, painless. Bedside or outpatient; …","12-Lead EKG vs Telemetry","12-lead EKG","12 perspectives at once.","A snapshot in time.","Can diagnose an MI.","Telemetry","A one-way perspective.","Continuous and ongoing.","Monitors only; cannot diagnose.","Ejection Fraction","Definition and values","The amount of blood pumped out of the left ventricle with each contraction; an indication of how effectively the heart is pumping.","Measured with an echocardiogram, and (per the lecture) also with a right-sided heart cath.","Normal: 55% to 70% of the blood sitting in the ventricle at the end of diastole is ejected (about half or a little more).","Low values, such as 40%, are seen in heart failure. A really, really low ejection fraction can mean the patient might need a heart transplant.","Very important information for a patient who might have heart failure."]},{"s":"Invasive Procedures▾","a":"invasive-procedures","k":["Invasive Procedures▾","TEE (transesophageal echocardiogram) — A probe is inserted into the esophagus. The plain echo shows the anterior heart; the TEE shows the posterior heart and can give a lot more information. Done in the vascular lab. — Contrast dye may be u…","EP study (electrophysiology study) — Evaluates the electrical health of the heart, usually for dysrhythmias not responding to standard treatment. Dysrhythmias are induced on purpose in a controlled environment to confirm the root of the ele…","Cardiac catheterization with angiography (\"heart cath\"; some older patients call it a \"dye test\") — Dye plus pictures. Gives information about the heart muscle, the coronary arteries, and the valves. Diagnostic: confirms the location and ex…","Left vs Right Heart Cath","Left heart cath","Enter an artery (femoral, radial, or brachial).","The catheter goes retrograde up into the ascending aorta / arch of the aorta.","The goal is always to get to the coronary arteries; this is the absolute only way to reach them.","Used when a patient has chest pain or angina and the question is coronary occlusion.","Places examined: the aorta, right coronary artery, left anterior descending, circumflex, and left main coronary artery; the left ventricle sits at the apex.","Right heart cath","Enter the venous system.","Can measure ejection fraction and look at the pressures of the right side of the heart.","Cannot look at the coronary arteries.","Sometimes only one side is done; a lot of times both are done.","Before the Heart Cath","Pre-procedure","NPO; meds with sips of water may be allowed (provider decision).","Signed consent (definitely invasive); the patient and family need to understand the procedure and what to expect.","Very little pain, because blood vessels don't have nerve fibers.","Warn the patient about the warm flush / hot flash when the contrast dye is injected; it is an expected finding, so they don't think something is wrong.","Pre-procedure checklist; monitor BUN and creatinine (dye can be nephrotoxic); empty the bladder before going.","Local anesthesia and likely IV sedation to keep them from being anxious.","After the Heart Cath","Recovery in the cath lab observation area before returning to the room — Standard recovery location.","Monitor clotting time closely before the sheath is pulled — Anticoagulants are used during the procedure and DC'd afterward; arteries are high-pressure vessels, so bleeding is possible (especially after a heparin drip).","Sheath removed, then pressure held up to about 20 minutes — A long time compared to a regular IV, because the vessel needs to seal.","Bed rest at least 8 hours if the femoral (groin) site was used; affected leg straight; head of bed below 30 degrees — So the vessel doesn't bleed.","Frequent assessment of circulation distal to the puncture: pulse, color, sensation — Make sure there is good perfusion on the other side of where the vessel was penetrated.","Potential Complications of a Heart Cath","Bleeding (number one) — An IV heparin drip is used during the procedure to decrease clot risk; monitor the puncture site closely for hematomas.","Emboli — Monitor for signs and symptoms of stroke.","Infection — A listed risk.","Dysrhythmias — A listed risk.","Damage to the heart — If the pericardium is accidentally poked, the patient may develop cardiac tamponade.","Allergy to the dye — Monitor for it.","Contrast nephrotoxicity — Risk is higher in kidney failure; see below.","Cardiac tamponade is named here as a cath complication — review it in Pericarditis","Contrast Nephrotoxicity","Acute kidney injury after dye","An acute kidney injury (AKI) that happens after a dye injection; a major cause of AKI in the hospitalized patient.","Increased risk with existing renal problems, diabetes, or fluid volume deficit.","Acetylcysteine, and sometimes sodium bicarbonate, are given before the procedure to offset the elevated BUN and creatinine.","Encourage the patient to force fluids afterward to dilute the dye going through the kidneys."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-cad-acs.html","t":"CAD & Acute Coronary Syndrome","secs":[{"s":"CAD & Acute Coronary Syndrome","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 3","Coronary Arteries & Terminology","The CAD Continuum","Risk Factors for CAD","Chronic Stable Angina","Nitroglycerin","Acute Coronary Syndrome: Unstable Angina, NSTEMI & STEMI","Angina Pain vs MI Pain","How an MI Evolves","Complications of an MI","Diagnosing ACS: EKG Changes","Initial Care of the ACS Patient","Treating Blockages: PCI, Thrombolytics & CABG","Ongoing Care & Recovery","Self-Test Flashcards","Built from the three Week 1 coronary voiceovers: coronary heart disease and chronic stable angina (part 1), acute coronary syndrome and MI (part 2), and caring for the patient with ACS, including PCI, thrombolytics, and CABG (part 3)."]},{"s":"Coronary Arteries & Terminology▾","a":"coronary-arteries-terminology","k":["Coronary Arteries & Terminology▾","The basics","Coronary heart disease, coronary artery disease (CAD), and ischemic heart disease are used interchangeably.","The coronary arteries perfuse the heart, getting oxygen to the heart muscle. We want patent coronary arteries.","You can predict what area of the heart an artery perfuses from the anatomy. The instructor is not going to ask you to memorize the arteries, but the providers look at a few main ones: the left main, the left anterior descending (LAD), the c…","The left main and LAD perfuse the left ventricle, the powerhouse chamber. Complete blockage there is sometimes called the \"widow maker\", because those patients often never make it to the hospital.","Atherosclerosis (\"hardening of the arteries\") is responsible for almost all cases of CAD, regardless of type."]},{"s":"The CAD Continuum▾","a":"the-cad-continuum","k":["The CAD Continuum▾","Think of coronary artery disease as a continuum, from chronic ischemic heart disease to acute coronary syndrome (ACS). Once it starts looking like unstable angina, it is collectively called ACS, because time is of the essence if it advances…","Stable angina — Unstable angina — NSTEMI — STEMI","Chronic ischemic heart disease; the patient lives with it. — ACS. No heart damage. — ACS. Heart damage. — ACS. More extensive heart damage.","\"A STEMI is worse than a non-STEMI, a non-STEMI is worse than unstable angina, and unstable angina is worse than stable angina.\""]},{"s":"Risk Factors for CAD▾","a":"risk-factors-for-cad","k":["Risk Factors for CAD▾","Non-modifiable","Age: the older someone gets, the more likely CAD is.","Gender: males are at greater risk than females.","Family history: especially relatives who developed CAD younger.","The four most firmly established modifiable","Elevated serum lipids (cholesterol, triglycerides); we want high HDLs and low LDLs.","Hypertension: untreated, the increased arterial pressure causes damage over time (the hardening). It can be silent and asymptomatic, so people don't perceive it as a big deal. Patients with CKD or diabetes have an even lower BP goal.","Tobacco use: risk is proportional to the number of cigarettes smoked.","Physical inactivity.","Other modifiable risk factors","Obesity: apple-shaped people (rotund in the belly) are at higher risk than pear-shaped people (hips wider than the abdomen).","Diabetes mellitus: poor glucose control aggravates coronary heart disease.","Depression or prolonged stress.","Teaching for Each Modifiable Risk Factor","Elevated serum lipids — Monitor lipids regularly; diet control (low-fat diet); statin medications.","Hypertension — Check BP regularly; antihypertensive medications; DASH diet (low-salt, low-fat).","Tobacco — Don't start; if they smoke, encourage stopping. Switching to lower-nicotine or filtered cigarettes does not impact the risk. The benefits of cessation are dramatic and start almost immediately, even after 20 years of smoking.","Physical inactivity — Middle-aged patients or those with a history of medical problems should see the provider before starting. Exercise sessions at least 40 minutes long with a 10-minute warm-up and cool-down, moderately 3 to 4 times a wee…","Obesity — Avoid fad diets; restrict saturated fats, sweets, and high-cholesterol foods; increase physical activity.","Diabetes — Tight glucose control: glycemic index diet, carb counting, diabetes education, hemoglobin A1C checked regularly.","Stress — Incorporate stress-reduction activities."]},{"s":"Chronic Stable Angina▾","a":"chronic-stable-angina","k":["Chronic Stable Angina▾","Angina means chest pain from a temporary loss of oxygenation to the heart muscle, more often than not from an occluded coronary artery (but you can't know without the tests).","Where the pain can be","Substernal, possibly radiating up to the neck.","The classic: substernal, radiating down the left arm. It can radiate down both arms.","Epigastric (troponins help rule cardiac in or out; sometimes it is just gastric reflux).","Mid-scapular. The instructor once had a patient with pain in the elbows: it is not exactly predictable.","What makes it \"stable\"","Occurs with moderate to prolonged exertion, in a pattern familiar to the patient.","Frequency, duration, and intensity stay pretty much stable.","Only slight limitations in activity; regulated on medication.","Usually associated with fixed atherosclerotic plaque.","Relieved by rest and/or nitroglycerin.","Managed with drug therapy; rarely requires aggressive therapy.","Diagnosing Stable Angina","Workup","Physical exam and complete history; EKG.","Exercise stress test, or a pharmacological stress test if they can't use the treadmill or bike: a drug with positive inotropic and chronotropic effects (increased contractility and heart rate) to stress the heart on purpose.","Low and intermediate risk results: manage symptoms without an intervention.","High risk results: more testing, such as cardiac catheterization followed by revascularization (PCI or CABG), and/or an echo or TEE.","Stress test, echo, and TEE come up again here — review them in CV Labs & Diagnostics","Goals and Medications for Stable Angina","Goal: decrease the workload on the heart and increase the oxygen supply, targeting the modifiable risk factors as appropriate.","Aspirin (acetylsalicylic acid), antiplatelet — 81 mg (the chewable) or 325 mg (the adult form); evidence doesn't show one works better. Anyone at risk for CAD needs to be taking an aspirin a day.","Lipid-lowering agents — Already covered in patho.","Beta blockers (\"-olol\") — The only classification proven to prevent re-infarction and improve survival for MI patients. Long-acting beta blockers are sometimes used.","Calcium channel blockers — Usually given in conjunction with the beta blocker.","Nitrates — Typically sublingual; first-line treatment for acute anginal symptoms. Long-acting nitrates also exist.","Sodium current inhibitors (Ranexa) — The newer therapy."]},{"s":"Nitroglycerin▾","a":"nitroglycerin","k":["Nitroglycerin▾","Current guideline: if chest pain has not improved, or has worsened, 5 minutes after the first nitro, the patient calls 911 and chews an aspirin immediately (antiplatelet effects begin within an hour and continue for several days). The old t…","Sublingual (most common) — Rapid acting. Under the tongue; don't swallow. Repeat every 5 minutes times 3 if needed. At home, if one doesn't work, activate EMS. In the hospital, take vital signs before each dose; you might give it every 5 mi…","Translingual — Rapid acting; on the tongue.","IV — Rapid acting; dosed in micrograms per minute (med math). Comes in a glass bottle with special vented tubing.","Skin patch and ointment — Use gloves to apply and remove (it can vasodilate you and give you a horrendous headache). Never defibrillate over the patch.","Isosorbide — For preventing angina attacks; taken daily. Tolerance builds up over time.","Teaching about sublingual nitro","Take it at the beginning of chest pain, or prophylactically before activity known to cause it (walking, bike riding, sexual activity).","Works in less than 3 minutes; keep it accessible (in your pocket).","Protect it from light (brown bottle); replace the bottle every 6 months.","It should tingle as it dissolves; no tingle may mean it has expired. Continued pain or no tingle: activate EMS.","Never combine with erectile dysfunction drugs; this can be fatal.","Side effects (all from vasodilation)","Headache (not a reason to skip the nitro).","Lowered BP: fainting and postural hypotension.","Flushing.","Always know the blood pressure before giving nitroglycerin, because it is going to lower it. A patient with a low baseline (for example 90/60) can plummet. If the BP is too low, no more nitro is given; something different, like IV morphine,…"]},{"s":"Acute Coronary Syndrome: Unstable Angina, NSTEMI & STEMI▾","a":"acute-coronary-syndrome-unstable-angina-nstemi-stemi","k":["Acute Coronary Syndrome: Unstable Angina, NSTEMI & STEMI▾","ACS is what happens when the slippery slope begins and the patient has more signs and symptoms than we want. It is divided into unstable angina and acute MI.","Unstable angina — No changes (or ST depression / deep T-wave inversion) — Not elevated — None, but a sign to be vigilant about slipping into an NSTEMI or STEMI","NSTEMI (non-ST-elevation MI) — No ST elevation (ST depression / deep T-wave inversion, or no changes) — Elevated — Yes","STEMI (ST-elevation MI) — ST elevation — Elevated — Yes, more extensive","Troponin timing (up in 2 to 3 hours, baseline in 10 to 14 days) is repeated here — review it in CV Labs & Diagnostics","Unstable Angina","How it differs from stable angina","Often occurs at rest, unannounced and unprecipitated; usually lasts more than 20 minutes.","New onset that markedly limits physical activity (stops the person in their tracks).","Increasing angina: more frequent, longer, with less exertion than the patient's previous stable angina.","Poorly relieved by rest or nitroglycerin.","Unpredictable and unstable. Angina is an emergency because unstable angina precedes an MI.","Atypical Presentations","Women and people with diabetes","Symptoms are sometimes atypical: no classic radiation down the left arm.","Fatigue is a more prominent manifestation (a very big symptom); shortness of air; indigestion; anxiety.","Older patients may also present atypically.","A silent MI is one the person didn't know they had. Most MIs are not silent."]},{"s":"Angina Pain vs MI Pain▾","a":"angina-pain-vs-mi-pain","k":["Angina Pain vs MI Pain▾","Angina pain","Precipitated by exertion or stress.","Relieved by rest and/or sublingual nitroglycerin.","Lasts less than 15 minutes.","MI pain","Occurs without a cause; sometimes wakes people from early-morning sleep (the instructor has read 4 to 6 a.m. is peak, possibly tied to the normal morning cortisol surge).","Relieved only by opioids (morphine).","Lasts 20 minutes or longer.","Often with nausea, diaphoresis, shortness of breath, and marked anxiety; can be associated with dysrhythmias.","Severe and immobilizing; not relieved by rest, position change, or nitrates.","Described as heaviness, pressure, tightness, burning, constriction, crushing (\"an elephant sitting on my chest\").","Ask about pressure, not just pain. A cardiac patient may say no to \"pain in your chest\" and yes to \"pressure in your chest.\" Choose words the patient understands."]},{"s":"How an MI Evolves▾","a":"how-an-mi-evolves","k":["How an MI Evolves▾","Time is muscle","Cells can stand about 20 minutes of ischemia before cell death starts.","The subendocardial layer is affected first; it takes about 4 to 6 hours for the entire thickness of the heart to necrose. (Chest pain at noon means full-thickness damage around 4 to 6 p.m.)","That is why ACS patients need to activate EMS and get to the hospital: waiting costs heart muscle.","The location of the MI correlates with the blocked coronary artery (an LAD blockage damages the apex, where the left ventricle is).","MIs are named by location: anterior, lateral, inferior, septal, posterior (for example, an inferior or inferolateral MI). You don't need to know this for the test; it is lingo you may hear."]},{"s":"Complications of an MI▾","a":"complications-of-an-mi","k":["Complications of an MI▾","Dysrhythmia — The most common complication of an MI and the most common cause of pre-hospital death (usually a ventricular dysrhythmia). Get a suspected MI on telemetry ASAP.","Heart failure — Muscle death, particularly at the apex and ventricle, loses the heart's pumping action. Keep MI and heart failure separate: they are different pathologies with different signs and symptoms, though one can impact the other.","Cardiogenic shock — Shock: loss of blood pressure and perfusion, and the body shuts down. Cardiogenic shock is when the heart is the problem, from severe left ventricular failure. High mortality.","Papillary muscle dysfunction — May produce a new murmur (listen to S1 and S2 for adventitious sounds).","Pericarditis — Occurs a couple of days after an MI, from the inflammatory response; shows up as a pericardial friction rub.","Pericarditis and the friction rub are covered again here — review them in Pericarditis"]},{"s":"Diagnosing ACS: EKG Changes▾","a":"diagnosing-acs-ekg-changes","k":["Diagnosing ACS: EKG Changes▾","The ST segment","We look for changes in the QRS, the ST segment, and the T wave (more in the dysrhythmia content).","At rest the ST segment should sit on the isoelectric line.","ST elevation = above the isoelectric line; with a positive troponin, that is a STEMI. Points to infarction (cell death, permanent).","ST depression = below the isoelectric line; sometimes happens with ischemia, sometimes infarction. Points to ischemia (temporary).","A STEMI shows ST elevation in two anatomically contiguous leads (contiguous = sharing a common border, touching each other).","A STEMI cannot be diagnosed by telemetry; it takes a 12-lead EKG.","Serial EKGs may be ordered because ischemia and infarction can change over a few hours.","STEMI vs NSTEMI","ST segment — Elevated — Depressed or normal","QRS — Often wide (\"wide and ugly\") — Normal","T wave — Peaked, then inverted — Inverted","Troponin — Elevated — Elevated","Occlusion — More extensive — More of an incomplete occlusion or transient thrombosis (shared with unstable angina)","Outcome — Larger infarct; people don't do as well — Better than a STEMI"]},{"s":"Initial Care of the ACS Patient▾","a":"initial-care-of-the-acs-patient","k":["Initial Care of the ACS Patient▾","Assess and stabilize","Consider ACS with chest discomfort, shortness of air, or other suggestive symptoms; remember atypical presentations in women, older patients, and patients with diabetes.","12-lead EKG within 10 minutes of arrival; repeat every 10 to 15 minutes if not definitive and MI is still suspected.","Airway, breathing, circulation. Sit the patient upright to support ventilation; supplemental oxygen per nasal cannula.","Baseline vital signs ASAP, including O2 saturation.","Telemetry ASAP to monitor for dysrhythmias (the number one complication) and ST changes. If you notice ST elevation on tele, the next best nursing action is a 12-lead EKG (cardiac nurses usually have a standing order).","IV access, for emergency or code medications.","Aspirin to chew and swallow (antiplatelet).","Assess pain with PQRST.","Pain Relief in ACS","Nitroglycerin first","0.4 mg sublingual is pretty standard, one at a time, spaced 5 minutes apart for persistent chest pain.","Check BP before each dose; it is likely to drop (a potent coronary vasodilator lowers peripheral vascular resistance).","If BP gets too low, switch to morphine.","Morphine","Eases the workload on the heart.","Decreases preload and afterload.","Helps with anxiety.","Patients often tolerate IV morphine better than nitroglycerin.","PQRST Pain Assessment","P — What started (precipitated) the pain?","Q — What does it feel like? Describe it for me.","R — Where is it, and does it radiate?","S — On a scale of 0 to 10, with 10 the worst imaginable, how bad is it right now?","T — When did it begin? (Timing helps decide the course of action.)","Labs and Ongoing Assessment","What to monitor","Cardiac markers: troponin is the most specific to cardiac muscle (some providers still order CK-MB or myoglobin).","Electrolytes, especially potassium, calcium, and magnesium: imbalances can be the root cause of dysrhythmias.","Hemoglobin and hematocrit; coagulation status (bleeding risk).","Heart sounds: listen for a murmur, gallop, or rub.","Lung sounds: an infarction in the left ventricle can lead to heart failure, and crackles could point to left-sided heart failure."]},{"s":"Treating Blockages: PCI, Thrombolytics & CABG▾","a":"treating-blockages-pci-thrombolytics-cabg","k":["Treating Blockages: PCI, Thrombolytics & CABG▾","The three things the health care system can do for blockages found on a heart cath.","PCI (percutaneous coronary intervention) — STEMI and NSTEMI. The first line of treatment for a confirmed MI; what the patient gets more times than not. — Within 90 minutes of ED arrival.","Thrombolytic (fibrinolytic) therapy — A STEMI patient may also be a candidate. — Within 30 minutes of ED arrival; most effective within 6 hours of the initial coronary event.","CABG (coronary artery bypass graft, \"open-heart surgery\") — Patients with diabetes, multi-vessel disease (3 or more vessels), or a blockage the catheter can't reach. — Emergent, or semi-elective (cath today, CABG the day after tomorrow).","PCI (Angioplasty, the \"Balloon Procedure\")","What happens","The heart cath shows whether blockages exist, where, how bad, and whether there is collateral circulation (little vessels the body grew around the blockage to perfuse the other side). It also evaluates left ventricular function.","It is a left heart cath (through the brachial or femoral artery) to reach the coronary arteries.","A guide wire and balloon catheter go to the occlusion; the balloon inflates and smashes the plaque back against the wall, then deflates and is removed.","Sometimes a stent is placed to reestablish patency.","Advantages over open-heart surgery","Percutaneous, through the vascular system; no cracking the chest.","Local anesthesia, not general.","Ambulatory shortly after.","Significantly shorter hospital stay; back to work and life sooner.","More PCIs are performed than CABGs.","PCI Nursing Care","Recovery is very similar to a heart cath — review post-cath care in CV Labs & Diagnostics","What the CAD lecture adds for PCI","Monitor closely for MI progression: chest pain, EKG changes, new or different dysrhythmias, hemodynamic instability (unstable BP and heart rate).","Dual antiplatelet therapy: aspirin plus a drug like clopidogrel (Plavix). Possibly IV heparin during the procedure. It is a delicate balance; we don't want the patient to bleed out.","The sheath is usually removed 4 to 6 hours after the procedure; an arterial puncture needs manual pressure for a good 20 minutes.","Assess distal to the puncture: for a left groin site, compare the left foot's DP and PT pulses and cap refill to the right foot.","Watch for bleeding externally (the dressing) and internally: a heart rate creeping up can be the body compensating for lost blood volume.","Thrombolytic Therapy (\"Clot Busters\")","The drugs","Fibrinolytics: they lyse fibrin to dissolve thrombi in the coronary arteries and restore blood flow.","Examples: tPA and reteplase.","Per the lecture, given during the cardiac catheterization.","PCI is more commonly performed than thrombolytic therapy.","Monitor closely for bleeding after administration.","Who should NOT get them","History of a brain bleed or stroke, particularly hemorrhagic stroke.","Recent abdominal surgery (bleeding risk).","Active bleeding.","Coagulation studies that are out of range.","CABG (Coronary Artery Bypass Graft)","The procedure","The surgeon sews (anastomoses) a graft proximal and distal to the blockage, literally bypassing it. Up to six grafts (for example \"CABG times one,\" \"CABG times three\").","The patient's own vessels are used, such as the internal mammary artery or the saphenous vein (the long vein from the groin down the medial leg to the ankle). Veins have valves, so the graft is turned in favor of blood flow. Which vessel is…","Valve replacement is sometimes done at the same time.","Stroke and MI — Listed risks after open-heart surgery.","Infection — Sternal wound or the leg where the vein was harvested; poor healing is likelier with atherosclerosis or peripheral arterial/vascular disease (poor perfusion to the periphery) and can lengthen the stay.","Dysrhythmias — ICU for a while, then telemetry for the rest of the stay.","Pleural effusion — Fluid in the pleural cavity; third spacing.","Pericardial effusion and cardiac tamponade — Excess fluid in the pericardial sac (pericardial effusion is to the heart as pleural effusion is to the lungs). A lot of fluid quickly squeezes the heart and it can't maintain cardiac output: car…","Renal failure — Monitor creatinine and urine output very closely.","Pericardial effusion and tamponade come up again here — review them in Pericarditis"]},{"s":"Ongoing Care & Recovery▾","a":"ongoing-care-recovery","k":["Ongoing Care & Recovery▾","After the procedure","Continuous telemetry; rest, comfort, activity restriction for a while; good pain control.","Expect anxiety; teach at the level where the patient is, since everyone is in a different place. The nurse can be a game changer.","Timing matters for emotional and behavioral reactions. Example: resuming sexual activity, especially if that is what brought them in. Have those conversations before discharge so they understand their limitations.","Needs at home span physiological, psychological, mental, spiritual, economic, and vocational areas."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-pericarditis.html","t":"Pericarditis","secs":[{"s":"Pericarditis","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 4","The Pericardium","Causes","Pericarditis Pain","The Pericardial Friction Rub","Complications: Pericardial Effusion & Tamponade","Diagnostics","Treatment","Self-Test Flashcards","The first of three voiceovers on inflammatory, infective, and valvular heart disease. The friction-rub details from the CAD lecture (where pericarditis came up as an MI complication) are gathered here too, so the rub lives in one place."]},{"s":"The Pericardium▾","a":"the-pericardium","k":["The Pericardium▾","Layers and the potential space","The pericardium covers the heart. \"-itis\" means inflammation or infection: with pericarditis think inflammation (endocarditis is more about infection).","The fibrous pericardium is the outside; it circles back around into the visceral and parietal pericardium, with the pericardial space in between.","The pericardial space is a potential space holding usually 5 to 10 mL of fluid, just enough to keep the heart lubricated and smooth while it pumps.","Beneath are the myocardium and the endocardium."]},{"s":"Causes▾","a":"causes","k":["Causes▾","Idiopathic — Often we really don't know why it happens.","Infective organisms — A variety sometimes precede pericarditis.","After an MI — A complication of an MI; usually shows up a few days after, from the MI's inflammation.","Connective tissue diseases — The pericardium is connective tissue. Examples: rheumatoid arthritis (RA) and lupus."]},{"s":"Pericarditis Pain▾","a":"pericarditis-pain","k":["Pericarditis Pain▾","How it presents","Substernal / precordial (behind the breastbone), radiating to the left side of the neck, shoulder, and back.","A grating, oppressive feel (oppressive: hard to catch your breath).","Aggravated by breathing (mainly inspiration), coughing, and swallowing.","Worse lying down; better sitting up and leaning forward (leaning forward minimizes the stretch on the pericardium). A positional thing.","Pericarditis Pain vs MI Pain","After an MI you have to tell the two apart, since pericarditis is an MI complication.","Pericarditis pain","Onset usually more sudden.","Substernal, left precordial; may radiate.","Sharp and stabbing, or aching and dull; oppressive.","Worse on inspiration.","Body movement can increase it; leaning forward makes it better.","Can wax and wane.","Sublingual nitroglycerin won't help: nitro is a coronary vasodilator, and pericarditis has nothing to do with coronary vasodilation.","MI pain","Position is not going to matter.","Breathing isn't going to impact the pain.","Ischemia is what nitroglycerin addresses.","MI pain is described in full in CAD & ACS"]},{"s":"The Pericardial Friction Rub▾","a":"the-pericardial-friction-rub","k":["The Pericardial Friction Rub▾","The hallmark finding of pericarditis is the pericardial friction rub.","What it sounds like and why","A \"squeaky heart\"; a grating, continuous grinding sound, like two pieces of sandpaper rubbing together.","The visceral and parietal pericardium normally have a little fluid between them. Inflammation takes away that fluidity, and the two layers rub (CAD lecture).","Same idea as a pleural friction rub, when the visceral and parietal pleura are inflamed (for example, pleurisy).","How to listen","Stethoscope at the lower left sternal border.","Patient leaning forward (brings the heart closer to the chest wall).","Ask the patient to hold their breath to eliminate breath sounds and focus on the heart.","From the CAD lecture: if the grating disappears when the patient holds their breath, it is not cardiac in origin (it is respiratory)."]},{"s":"Complications: Pericardial Effusion & Tamponade▾","a":"complications-pericardial-effusion-tamponade","k":["Complications: Pericardial Effusion & Tamponade▾","Pericardial Effusion","Fluid in the pericardial space","Pericardial effusion is to the pericardium what pleural effusion is to the pleural space: third spacing, a collection of fluid that serves absolutely no purpose.","Symptoms come from the fluid squeezing the heart.","Cues: cough, tachypnea, difficulty breathing, and heart sounds that aren't crisp (fluid between the stethoscope and the heart).","The pericardial space doesn't expand much. As it fills, the heart gets squished, can't contract and release like it should, and cardiac output suffers.","Seen on an echocardiogram (or TEE), which delineates the heart's structures.","Cardiac Tamponade","Tamponade is time-sensitive and can be life-threatening. It develops when a pericardial effusion increases in volume.","Dyspnea, angina, restlessness, anxiety — Listed manifestations.","Decreased cardiac output (changes in vital signs) — Blood pressure is an indirect measure of cardiac output.","Marked jugular venous distension — Backup of fluid from the tamponade.","Very muffled heart sounds — Fluid around the heart.","Narrowed pulse pressure — See below.","Pulsus paradoxus (a classic) — See below.","Narrowed pulse pressure","Pulse pressure = systolic minus diastolic.","120/80 gives a pulse pressure of 40: pretty average.","In tamponade it narrows, such as 110/90: a pulse pressure of 20.","Pulsus paradoxus","BP normally drops a little with breathing, but not significantly. Significant = greater than 10 mmHg.","Systolic BP is 10 mmHg or more higher on expiration than on inspiration. (Lewis says it one way, UpToDate the other: 10 mmHg lower on inspiration. Same thing.)","For the skilled practitioner; the instructor has assessed it when the patient has an arterial line (systolic waxes and wanes with breathing). You won't get checked off on it.","1. Inflate the cuff beyond a palpable BP — You are palpating, not auscultating as in a normal BP.","2. Deflate gradually; note when sounds are first audible on expiration only — First number.","3. Note when sounds are audible on both inspiration and expiration — Second number.","4. A difference greater than 10 indicates tamponade — Lewis has a table, and there is a video on Canvas."]},{"s":"Diagnostics▾","a":"diagnostics","k":["Diagnostics▾","EKG — Diffuse, widespread ST elevation in almost all leads. ST elevation in only two contiguous leads points to an acute MI instead.","Echocardiogram — Determines whether pericardial effusion or cardiac tamponade has occurred.","WBC, sed rate, CRP — May be elevated (it's an \"-itis\"). Sed rate and CRP are non-specific inflammation markers: sed rate more chronic, CRP more acute, and neither tells where the inflammation is.","ST elevation in an MI is covered in CAD & ACS"]},{"s":"Treatment▾","a":"treatment","k":["Treatment▾","Bed rest, head of bed at 45 degrees — Plus measures to decrease anxiety.","Antibiotics — Only if bacteria is the causative agent; otherwise not indicated.","NSAIDs (the hallmark treatment) — Good old-fashioned NSAIDs: naproxen or high-dose ibuprofen (both over the counter). No morphine or opioids, because the problem is inflammation. Diagnosing it matters, because treatment differs from, say, p…","Colchicine — For recurrent pericarditis (covered again with arthritis and gout).","Prednisone taper — Only if NSAIDs are ineffective.","Pericardiocentesis (emergency) — Pericardiocentesis is to the pericardium as thoracentesis is to the pleural space: a needle removes the fluid. Per the lecture, removing about 5 to 10 mL can increase stroke volume by as much as 50%, letting…"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-endocarditis.html","t":"Infective Endocarditis","secs":[{"s":"Infective Endocarditis","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 5","What It Is","Subacute vs Acute","Who Gets It & What Causes It","Vegetations & Emboli","Manifestations","History & Diagnostic Tests","Prophylactic Antibiotics","Treatment & Nursing Care","Self-Test Flashcards","With pericarditis think inflammation; with endocarditis think infection. \"Infective endocarditis\" is the longer name, but it is usually just called endocarditis."]},{"s":"What It Is▾","a":"what-it-is","k":["What It Is▾","The endocardium and the valves","The endocardium is the inside of the heart, which is a cue to where the problem is.","The valves are a very movable part of the heart, and a lot of the manifestations of endocarditis relate to the valves.","Endocarditis occurs when blood flow within the heart lets the organism infect previously damaged valves or another endothelial surface."]},{"s":"Subacute vs Acute▾","a":"subacute-vs-acute","k":["Subacute vs Acute▾","Subacute","Affects those with pre-existing heart problems.","Typically milder in presentation.","Can be more chronic.","Acute","Affects healthy valves.","Manifests itself quicker.","Therapy needs to be prompt and vigorous."]},{"s":"Who Gets It & What Causes It▾","a":"who-gets-it-what-causes-it","k":["Who Gets It & What Causes It▾","Two things have to happen","A history of damaged epithelium, such as a valve replacement or a defect.","A port of entry for infection to reach the endocardium: a sore, a dental procedure, IV drug use.","Causative organisms","Most common: Staphylococcus aureus and Streptococcus viridans (bacterial).","Other possible pathogens: fungi and viruses, so not all endocarditis is bacterial."]},{"s":"Vegetations & Emboli▾","a":"vegetations-emboli","k":["Vegetations & Emboli▾","Why vegetations matter","Infection on the moving valves develops vegetations, for example mitral valve vegetation between the left atrium and left ventricle. Bacterial endocarditis of the mitral valve covers it with large, irregular vegetations.","The valves are constantly opening and closing, so vegetations can slough off and become emboli, dispersed into the vascular system.","Left-sided vegetation","Leaves the heart into the organs.","Can cause a limb infarction or an infarction in any organ of the body.","Right-sided vegetation","Blood from the right side goes to the lungs.","Like a pulmonary embolus."]},{"s":"Manifestations▾","a":"manifestations","k":["Manifestations▾","Very non-specific at first","Timing: the transcript says it typically occurs \"within 10 two weeks\" of the infectious organism (unclear wording in the transcript, so no firm number is given here).","Low-grade fever, chills, malaise, fatigue, anorexia, joint pain, muscle pain. If you didn't know better, you'd think it was the flu.","Clubbing of the fingers is the odd one out, and it comes later in the disease process, not early.","Findings Unique to Endocarditis","The instructor says to commit these to memory: \"you don't hear about these in any other condition,\" and they \"would be great NCLEX questions.\"","Splinter hemorrhages — A longitudinal streak in the nail bed. — Vascular manifestation: vegetations leaving the left side of the heart and going to the periphery.","Petechiae — Little red hemorrhagic spots; smooth, flat (a macule), not raised, not painful. — Micro-emboli of vegetation. Fingers, eye, mouth, ankles, antecubital and popliteal areas.","Osler nodes — Tender papules on the fingertips and toes; look like they have a little pus in them. — Relatively uncommon, but highly suggestive of (specific to) infective endocarditis.","Janeway lesions — Flat, macular, painless red spots on the palms and soles. — Relatively uncommon, but highly suggestive.","Roth spots — Little hemorrhagic areas in the back of the retina. — Relatively uncommon, but highly suggestive.","Telling the two apart: Osler nodes are tender (fingertips and toes); Janeway lesions are painless and flat (palms and soles).","Clinical Manifestations","New-onset murmur — A murmur is about the opening and closing of the valve. You should hear S1 and S2 crisply; damaged valves produce a murmur, an adventitious heart sound graded from 1 to 6.","Heart failure — The most common complication of infective endocarditis. Damaged valves leak blood back instead of letting it go one direction, the blood backs up, and the patient develops left- and right-sided heart failure."]},{"s":"History & Diagnostic Tests▾","a":"history-diagnostic-tests","k":["History & Diagnostic Tests▾","History — Recent dental, urological, or gynecologic procedure; IV drug use.","Blood cultures — Three cultures drawn over an hour apart from three different sites; positive results are indicative of endocarditis.","Echocardiogram — Evidence of endocardial enlargement."]},{"s":"Prophylactic Antibiotics▾","a":"prophylactic-antibiotics","k":["Prophylactic Antibiotics▾","Who gets prophylaxis","A mechanical heart valve.","A history of endocarditis.","A history of congenital heart disease.","A heart transplant.","Before which procedures","Any dental visit.","A biopsy of any kind.","Tonsil and adenoid removal.","A wound infection or urinary tract infection."]},{"s":"Treatment & Nursing Care▾","a":"treatment-nursing-care","k":["Treatment & Nursing Care▾","Treatment","Typically hospitalized initially; medications usually given IV.","Complete eradication may take weeks upon weeks; relapses are common.","Home care may include going home with a PICC line for IV antibiotics.","Nursing care and teaching","Identify and educate high-risk individuals so they can avoid infections.","Good rest; good oral hygiene (the mouth is where bacteria can enter).","Let the provider know before any dental procedure.","Refer IV drug users to drug rehab.","Monitor for the non-specific signs and symptoms that indicate a recurrence.","Endocarditis as a cause of valve regurgitation comes up again — review it in Valvular Heart Disease"]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-valvular-heart-disease.html","t":"Valvular Heart Disease","secs":[{"s":"Valvular Heart Disease","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 6","Valve Review","Types of Valve Problems","Manifestations","Conservative Management","Repairing the Valve","Replacing the Valve: Mechanical vs Biologic","After Valve Replacement","Self-Test Flashcards","The last of the three inflammatory, infective, and valvular voiceovers. The instructor says you really need to know your valves: where they are and what they do."]},{"s":"Valve Review▾","a":"valve-review","k":["Valve Review▾","What the valves do","Valves let blood leave one chamber and go into the next. When the next chamber contracts, the valve slams shut, keeping blood moving in a unidirectional path. Stenosis, prolapse, or regurgitation messes with that and causes backflow.","AV valves: tricuspid and mitral. Semilunar valves: pulmonic and aortic.","The mitral lies between the left atrium and left ventricle; the aortic lies after the left ventricle, before systemic circulation.","Left heart valves take more of a beating, because the left side is the high-pressure system. A repaired or replaced valve is, more times than not, the mitral or the aortic.","Auscultation Landmarks","Aortic — Right sternal border, 2nd ICS — S2 heard best (the base of the heart).","Pulmonic — Left sternal border, 2nd ICS","Erb's point — Left sternal border, 3rd ICS — S1 and S2 both heard best simultaneously in one place.","Tricuspid — 4th ICS","Mitral — 5th ICS, left midclavicular line — S1 heard best (the apex; closure of the mitral valve)."]},{"s":"Types of Valve Problems▾","a":"types-of-valve-problems","k":["Types of Valve Problems▾","Stenosis — The door that won't open all the way. — Constriction or narrowing; blood backs up. — Often a history of rheumatic heart disease.","Regurgitation — The door that won't close all the way. — An incompetent valve; blood backs up into the previous chamber when it shouldn't. — Older age, or infective endocarditis.","Prolapse — The leaflets buckle. — Etiology unknown. The most common valve problem in the US; often seen in younger people and may improve with age.","Infective endocarditis damages the valves — review it in Infective Endocarditis"]},{"s":"Manifestations▾","a":"manifestations","k":["Manifestations▾","They start to look like heart failure","Lewis has a long list; you don't need to memorize all of it. Stick with the classic ones below.","Regardless of the cause, blood backs up, and the patient shows left and right heart failure.","Backup from the right side goes to the periphery; backup from the left side shows up in the lungs.","Classic manifestations: exertional dyspnea, weakness, fatigue, possibly chest pain, maybe a murmur."]},{"s":"Conservative Management▾","a":"conservative-management","k":["Conservative Management▾","Initially we try not to do surgery; treatment escalates if the patient becomes symptomatic enough.","Prophylactic antibiotics as necessary — To prevent rheumatic fever, rheumatic heart disease, and infective endocarditis.","Sodium restriction — \"Salt sucks and water follows salt\": keep excess fluid off board.","Heart failure medications — To treat and control the heart failure that has resulted from the valve problem.","Anticoagulants and/or antidysrhythmics — May be ordered."]},{"s":"Repairing the Valve▾","a":"repairing-the-valve","k":["Repairing the Valve▾","If surgery is needed, we would rather repair the patient's own valve than replace it. Percutaneous options include a commissurotomy, a transluminal balloon valvuloplasty, or a percutaneous valve replacement (such as an aortic valve replacem…","Commissurotomy — \"-otomy\" means opening: the provider opens up a stenosed valve.","Balloon valvuloplasty — For a stiff valve: a balloon is inflated to open it up. Like a commissurotomy, the goal is to open a valve that isn't as open as it should be, without replacing it.","Annuloplasty — Surgery to repair and reshape the valve, using annuloplasty rings so it works better."]},{"s":"Replacing the Valve: Mechanical vs Biologic▾","a":"replacing-the-valve-mechanical-vs-biologic","k":["Replacing the Valve: Mechanical vs Biologic▾","Mechanical valve","More durable; lasts longer.","Needs lifetime anticoagulation: the body sees the mechanical pieces as foreign, and clots might form. That is the disadvantage.","For someone who will be compliant with lifetime anticoagulation.","You will hear a click (it is metal). Some say you can hear it from the bedside without a stethoscope.","Biologic valve (usually porcine, a pig valve)","Less durable; doesn't last as long (less than 8 to 10 years).","No need for anticoagulation.","For someone older with a shorter life expectancy, or someone unwilling or unable to adhere to anticoagulation.","No click; you hear only the closure of the valve.","Adventitious heart sounds so far: a rub, a murmur, a gallop, and now a click. Who has a click? Someone with a mechanical valve."]},{"s":"After Valve Replacement▾","a":"after-valve-replacement","k":["After Valve Replacement▾","Recovery and anticoagulation","Recovery is similar to a CABG; sometimes valves are replaced at the same time as the bypass (\"killing two birds with one stone\").","A prosthetic valve means anticoagulation. On warfarin (Coumadin), the INR is monitored for a lifetime.","Per the lecture: normal INR 0.8 to 1.1; goal INR on warfarin 2.5 to 3.5. Thin the blood, but not too thin; any higher and they could have issues.","Antidote for bleeding: vitamin K.","CABG recovery is the comparison point — review it in CAD & ACS","Eat vitamin K foods consistently (kale greens, for example); don't binge — Vitamin K messes with the clotting factors and reverses the effect of warfarin.","Electric razor instead of a straight-edge razor — Take measures not to bleed.","Monitor for any signs of excessive bleeding — Anticoagulated.","Avoid an MRI — The valve has metal.","Wear a medical alert bracelet or card — Always."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"week1-big-picture-overview.html","t":"Big Picture Overview","secs":[{"s":"Big Picture Overview","a":"","k":["Week 1 · Topic 7","The Lectures' Own Order","The Five Topics at a Glance","Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic","High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place","This page doesn't teach anything new. It is a map of Week 1's five topics, so you can see how they fit together instead of studying each one as if it were unrelated to the rest. The Comprehensive Study Guide is separate: it puts all five pa…"]},{"s":"The Lectures' Own Order▾","a":"the-lectures-own-order","k":["The Lectures' Own Order▾","Week 1 runs from the tests, to the most common problem (blocked coronary arteries), to the three inflammatory, infective, and valvular conditions. The instructor introduced them as one set: \"caring for the patient with inflammatory, infecti…","The Tools — How We Look at the Heart — Topic 2, CV Labs & Diagnostics — Serum markers (troponin for MI, BNP for heart failure, CRP and ESR for inflammation), then non-invasive tests (12-lead, tele, Holter, stress test, echo), then invasive …","The Blocked Artery — CAD to MI — Topic 3, CAD & Acute Coronary Syndrome — A continuum: stable angina, unstable angina, NSTEMI, STEMI. Atherosclerosis causes almost all of it. Time is muscle: PCI within 90 minutes, thrombolytics within 30.","Outside the Heart — Inflammation — Topic 4, Pericarditis — The pericardium is inflamed; the friction rub is the hallmark. Its danger is fluid: effusion, then tamponade.","Inside the Heart — Infection — Topic 5, Infective Endocarditis — The endocardium and valves are infected; vegetations break off as emboli. Its most common complication is heart failure.","The Doors — Valves — Topic 6, Valvular Heart Disease — Stenosis (won't open), regurgitation (won't close), prolapse (buckles). All of them end up looking like heart failure."]},{"s":"The Five Topics at a Glance▾","a":"the-five-topics-at-a-glance","k":["The Five Topics at a Glance▾","2 — CV Labs & Diagnostics — Troponin is the most sensitive marker of myocardial damage (up in 2 to 3 hours, back in 10 to 14 days); a 12-lead can diagnose an MI but telemetry only monitors; only a left heart cath reaches the coronary arteri…","3 — CAD & Acute Coronary Syndrome — Stable angina is predictable and relieved by rest or nitro; unstable angina, NSTEMI, and STEMI are ACS. One nitro without relief at home means call 911 and chew an aspirin; always check BP before nitro. D…","4 — Pericarditis — Inflammation of the pericardium: positional pain, better leaning forward, worse on inspiration, not relieved by nitro; friction rub at the lower left sternal border; diffuse ST elevation; treated with NSAIDs. Watch for ta…","5 — Infective Endocarditis — Needs damaged epithelium plus a port of entry; flu-like at first; splinter hemorrhages, petechiae, Osler nodes, Janeway lesions, and Roth spots are the findings to memorize; three blood cultures; weeks of IV ant…","6 — Valvular Heart Disease — Know your valves and landmarks; repair before replace; mechanical valves last longer but need lifetime anticoagulation and click, biologic valves need none but wear out sooner."]},{"s":"Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","a":"ideas-that-show-up-in-more-than-one-topic","k":["Ideas That Show Up in More Than One Topic▾","Each idea is fully taught in one place, but it resurfaces as supporting detail elsewhere. If something feels like déjà vu, this table shows why.","Troponin — CV Labs & Diagnostics — \"Serum Cardiac Markers\" — CAD & ACS: it is what separates unstable angina (normal) from NSTEMI and STEMI (elevated), and it helps rule cardiac in or out with epigastric pain.","12-lead EKG vs telemetry — CV Labs & Diagnostics — \"Non-Invasive Procedures\" — CAD & ACS: a STEMI can't be diagnosed by tele; ST elevation on tele means get a 12-lead.","ST elevation — CAD & ACS — \"Diagnosing ACS: EKG Changes\" (two contiguous leads = MI) — Pericarditis: diffuse ST elevation in almost all leads.","Heart cath care and complications — CV Labs & Diagnostics — \"Invasive Procedures\" — CAD & ACS: PCI recovery is very similar to a heart cath.","CRP and sed rate — CV Labs & Diagnostics — \"Inflammation Markers\" — Pericarditis: may be elevated, since it is an \"-itis.\"","Echocardiogram — CV Labs & Diagnostics — Pericarditis (checks for effusion and tamponade) and Infective Endocarditis (endocardial enlargement).","Pericarditis as an MI complication; the friction rub — Pericarditis — \"The Pericardial Friction Rub\" — CAD & ACS: pericarditis a couple of days after an MI.","Pericardial effusion and cardiac tamponade — Pericarditis — \"Complications\" — CV Labs & Diagnostics (poking the pericardium during a cath) and CAD & ACS (after CABG).","Heart failure — Its own content comes later in the course (\"we will talk about heart failure soon\"). — CAD & ACS (MI complication; crackles), Infective Endocarditis (most common complication), Valvular Heart Disease (what valve problems loo…","Murmurs — Infective Endocarditis — new-onset murmur, graded 1 to 6 — CAD & ACS (papillary muscle dysfunction) and Valvular Heart Disease (rub, murmur, gallop, click).","Prophylactic antibiotics — Infective Endocarditis — \"Prophylactic Antibiotics\" — Valvular Heart Disease: preventing rheumatic fever, rheumatic heart disease, and endocarditis.","Anticoagulation and bleeding — Valvular Heart Disease — warfarin, INR, vitamin K — CV Labs and CAD & ACS: heparin during caths and PCI, dual antiplatelets, bleeding after thrombolytics."]},{"s":"High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","a":"high-alert-safety-rules-all-in-one-place","k":["High-Alert Safety Rules — All in One Place▾","The danger callouts and safety statements from across the five pages, condensed. Click through to a topic for the full explanation.","CV Labs & Diagnostics — Telemetry cannot diagnose; get a 12-lead. After a cath: watch for bleeding and hematoma, hold pressure, bed rest with the leg straight and head of bed below 30 degrees after a femoral approach, check distal pulses, c…","CAD & ACS — One nitro without relief at home: call 911 and chew an aspirin. Check BP before every nitro. Never combine nitro with ED drugs; never defibrillate over a nitro patch. 12-lead within 10 minutes of arrival. A rising heart rate aft…","Pericarditis — Cardiac tamponade is time-sensitive and life-threatening: muffled heart sounds, JVD, narrowed pulse pressure, pulsus paradoxus. Emergency pericardiocentesis.","Infective Endocarditis — Acute endocarditis needs prompt, vigorous therapy. Prophylactic antibiotics before dental work and other listed procedures for high-risk patients. Vegetations can embolize: left side to organs and limbs, right side …","Valvular Heart Disease — Mechanical valve: lifetime anticoagulation and INR monitoring; vitamin K is the antidote; consistent vitamin K intake; electric razor; no MRI; medical alert bracelet."]}]},{"f":"medsurg1-week1-respiratory.html","t":"Respiratory","secs":[{"s":"Respiratory","a":"","k":["Med Surg I Review · Week 1","Respiratory Labs & Diagnostics","Upper Respiratory Problems","Pneumonia","COPD","Tuberculosis","Lung Cancer","Self-Test Flashcards","Med Surg I (NUR 334) review content. Built from the NUR 334 Week 1 voiceovers (respiratory labs and diagnostics, upper respiratory problems, obstructive sleep apnea, trachs, pharyngitis, head and neck cancer, pneumonia, COPD, TB, lung cance…"]},{"s":"Respiratory Labs & Diagnostics▾","a":"respiratory-labs-diagnostics","k":["Respiratory Labs & Diagnostics▾","The NUR 334 instructor: every system starts with a labs-and-diagnostics voiceover, you are held accountable for the related nursing care (pre- and post-procedure) of each one, and you should be able to explain each test to a patient. ABGs a…","Pulse Oximetry","O2 sat","The amount of hemoglobin that is carrying oxygen.","Normal: 95% or greater (voiceover); 95 to 100 (live lecture). Less than 90% can indicate hypoxemia (live lecture).","A low sat could mean hypoventilation, atelectasis, a pneumothorax, or other lung issues.","Not invasive; intermittent (with vital signs, Q4) or continuous (fresh post-op). Commonly used to titrate oxygen.","Best on a finger without nail polish (or fungus); the earlobe or toe is sometimes used, but a finger probe belongs on a finger.","On oxygen, we don't love 100%: wean it (for example from 4 L to 2 L nasal cannula). The exception is hyperoxygenating before trach suctioning. 100% on room air is fine.","The bus analogy: the red blood cell is the bus, hemoglobin is the seats (iron is what the seats are made of), and oxygen is the passengers. The O2 sat is how full the buses are; the hematocrit is how much of the highway is made up of buses.","Sputum Studies","The instructor says to commit these three to memory.","Culture and sensitivity (C&S) — Bacteria. The culture identifies the pathogen; the sensitivity shows which antibiotic works. Results can take 48 to 72 hours.","Cytology — Cancer cells (malignancy or abnormal changes); ordered when lung cancer is suspected.","Acid-fast bacillus (AFB) — Active tuberculosis; the definitive diagnosis of TB.","Collecting sputum","From deep in the lungs, not saliva: have the patient cough (expectorate), don't let them just spit in the jar.","Sterile container; to the lab as soon as possible (not sitting on the bedside table all day).","The morning specimen is best: secretions have settled overnight and the patient hasn't eaten or drunk.","Suction the patient if they can't cough productively.","Imaging","Chest X-ray — A two-dimensional picture of the lungs (and heart and chest wall): pneumonia, masses, pleural effusions. — Remove metal between the neck and waist (bra straps, necklaces). Usually two views. The voiceover names the most common…","CT (CAT) scan — Structures in cross-section; better visualization. — May need sedation to lie still or for claustrophobia; hard table, loud clicking. Contrast is provider preference and is usually iodine-based: ask about contrast allergy (a…","MRI — Lesions hard to assess by CT; distinguishes vascular from non-vascular structures. — Contrast is not iodine-based. Closed MRI may need sedation for claustrophobia. Remove all metal.","PET scan — A radioactive tracer through the IV; looks at physiology and function (blood flow, oxygen uptake, sugar uptake), not structure. — Used a lot for cancer (has it spread?) and some brain disorders.","Shellfish and contrast: research says a shellfish (or iodine) allergy is no longer linked to contrast reactions, so in practice it isn't a contraindication, but NCLEX and standardized tests \"can be late to the game.\" An allergy to contrast …","CT with contrast and the kidneys: if the creatinine is elevated (for example, \"their creatinine's 2\"), let the provider know before the scan. Whether to proceed is the provider's risk-benefit call.","TB Testing","Skin test (Mantoux / PPD) and IGRA","The TB skin test is intradermal (needle at a 10 to 15 degree angle) and should raise a bleb. Read in 48 to 72 hours.","The IGRA (interferon gamma release assay; QuantiFERON-TB Gold) is a blood test; no return visit to read it. Neither test is better than the other.","Both are screening tests: they do not tell latent from active TB. Positive screen, then chest X-ray; positive chest X-ray, then sputum for AFB.","Reading the skin test (5, 10, and 15 mm) is under Tuberculosis below","Bronchoscopy","What it does","Scoping (directly visualizing) the bronchi and airways.","Obtain a biopsy; remove secretions the patient can't expectorate (lavage); remove a foreign body; diagnose infection, tumors, bleeding.","Nursing care","Signed consent (invasive). Oropharynx anesthetized; sedation (conscious sedation or general).","NPO before: 8 to 12 hours.","NPO until the gag reflex returns.","Blood-tinged mucus is expected: document and continue to monitor; no need to call the provider.","After a biopsy, watch for bleeding (an unexplained rising heart rate) or a pneumothorax (abrupt shortness of air).","Lung Biopsy","Transbronchial — Through a bronchoscope, in the endoscopy suite.","Transthoracic needle aspiration (percutaneous) — A needle through the thorax, with CT (or ultrasound) guidance, in radiology.","Open lung biopsy — An incision, in the OR.","VATS (video-assisted thoracic surgery) — A camera, in the OR.","\"-scopy\" means scoping an orifice (bronchoscopy, colonoscopy, cystoscopy); endoscopy is the umbrella term. Break big words apart to figure them out.","Thoracentesis","Removing pleural fluid","The provider puts a large-bore needle into the pleural space for a pleural effusion (fluid where it doesn't belong; third spacing that reduces surface area for oxygen). \"-centesis\" removes fluid or gas (paracentesis is the abdominal version…","Can be diagnostic (analyze the fluid), therapeutic (relieve dyspnea), or both.","Before: consent; sit upright with elbows on the overbed table (opens the intercostal spaces); stay very still and don't talk.","After: a chest X-ray to rule out a pneumothorax; assess for hypoxia. Risks: pneumothorax, bleeding, infection.","Pulmonary Function Tests & Peak Flow","PFTs","Measure lung function with respect to time (seconds); usually done by respiratory therapy, nose pinched, mouth sealed on the device.","Total lung capacity: inhale as deep as you can and exhale as deep as you can.","FEV1 (forced expiratory volume in 1 second): a deep breath in, then push it out as fast as you can. It is lower in obstructive pulmonary disease, where getting air out is the problem.","Peak flow meter","More of an asthma device (peds content), carried by the patient to check their own FEV1.","They track their personal best; a drop from it may signal an early asthma attack."]},{"s":"Upper Respiratory Problems▾","a":"upper-respiratory-problems","k":["Upper Respiratory Problems▾","Epistaxis (Nosebleed)","Causes","Irritation (dry air), trauma, infection, a foreign body (kids), tumors.","Hypertension; blood dyscrasias; anticoagulants; chemotherapy.","With a spontaneous nosebleed, ask about anticoagulants: they affect the cause and how fast it stops. Antidotes: protamine sulfate for IV heparin, vitamin K for warfarin.","Anterior (most common)","Upright, leaning forward (leaning back sends blood down the throat: aspiration risk). Keep calm.","Pinch the soft tissue just below the bony prominence; pinching the bone won't stop it. Breathe through the mouth.","Lateral pressure with ice to vasoconstrict; nasal tampon or packing if needed.","Discourage nose blowing.","Posterior (less common, more serious)","An emergency; usually needs hospitalization (and often a specialist). Hard to stop because you can't pinch it.","Balloon catheter (tamponade) or posterior packing; can impede breathing.","Humidification, oxygenation, bed rest, pain control, oral care.","Teaching: saline spray, humidification, avoid aspirin and NSAIDs, avoid strenuous activity.","Live-lecture priority: for a patient with nasal packing, the priority assessment is the oxygen saturation: packing can obstruct the airway. Pain and dressing drainage matter, but think ABCs first.","Acute Pharyngitis","Viral — The most common; 9 times out of 10 a sore throat is viral. — Symptomatic care: soothing treatments, analgesics, antipyretics. No antibiotics.","Bacterial (streptococcal, group A strep) — Only about 1 in 10 adults. Sudden sore throat, swollen red tender tonsils, lymphadenopathy, fever. Hard to tell from viral by symptoms, so a rapid strep test is done. — Requires antibiotics. Not ev…","Fungal (thrush) — Candida albicans; for instance in patients on antibiotics. — Nystatin (yellow solution) swish and spit or swish and swallow.","Obstructive Sleep Apnea (OSA)","What and who","Repetitive collapse of the upper airway during sleep (the mandible drops back and blocks it). Apnea = breathing stops; hypopnea = it slows considerably.","Primary risk factors: increased age, male, obesity (large neck circumference), and nasopharyngeal structural abnormalities. Smoking is a less established risk factor.","Signs: waking tired, loud snoring and noises (often reported by the partner), morning headaches, daytime sleepiness; witnessed apnea on the hospital monitor.","Complications: motor vehicle crashes, neuropsychiatric dysfunction (lost REM sleep), pulmonary pressure, hypertension, heart failure, metabolic syndrome.","Gold standard diagnosis: polysomnography (a sleep study).","Treatment","Weight reduction, exercise; avoid alcohol and smoking; sleep on the side, not the back; sleep hygiene (routine, off the phone and TV).","Oral appliances (keep the mandible pulled up) are preferred over CPAP for mild to moderate cases.","Surgery: tissue removal, jaw repositioning, or (in serious cases) a trach.","The big problem is adherence: an estimated 20 to 40% don't use their CPAP. If a CPAP patient isn't rested, ask whether they are actually using it.","Pressure — One continuous setting the whole time — Two settings: higher on inhalation, lower on exhalation (easier to push air out)","Main use — The mainstay for OSA; spontaneous breathing, keeps the airway open — COPD (trouble exhaling), neuromuscular disease, chest wall deformities, OSA","Oxygen — No supplemental O2 at home (sometimes added in the hospital) — Not stated","Cost and complexity — Simplest, works well, cheap — Fancier, more expensive; often a last step to prevent intubation","Trach Review","Parts","Flange (faceplate on the outside), outer cannula, inner cannula (inside the outer cannula; removed for cleaning), and the obturator (only for reinserting a trach that came out).","Shiley (white plastic): has a cuff; disposable inner cannula you throw away at trach care; every fresh post-op trach starts as a Shiley.","Jackson (metal): no cuff; the inner cannula is cleaned with a brush; used for long-term or permanent trachs.","The cuff and pilot balloon go together: inflated pilot balloon = inflated cuff. The cuff is inflated when the patient is on a ventilator (so all air goes from the vent to the patient) and typically deflated off the vent. A speaking valve re…","An oral ET tube is used only about a week to 10 days before a trach is placed (FYI). Trachs can be temporary (downsized, then removed) or permanent.","Trach suctioning and care are fair game on the exam.","The trach comes out: the obturator is always taped at the bedside, with a spare trach and a bag-valve device with a trach adapter. Take out the inner cannula, put the obturator in the outer cannula, slightly extend the neck, guide it into t…","Preventing thick secretions (live-lecture question)","The question asks about preventing thickening: changing the inner cannula treats it but doesn't prevent it; low Fowler's is too low for the airway; vital signs don't prevent it. Read what the question is actually asking before you look at t…","Head & Neck Cancer","Who and why it is devastating","Tobacco causes 85%; more men; usually over 50. Under 50, often linked to HPV.","Symptoms (hoarseness, dysphagia, a neck mass) come late; very few early symptoms means high mortality. Most have locally advanced disease at diagnosis.","Surgery is first-line (then radiation and chemo): vocal cord stripping, laryngectomy (removal of the voice box), tracheostomy, lymph node removal, neck dissection.","Huge impact on body image (and it's the airway).","After a laryngectomy","A permanent tracheostomy; the natural voice is lost.","Air no longer enters through the nose or mouth, only the stoma; sputum comes out the stoma.","The patient can eventually eat and drink (the esophagus is intact), but has tube feeding at first (no swallowing for about 10 days to 2 weeks while swelling settles).","Code on a laryngectomy patient: do not bag the mouth and nose. Bag the stoma, using a pediatric face mask that fits over it.","Artificial larynx (electrolarynx) — Probably the most common; held against the throat to vibrate. Usable right after surgery, easy, well understood (sounds a bit robotic).","Tracheoesophageal voice restoration — A valve placed in the area lets the patient talk.","Esophageal speech — The oldest; hands-free with no device, but hard to learn: air is sucked into the esophagus and \"burped\" out to form words.","Post-op care (including radical neck dissection)","Airway is number one; trach collar with O2 and humidification.","Cough regularly; suction (Yankauer and deep trach suctioning). A mucus plug can cause a code.","Blood-tinged sputum can be expected for the first couple of days.","Pulmonary toilet, stoma care, pain control (enough for good pulmonary toilet); Jackson-Pratt drain.","Often malnourished before surgery: tube feedings early. Physical and speech therapy; psychosocial support."]},{"s":"Pneumonia▾","a":"pneumonia","k":["Pneumonia▾","An acute infection of the lung parenchyma (the functional unit, where the alveoli exchange O2 and CO2). Can be bacterial, viral, or fungal.","Risk factors","Older age; altered level of consciousness (weak cough, aspiration risk); can't expectorate secretions.","Immobility or bed rest; debilitating illness; malnutrition; smoking.","Tracheal intubation (on a ventilator in the ICU).","Picture it: an older man from a nursing home after a stroke, low albumin, a smoker.","Classifications","CAP (community-acquired): not hospitalized or living in long-term care within 14 days of symptoms.","HAP (hospital-acquired): begins 48 hours or more after admission, not present on admission; non-intubated patients.","VAP (ventilator-associated): on a ventilator in the ICU.","Viral — The most common; may be mild and self-limiting (usually resolves in 3 to 4 days) but can be life-threatening. Cough is scanty or non-productive.","Bacterial — Usually more serious; sometimes hospitalized. Cough is typically productive, possibly purulent.","Aspiration — Material from the mouth or stomach enters the trachea and lungs. Risk: decreased LOC, dysphagia (thin liquids), NG and feeding tubes (Dobhoff tubes are placed post-pyloric). Can be silent (no vomiting or coughing).","Opportunistic — Immunocompromised patients.","Manifestations and diagnosis","Often follows a simple respiratory infection (a \"post-influenza pneumonia\"), then fever, chills, cough, malaise, painful deep breaths and coughing, dyspnea, fatigue, myalgia; crackles.","In the elderly, a new onset of confusion can be one of the first signs of any infection.","Severe cases: respiratory distress and failure (patients with comorbidities tolerate it worst).","Chest X-ray: infiltrates (haziness where it should be clear).","CBC with diff: leukocytosis (high WBC). An acute bacterial infection may show a shift to the left: more neutrophils, including immature neutrophils released early.","Sputum C&S identifies the bacteria and the antibiotic; a viral infection grows no bacteria (and gets no antibiotics).","Don't mix these up (the instructor says exam options will test it): leukocytosis = increased WBCs; leukopenia = decreased WBCs.","Prevention","Pneumococcal vaccine for those at risk or of age; flu vaccine.","Don't smoke; balanced diet.","In the hospital: know who is at risk; incentive spirometer, turn-cough-deep breathe, ambulate.","Standard precautions; hand hygiene.","Care","Trend vital signs and pulse ox; listen to lungs at the start of the shift for a baseline.","Oxygen as needed to keep the sat above 95%.","Pulmonary toilet; the incentive spirometer (make sure they use it and document it). Chest physiotherapy.","Increase fluids to thin secretions (unless contraindicated, such as severe heart failure).","Ambulation balanced with energy conservation (cluster care).","Antibiotics if bacterial; analgesics; antipyretics.","Live-lecture question: what to do for a pneumonia patient","Appropriate: acetaminophen for fever (a higher temperature raises the cells' oxygen demand), notify the provider, check urine output (hydration), and encourage fluids.","Not appropriate: complete bed rest (it worsens pneumonia)."]},{"s":"COPD▾","a":"copd","k":["COPD▾","Obstructive pulmonary disease is an umbrella term for asthma, chronic bronchitis, and emphysema. Asthma is taught in peds, not med surg. COPD usually means emphysema and/or chronic bronchitis. It is preventable and treatable.","Emphysema","Alveolar damage: alveoli overinflated, stretched, destroyed.","Impaired gas exchange at the parenchyma.","Barrel chest, dyspnea, but not the daily sputum of bronchitis.","Chronic bronchitis","Inflammation with excessive mucus in the bronchi.","Coughing something up every single day.","Findings","The problem is getting air out, not in. Diagnosis: history, symptoms, spirometry.","Barrel chest: AP diameter closer to 1:1 instead of the normal 2:1 (on X-ray: widened AP diameter, flattened diaphragm).","Decreased or diminished breath sounds; wheezes, rhonchi, crackles (especially in an exacerbation); prolonged expiration.","Advanced disease: pursed-lip breathing (creates positive pressure to push air out; most patients already do it), neck vein distension and peripheral edema (from pulmonary hypertension backing things up), and cachexia (they burn so many calo…","Nursing problems: activity intolerance (sat may drop talking or eating), ineffective breathing pattern (usually too fast), ineffective airway clearance, impaired gas exchange, anxiety, poor nutrition.","Nursing implications","Smoking cessation: the single most effective and cost-effective intervention (Kentucky has many smokers).","Flu and other vaccines; catch respiratory infections early.","Inhaler therapy is the mainstay of self-management; adherence is critical (non-adherent patients end up with exacerbations). Long-acting beta agonists and long-acting muscarinic antagonists bronchodilate; inhaled steroids reduce inflammatio…","Home or hospital O2 with a lower sat target than the average patient.","Tripod position and pursed-lip breathing (they usually already do both); huff coughing; energy conservation and relaxation; high calorie needs; psychosocial support.","COPD Exacerbation","When they come into the hospital","A flare-up: worsening dyspnea, cough, or sputum beyond their usual day-to-day variation, needing a change in treatment. The most common cause is a respiratory infection (often after an upper respiratory infection).","Mainstay: oxygen for hypoxemia. Sat goal: 88 to 92%. 99 to 100% is too high.","Non-invasive positive pressure (BiPAP) when hypercapnic (rising CO2).","Bronchodilators (MDI at home; nebulizer in the hospital); chest physiotherapy; antibiotics if bacterial.","CO2 narcosis, and never withhold oxygen. A long-term CO2 retainer's drive to breathe is low oxygen (ours is high CO2). Too much oxygen (for example 6 L with a sat of 99%) can take that drive away. Only an ABG proves someone is a CO2 retaine…"]},{"s":"Tuberculosis▾","a":"tuberculosis","k":["Tuberculosis▾","Caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis. Resistant to isoniazid (INH) and rifampin = multi-drug resistant TB (MDR-TB). Risk: poor, underserved, and minority populations; IV drug users; poor sanitation; crowded living; immunosuppression.","Primary — Inhaled; the immune response encapsulates the organism. Usually asymptomatic. — No","Latent — A persistent immune response with no clinical signs. Positive skin or blood test; chest X-ray may or may not show a Ghon nodule. — No","Active (reactivated) — Develops in about 5 to 10% of latent cases, possibly years later; higher risk in older, immunosuppressed people. Sputum positive for AFB. Fever, night sweats, weight loss, productive cough, hemoptysis for longer than …","Reading the TB Skin Test","Measure induration (the raised area), not redness. Redness that isn't raised is not positive. The instructor wants you familiar with these cut-offs.","15 mm or more — Healthy people with no risk factors.","10 mm or more — Immigrants, drug users, nursing home residents, young children.","5 mm or more — The immunosuppressed: HIV, recent contact with TB, organ transplant.","After a positive screen","Positive skin test or IGRA, then a chest X-ray. Clear chest X-ray (no Ghon nodule) = latent TB. Nodule present = more testing (sputum for AFB) for active TB.","TB Treatment & Care","Drug therapy","The mainstay; months (6 or more) to years. Strict adherence is essential. Learn it as \"isoniazid,\" not just INH.","Directly observed therapy (DOT): supervised dosing to increase adherence and decrease MDR-TB, and to assess adverse effects.","Isoniazid (INH) — Hepatotoxicity (with acetaminophen, one of the two hepatotoxic drugs to know in med surg); peripheral neuropathy.","Rifampin — Red-orange secretions (bright red urine that isn't hematuria).","Ethambutol — Visual acuity changes; trouble telling colors apart.","In the hospital","Airborne precautions in a negative pressure room (air pushed outside, not to other rooms).","A fitted HEPA mask (fit testing).","At home","Preventive drugs for high-risk contacts (a spouse, for example); test contacts.","Cover mouth and nose when coughing; mask in crowds.","Sputum for AFB every couple of weeks: three negative cultures typically means no longer infectious."]},{"s":"Lung Cancer▾","a":"lung-cancer","k":["Lung Cancer▾","Presentation and diagnosis","One of the most prevalent cancers in Kentucky. About 10% are found incidentally on an X-ray for something else.","Few early symptoms; often masked by a smoker's chronic cough. Most common symptom: a persistent cough (productive); also hemoptysis, weight loss, chest pain.","Chest X-ray: a mass (consolidation) or infiltrate (haziness). CT or MRI for metastasis.","Sputum cytology: only 20 to 30 of 100 patients with lung cancer test positive.","The definitive diagnosis is a biopsy: fine needle aspirate, bronchoscopy, or VATS (provider preference).","Care","Problems: airway clearance, ineffective breathing, anxiety, malnutrition, fatigue (space activities, allow rest), knowledge deficit.","Smoking cessation; anxiety reduction and rapport.","Surgery: pneumonectomy (whole lung), lobectomy, or wedge resection (surgeon preference); plus radiation and/or chemo.","After a pneumonectomy, no breath sounds on that side (sounds near midline may be referred)."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"medsurg1-week2-hematology.html","t":"Hematology: Anemia, Polycythemia, Central Lines & Blood Products","secs":[{"s":"Hematology: Anemia, Polycythemia, Central Lines & Blood Products","a":"","k":["Med Surg I Review · Week 2","Hematology Labs & Diagnostics","Anemia","Nursing Care of the Anemic Patient","Polycythemia","Central Venous Catheters","Blood Products","Transfusion Reactions","Self-Test Flashcards","Med Surg I (NUR 334) review content. Built from the eight NUR 334 Week 2 voiceovers: hematology labs and diagnostics, hematologic problems (RBCs), the anemias, nursing care of the anemic patient, polycythemia, central venous catheters, bloo…"]},{"s":"Hematology Labs & Diagnostics▾","a":"hematology-labs-diagnostics","k":["Hematology Labs & Diagnostics▾","The CBC","RBC count — Not talked about much clinically; the H&H is used far more.","Hemoglobin and hematocrit (H&H, \"the crit\") — See the values to memorize below.","WBC count — Normal 5,000 to 10,000. A differential breaks it into neutrophils, lymphocytes, monocytes, eosinophils, and basophils as percentages that always add up to 100%.","Platelet count — Normal starts at 150,000 (the upper end \"depending on where you look\").","\"Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas\" (neutrophils, lymphocytes, monocytes, eosinophils, basophils), carried forward from 325. The neutrophil is the most plentiful WBC and the first to arrive at an acute bacterial infection. Mature neutrophils ar…","Hemoglobin & Hematocrit","Commit these to memory \"forever\" (the instructor's words): Hemoglobin women 12 to 16, men 14 to 18. Hematocrit women 37 to 47%, men 42 to 52%.","Definitions","Hemoglobin: the iron-containing pigment of the RBC.","Hematocrit: the percentage of total blood volume made up of RBCs. The hematocrit is roughly three times the hemoglobin (a hemoglobin around 12 goes with a crit of 36).","A low crit is anemia (anemia doesn't cause it); the cause might be bleeding, bone marrow failure, dietary deficiency, renal disease. Too many RBCs = polycythemia.","Trend, don't read stand-alone values: a post-op H&H of 9.2 and 28.1 that was lower yesterday is trending up; keep an eye on it.","False lows and false highs","Fluid retention (heart failure, kidney failure): extra plasma lowers the percentage = a false low hematocrit, not true anemia.","Dehydration: less plasma raises the percentage = a false high hematocrit, not true polycythemia.","The bus analogy again: RBC = bus, hemoglobin = seats, iron = what the seats are made of, oxygen = passengers. O2 sat = how full the buses are (we want 95% or higher). Hematocrit = how much of the highway is buses.","RBC Morphology","Color, size, and shape; it helps the provider find the cause of an anemia. You don't need to memorize the MCV or MCHC numbers, just the terms.","MCV — Size (V = volume) — Normocytic (normal), microcytic (small, e.g. iron deficiency), macrocytic (large, e.g. the megaloblastic B12 and folic acid deficiencies; large but fragile)","MCHC — How concentrated the RBC is with hemoglobin — Normochromic (normal), hypochromic (low, pale)","Iron Studies & Other Anemia Tests","Serum iron — Low in iron deficiency anemia. About 70% of iron is in the hemoglobin of RBCs; the rest is stored as ferritin and bound to transferrin.","Serum transferrin — The protein that transports iron; decreased in iron deficiency anemia.","Serum ferritin — The protein that stores iron. Decreased ferritin is the most sensitive test for iron deficiency anemia.","Intrinsic factor antibodies — Intrinsic factor (made by the stomach's parietal cells) is needed to absorb B12. Autoimmune antibodies against it = pernicious anemia, the most common cause of B12 deficiency.","Serum B12 and folate — Assess the presence of each.","Stool for guaiac (occult blood) — For anemia of unknown origin; the GI tract (a GI bleed) is suspected first. Not needed if you can see frank blood.","EGD and colonoscopy — Scope for a bleed: esophagogastroduodenoscopy (top), colonoscopy (bottom); usually in the endoscopy suite.","Coombs test — Differentiates types of hemolytic anemia. Direct: suspected incompatible blood was hung. Indirect: screening before a transfusion (the \"type and screen\").","Bone marrow biopsy — For low RBCs, platelets, and WBCs without a known cause; the patient with aplastic anemia. Red marrow, usually the iliac crest. Informed consent, time-out, premedicate; gentle pressure after and assess the site for blee…","Transferrin transports; ferritin stores. These iron tests are for anemia of unknown origin, not for someone who bled in surgery yesterday.","Melena vs iron stools","Melena: dark, tarry, very malodorous, sticky stool from digested blood. Significant.","Stool on iron is also black, but that is expected. Teach the difference: blood is very malodorous and tacky."]},{"s":"Anemia▾","a":"anemia","k":["Anemia▾","Blood cell terms","Leukopenia = low WBCs; neutropenia = low neutrophils (big trouble: the first responders are gone); leukocytosis = too many WBCs (infection).","Thrombocytopenia = low platelets (thrombocytes). Erythrocytes = RBCs. \"-penia\" = low or deficient.","Pancytopenia = low RBCs, WBCs, and platelets (aplastic anemia).","Causes of Anemia","Decreased RBC production — Not enough hemoglobin synthesis (iron deficiency); impaired DNA synthesis (B12 or folic acid: the megaloblastic anemias); decreased RBC precursors (aplastic anemia, chemotherapy, chronic disease). Chronic kidney f…","Blood loss — Acute (ruptured spleen, ruptured aneurysm, surgery): no time to compensate; quick cardiac instability and a rising heart rate. Chronic (heavy menstrual periods, GI bleeding such as a peptic ulcer or ulcerative colitis): insidio…","Increased RBC destruction (hemolysis) — Sickle cell anemia (peds); a hemolytic transfusion reaction from incompatible blood.","Post-op patients commonly come back with a low H&H; that is expected. GI bleeding is always on the list for anemia of unknown origin.","Severity & Why the Symptoms Happen","Mild — 10 to 14 — Maybe tired with exercise.","Moderate — 6 to 10 — Cardiopulmonary symptoms, possibly even at rest.","Severe — Less than 6 — Symptoms at rest; can be life-threatening with comorbidities. \"A hemoglobin of 6 should make your heart rate go up as the nurse.\"","Fatigue — Not enough oxygen to the muscles, so not enough energy.","Pallor (palms, nail beds, conjunctiva) — Peripheral vasoconstriction sends blood to the vital organs (heart, lungs).","Tachycardia, thumping in the chest — Cardiac output rises to compensate.","Bone pain (sternum, iliac crest) — Erythropoietin rises and the marrow works to make more RBCs.","Angina, MI, worsening heart failure — Cardiac muscle hypoxia (severe anemia, life-threatening).","Hypoxia, dyspnea, increased respiratory rate — Overall poor oxygenation.","Write this down: CO = HR × SV. Cardiac output (blood ejected from the left ventricle per minute) = heart rate × stroke volume (blood ejected per beat). On med surg you measure it with vital signs: blood pressure is an indirect measure of ca…","The Types of Anemia","Iron deficiency — Poor intake (anorexia, can't afford food), poor gut absorption, blood loss (menstruation), hemolysis. — Chronic may be asymptomatic. Most common symptom: pallor (lost line of demarcation in the conjunctiva). Glossitis (red…","B12 (cobalamin) deficiency — Pernicious anemia (autoimmune; no intrinsic factor; the most common cause); bowel surgery or malabsorption; strict vegans; alcohol abuse; gastrectomy (lost parietal cells, not autoimmune). — Insidious GI signs (…","Folate deficiency — Usually decreased intake: pregnancy, alcohol abuse. — Looks a lot like B12 but without the neurologic problems; assess neuro signs to tell them apart. — Stated as \"microcytic normochromic, just like the other megaloblast…","Aplastic anemia — Usually idiopathic; sometimes thought autoimmune. — Pancytopenia: anemic (fatigue), leukopenic/neutropenic (infection risk), thrombocytopenic (bleeding risk). A whole different ballgame; diagnosed with a bone marrow biopsy…","Lecture oddity, left as stated: the folate slide is read as \"microcytic normochromic, just like the other megaloblastic anemias,\" while earlier the same lectures call the megaloblastic (B12 and folic acid) anemias macrocytic."]},{"s":"Nursing Care of the Anemic Patient▾","a":"nursing-care-of-the-anemic-patient","k":["Nursing Care of the Anemic Patient▾","Nursing problems","Activity intolerance (probably the most common): fine at rest, but heart rate up, palpitations, anxiety, and short of breath with activity. Fatigue is being tired even lying in bed.","Nutrition (not taking in or not absorbing nutrients).","Aplastic anemia: risk for bleeding (thrombocytopenia), infection (neutropenia), and fatigue (anemia), all in one patient.","Ongoing care","Trend labs; watch for hypoxemia; vital signs and O2 sat; neuro checks (B12).","Oxygen for the very anemic patient (fewer buses need all the help they can get).","Rest periods.","Keep them warm, but no heating pads (paresthesia risk).","Possible PRBC transfusion.","Treat the cause: nutrition, stop alcohol, supplements, find and stop the bleeding.","Medications","Iron (ferrous sulfate) — PO is inexpensive and easy; once daily is acceptable (newer evidence). Absorbed in the duodenum; best in an acidic environment, so before meals, but take with food for the first couple of weeks if it causes nausea (…","Vitamin B12 (cobalamin, cyanocobalamin) — PO, IM, subcutaneous, or intranasal. IM is preferred with severe neurologic problems. With some intrinsic factor present, PO megadoses (typically 1,000 mcg, of which only 2.4 mcg is absorbed) work a…","Folic acid — By mouth; no real adverse effects; a big vitamin in pregnancy.","Epoetin (synthetic erythropoietin) — For chronic kidney disease and cancer patients; often given with iron (\"the buses and the wheels\"). IV or subcutaneous about three times a week. Black box warnings; discontinued when the hemoglobin is ab…","Food teaching (a favorite select-all-that-apply topic)","Iron-rich foods; most books say give vitamin C with iron (the literature is mixed).","B12 is only in animal products: strict vegans need supplements.","Folic acid: green leafy vegetables, legumes, grains, orange juice, nuts."]},{"s":"Polycythemia▾","a":"polycythemia","k":["Polycythemia▾","An abnormally high H&H and RBC count. Three classifications.","Relative — Decreased plasma volume or dehydration: the false high hematocrit; not a true increase in RBCs.","Primary (polycythemia vera) — A genetic change during the person's lifetime. Not preventable; never goes away.","Secondary — Compensation for tissue hypoxia: COPD (the most common cause overall) or a lifetime at high altitude. \"My body compensates by making more buses.\"","Manifestations","Thicker, more blood: hypertension, headache, trouble concentrating.","Ruddy (dusky red) complexion; possible cyanosis.","Clots or hemorrhage: angina, MI, TIA, stroke. The most common serious acute complication is a stroke.","Hypermetabolic: night sweats, weight loss.","Pruritus (itching) worsened by a hot bath is classic; pain in the fingers and toes.","Collaborative care","Phlebotomy is the mainstay for chronic polycythemia: 300 to 500 mL every other day at first, eventually every few months.","Goal: hematocrit below 42 in women, below 45 in men.","Hydration (the blood is so viscous).","Anticoagulants (clot risk); mobility matters but clot risk is high.","Antihistamines (diphenhydramine) for serious itching.","Ongoing evaluation and support."]},{"s":"Central Venous Catheters▾","a":"central-venous-catheters","k":["Central Venous Catheters▾","Why a central line","TPN: very hypertonic (10 to 20% dextrose instead of 5%); peripheral veins can't handle it.","Chemotherapy: often vesicant (serious tissue damage if it infiltrates) and long-term.","Long-term antibiotics (for example, weeks for endocarditis).","Loss of peripheral access.","Non-tunneled (\"deep line\") — Provider or acute care NP; subclavian or internal jugular (IJ); tip usually in the superior vena cava. — Up to 5 ports (usually 2 or 3); 7 to 10 inches; multiple lumens can run incompatible infusions. Chest X-ra…","Tunneled (Hickman, Groshong, Broviac) — Surgeon, in the OR; tunneled through subcutaneous tissue, exits lower than it enters. — Stable; long-term frequent infusions; cancer floor and dialysis access. No needle sticks, but a prolonged break …","Implanted port (Port-a-Cath, power port) — Surgeon, in the OR; under the skin of the chest wall. — IV therapy up to a year or more; no external tubing when not accessed (less body-image impact). Accessed with a Huber needle (up to about 2,0…","PICC (peripherally inserted central catheter) — Specially trained PICC nurses at the bedside; usually the antecubital area, threaded so the tip lies in the superior vena cava. — A central line (the tip location decides it). Not tunneled; ab…","Complications of central lines","Bleeding at the site, air embolus, pneumothorax.","CLABSI (central line-associated bloodstream infection): a huge share of hospital-acquired infections; can cause sepsis and death.","CHG-impregnated dressings help prevent CLABSI; a StatLock keeps the catheter from moving (wiggling enlarges the insertion hole).","Removing a non-tunneled CVC or PICC (RN)","Check the INR first (especially on anticoagulants).","Patient supine or Trendelenburg (PICC: lying down, hand below heart level).","Valsalva (bear down or hum) during removal so air isn't sucked into the vessel.","PICC: withdraw slowly, inch by inch (too fast can spasm the vein).","Pressure 3 to 5 minutes; occlusive dressing.","Measure the catheter against the charted length so nothing is left in.","Nursing students at UK HealthCare may not provide direct CVC care (CLABSI bundle): no accessing or flushing ports, no pushing through a central line, no hanging primary fluids, no dressing changes. The one exception: hanging a secondary med…"]},{"s":"Blood Products▾","a":"blood-products","k":["Blood Products▾","Blood Types","Compatibility","Two antigen sets: ABO (A, B, both, or neither) and Rh (have it or don't).","You can only receive blood with antigens you already have; a foreign antigen causes a hemolytic reaction.","O negative = universal donor (no A, B, or Rh). AB positive = universal recipient.","Examples: B positive can receive O negative, O positive, B negative, B positive. A negative can receive only O negative and A negative.","You don't have to memorize the chart, but you must understand it well enough to answer, for example, a select-all-that-apply on which types an AB negative patient can receive. (Most common types, O positive and A positive, are not on the te…","When PRBCs Are Given","Less than 6 — Transfusion recommended except in extenuating circumstances.","6 to 7 — Transfusion probably given; is the patient symptomatic (low BP, compensating high heart rate)?","7 to 8 — Appropriate for patients undergoing surgery or with heart disease.","8 to 10 — Usually not, unless serious comorbidities: oncology, risk for MI or ACS, active bleeding.","Greater than 10 — Usually not, except in exceptional cases.","Before & During a Transfusion","Know the H&H and confirm the order — An informed nurse checks the hemoglobin when PRBCs are ordered.","Type and crossmatch within the last 72 hours — Type = their blood type; crossmatch = mixing a sample of the unit with their blood to check for hemolysis (the indirect Coombs). Redone every 72 hours.","Positive ID, signed consent, teaching — Premedicate if ordered (acetaminophen for fever risk, diphenhydramine for itching risk).","IV access: 20 gauge or larger (18 ideal) — Lewis says 22; the instructor doesn't accept 22 (\"we're gonna say 20 or higher\"). Smaller gauges can break down RBCs (hemolysis).","Blood (Y) tubing with a filter; normal saline only — Never dextrose or lactated Ringer's (hemolysis). Prime with saline, then blood; flush with saline at the end to get every last drop.","Have everything ready before getting the blood — Once it's on the floor you have 30 minutes to start it; if you can't, return it to the blood bank (never the med fridge).","Final ID check at the bedside — Textbook: two RNs (UK uses barcode scanning as the second check).","Stay with the patient the first 15 to 30 minutes — Cannot be delegated (and a student can't be the one to stay). Teach them to report itching, chills, shortness of breath, back pain, or feeling \"weird\"; a cold IV site is expected.","Vital signs at 15 minutes, then hourly, and at the end — If tolerated, increase the rate; aim for about 2 hours (250 to 350 mL per unit of PRBCs).","Never let it hang more than 4 hours — Bacterial proliferation. If not finished by 4 hours: take it down, document, tell the provider.","Know the post-transfusion orders — Repeat H&H? A diuretic (furosemide) between units for fluid-overload risk? A second unit?","The two time limits to commit to memory: 30 minutes to start once the blood reaches the floor, and 4 hours maximum hang time.","Other Blood Products","Packed RBCs (PRBCs) — The severely anemic (\"only the buses\") — No plasma, WBCs, or platelets; 250 to 350 mL; type and crossmatch required.","Platelets — Thrombocytopenia (often chemo patients); usually when the count is below 20,000 — 30 to 60 mL per bag (sometimes a \"six pack\"); stored at room temperature, good 1 to 5 days; agitate the bag (they clump); give as fast as tolerate…","Fresh frozen plasma (FFP) — Bleeding risk from lack of clotting factors (liver disease, hemophilia, von Willebrand's); rarely as a volume expander — About 250 mL; rich in clotting factors (prothrombin); ABO compatibility required; stored up…","Albumin — Low albumin (for example, liver patients with fluid shifted into the third space) — A colloid with \"pulling power\" that keeps or pulls fluid into the intravascular space. 5% equals plasma; 25% is hypertonic. Compatibility not a fa…"]},{"s":"Transfusion Reactions▾","a":"transfusion-reactions","k":["Transfusion Reactions▾","Suspect a reaction? Stop the transfusion first. Then hang a new bag and tubing of normal saline at the same IV site (don't just switch to the saline on the blood tubing; that tubing is full of blood). Keep the line open, assess and take vit…","Acute hemolytic (the most life-threatening) — Wrong (incompatible) blood. Immediate: facial flushing, fever, headache, low back pain (kidneys), hematuria, dyspnea, dropping BP, cardiac arrest. — Support the BP (it is shock; colloids such as…","Febrile non-hemolytic (the vast majority of fevers) — A gradual temperature rise (not immediate); no back pain, no hematuria, no hemodynamic compromise. — Notify the provider; probably acetaminophen and restart. — Leukocyte-reduced products…","Mild allergic (the most common type) — Itching, flushing, urticaria (hives). Sensitization to donor WBCs. — Stop, notify; diphenhydramine or a steroid; usually restart slowly. — Antihistamines and steroids beforehand; washed RBCs.","Severe allergic (anaphylactic) — Anxiety, urticaria, bronchospasm, respiratory and cardiovascular compromise, low BP, possible cardiac arrest. — Treat like a code: CPR, oxygen, epinephrine, antihistamines, steroids, maybe a beta-2 agonist. …","Bacterial sepsis — Rapid chills and high fever, vomiting, diarrhea, marked hypotension. — Stop and return the blood; cultures; notify; antibiotics, IV fluids, vasopressors (likely ICU). — Infuse within 4 hours; aseptic technique.","TACO (transfusion-associated circulatory overload) — Often an older patient with heart failure, blood given too fast: lung congestion, crackles at the bases, dyspnea, increased respirations, headache, distended neck veins. — The only one wh…","Leukocyte-reduced products","Leukocytes collected with whole blood can cause febrile non-hemolytic and allergic reactions.","Pre-storage leuko-reduction lowers that risk; most blood at UK is leukocyte-reduced.","Autologous donation vs autotransfusion","Autologous donation: donating your own blood before a planned surgery. Less common now (expensive, often wasted).","Autotransfusion: collecting blood lost during surgery (for example, open-heart) and giving it back. No reaction risk; it's their blood."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"medsurg1-week3-periop-fluids-electrolytes.html","t":"Perioperative Care, Fluids & Electrolytes","secs":[{"s":"Perioperative Care, Fluids & Electrolytes","a":"","k":["Med Surg I Review · Week 3","Perioperative Overview","Pre-op","Intra-op","Post-op","Fluid Volume Imbalances","Electrolytes","Self-Test Flashcards","Med Surg I (NUR 334) review content. Built from the eleven NUR 334 Week 3 voiceovers: perioperative overview and pre-op (two parts), intra-op, post-op (two parts), fluid volume imbalances, and the sodium, potassium, calcium, magnesium, and …"]},{"s":"Perioperative Overview▾","a":"perioperative-overview","k":["Perioperative Overview▾","Pre-op — The anesthesiologist assesses and introduces; care is planned with the patient. The surgical site is marked (preventing wrong-site surgery). Very anxiety-producing for the patient.","Intra-op — From being wheeled into the OR until transfer to the PACU. Nursing roles: scrub nurse, circulating nurse, RN first assist.","Post-op — The PACU: airway first, recovery from general anesthesia, pain management, close vital signs (patients can be hemodynamically unstable and change quickly). Then back to the ICU, step-down, or med surg unit.","Purposes and settings","Purposes: diagnosis, cure, palliation (treating symptoms), prevention, exploration, cosmetic improvement.","Ambulatory: home the same day. Same-day admit: surgery in the morning, admitted overnight (usually one night). Inpatient: in the hospital before and after.","Timing","Elective: not urgent; improves quality of life (cataracts). Low risk, but not no risk.","Semi-elective: more time-sensitive, but not needed within 24 hours (gallstones, fibroid bleeding).","Urgent: within 24 hours of diagnosis (hip fracture, an appendix about to rupture).","Emergency: can't be delayed, ideally within 2 hours (unstable GI bleed, subdural hematoma)."]},{"s":"Pre-op▾","a":"pre-op","k":["Pre-op▾","The Pre-op History","Steps in the interview","Review what is documented before the interview and just double-check it (asking the same questions repeatedly is annoying). Can happen in advance (a phone call) or on the day.","Confirm understanding of the procedure (teach-back: \"tell me about the procedure you're going to have\").","Previous surgeries and anesthetics; family history of anesthesia problems (malignant hyperthermia is familial).","All medications: prescription, over-the-counter, herbals.","Allergies and intolerances, including latex (see below).","Illicit drug use: not optional to ask, even if sensitive.","Tobacco: quitting 6 weeks before surgery is worth it (smoking compromises oxygen binding); quitting any time before is better than not at all.","Possibility of pregnancy.","End with: \"Is there anything else you'd like to share with me?\"","Latex allergy clues: allergy to kiwis, bananas, avocados, chestnuts, or a poinsettia plant; daily latex exposure; past reactions to balloons or condoms; a history of allergies and asthma. Latex allergy can be anaphylactic.","Review of Systems","Respiratory — Risk of atelectasis, infection, prolonged mechanical ventilation (a heavy smoker with COPD is harder to wean), bronchospasm.","Cardiac — Don't increase myocardial oxygen demand.","Hearing, vision, cognition — Know the baseline: is post-op confusion their baseline or the anesthesia?","Kidneys — Creatinine for kidney function.","Pregnancy — Is or has been pregnant.","Liver — Clotting and metabolism problems (affects how much medication).","Integument — Pressure ulcers, rashes, breakdown.","Musculoskeletal and mobility — Patients may be put in extreme positions during surgery.","Diabetes, thyroid — May need medication adjustments.","Steroids — Raise blood sugar, delay wound healing, impair skin integrity.","Fluids and electrolytes — Recent nausea, vomiting, diarrhea. Older patients have a narrow margin of fluid safety: a patient's age in a test question (an 82-year-old) usually plays into the answer.","Nutrition — Obesity; malnutrition shown in lab values.","The Joint Commission requires a history and physical (H&P) in the chart before the OR. Diagnostic results must be in the chart too. Teach the patient what they'll see and feel (a cold room, warm blankets, bright lights) and the process rule…","Informed Consent","The surgeon's job","Full disclosure: diagnosis, purpose, risks (even common non-serious and serious uncommon ones), alternatives, the risk of no treatment, who performs it, short- and long-term costs, hospital stay, pain, recovery, loss of function, scarring.","The patient must show comprehension; signing is voluntary.","The nurse's job","Witness the signature and act as the patient advocate.","You can answer follow-up questions, but if the patient expresses uncertainty about the procedure, explore it, and the next best action is to let the provider know they are having second thoughts.","Minors, unconscious, or mentally incompetent patients: a legal representative; an emancipated minor can consent. A true medical emergency can override consent when life is at stake.","Day of Surgery & the Pre-op Checklist","Fasting and checklist","No more blanket \"NPO after midnight.\" Typically liquids are held a few hours and food 8 or more hours, but follow the surgeon's orders (don't memorize the table; gut surgery may mean longer NPO).","Checklist: height and weight; baseline vital signs; H&P in the chart; signed consent; type and crossmatch; latest labs (H&H, coags/PT-INR); surgical site marked; ID and allergy bands; NPO since when; recent glucose; skin prepped (CHG wipes …","Valuables (dentures, hearing aids, wigs, glasses): send them with family, or secure them per protocol."]},{"s":"Intra-op▾","a":"intra-op","k":["Intra-op▾","Circulating nurse — Not scrubbed, gowned, or gloved (but masked and capped); stays in the unsterile area, \"runs the show,\" records nursing care.","Scrub nurse — Scrubbed in, gowned and gloved; stays inside the sterile field.","RN first assist — A nursing role assisting the surgeon (at UK, often medical students assist).","CRNA — Certified registered nurse anesthetist; extensive schooling after nursing school plus ICU experience.","The four intra-op concepts: identification, safety, positioning, asepsis","At least two patient identifiers.","Site marking by the surgeon (and often the patient's initials) when there is a left and right or multiple structures (fingers, toes, spinal levels); as close to the incision as possible and visible after draping.","Time-out: the whole team stops before the incision to verify the patient, site, level, procedure, position, and implants or special equipment.","Positioning (supine, prone, lithotomy, side-lying) with secured extremities and padding: improper positioning over many hours can cause muscle strain, joint damage, pressure ulcers, and nerve damage.","A meticulously sterile field.","General — Inhaled and/or injected drugs: loss of all sensation and consciousness, with amnesia. Phases: pre-induction, induction, maintenance, emergence. Given by an anesthesiologist or CRNA.","Regional — Injected near nerves to block an area: nerve block, Bier block, spinal, epidural.","Local — Temporary loss of feeling by inhibiting nerve endings in one part of the body.","Monitored anesthesia care (MAC) — Formerly \"conscious sedation\": no inhaled agents; conscious but sedated (a colonoscopy, for example).","Intra-op nursing concerns: infection, positioning injury, and imbalanced body temperature (patients can become hypothermic in the OR).","Malignant Hyperthermia","Malignant hyperthermia: an inherited muscle disorder triggered by certain volatile anesthetics; rare, but high mortality if untreated (\"all hands on deck\"). The most common initial sign is an unexpected rise in end-tidal CO2 (hypercarbia), …","Not predictable","Familial tendency, but some patients have no family history.","Over 50% had uneventful general anesthetics before, so past uneventful surgeries don't rule it out."]},{"s":"Post-op▾","a":"post-op","k":["Post-op▾","PACU basics","All orders must be rewritten after surgery; pre-op orders don't carry over. Read every new order.","PACU handoff: patient history, intra-op management, vital signs, lab results, estimated blood loss.","Initial PACU assessment: airway, breathing, circulation, pain, level of consciousness, voiding, bowel sounds, safety needs.","Possible problems: neuro/psych, GI (delayed gastric emptying, paralytic ileus), urinary (trouble voiding, infection with a Foley), respiratory (especially with lung history or smoking), cardiovascular, integument (dehiscence, hematoma, infe…","Respiratory","Problems","Hypoxia from shallow breathing, anesthesia, obesity, respiratory depression (especially first-time narcotics), laryngeal spasm.","Atelectasis and pneumonia.","Prevention","Pulmonary toilet; head of bed elevated; vital signs and O2 sat; suction if needed.","Effective coughing with splinting for abdominal or thoracic incisions (a pillow; CABG patients get a red heart pillow). Coughing after these surgeries is mandatory.","Early mobilization.","Around-the-clock pain dosing so they can cough and move.","Cardiovascular","Decreased cardiac output and VTE","Monitor vital signs (BP is the indirect measure of cardiac output), skin temperature, and color (peripheral perfusion). Trend and compare to baseline.","DVT (most often elderly, obese, immobilized) can become a PE; together called VTE. Prevent with leg and ankle exercises (dorsiflex, plantar flex, circles: they mimic walking's muscle pump), SCDs, early ambulation, phlebitis assessment.","TED hose must fit correctly (an ill-fitting pair caused a stage 2 pressure ulcer); SCDs are used more now.","BP less than 90 or greater than 160 — Notify the provider (rule of thumb).","Hypotension with a normal pulse and warm pink skin — Keep an eye on it. (A baseline of 90/60 may be normal for that patient if asymptomatic.)","Hypotension with a fast pulse or cold, clammy skin — Possible impending hypovolemic shock: immediate intervention.","Heart rate greater than 100 (for example 110) — Assess why: pain (morphine just given?), anxiety, or fluid volume deficit? Treatment depends on the cause. Normal is 60 to 100.","Narrowing pulse pressure (120/80 = 40, now 110/80 = 30) — Can indicate hemodynamic compromise.","Gradual BP trends over several readings; any rhythm change — Noteworthy; report rhythm changes.","Neurologic & Psychological","Emergence delirium and more","A restless patient always means think hypoxia first (do they need more oxygen?); also pain, anesthetic agents, the ET tube.","Post-op delirium is more common in the elderly. Post-op depression exists (lack of sleep, lack of home support, body image, pain): assess mood and allow time to talk.","Assess LOC, orientation, memory, following commands, grips, moving extremities, pupils; always compare to baseline (was that weak left grip there before?).","Pain & Temperature","Pain","Post-op pain is continuous and predictable: around-the-clock dosing, not waiting for the patient to ask.","Pain increases as anesthesia wears off. Opioids and non-opioids first for severe pain, supplemented by non-drug measures.","Watch every patient on opioids for respiratory depression. Document outcomes.","Temperature","Often cold after surgery: warm blankets; check the temperature.","First 48 hours: a low-grade temp is usually expected, the inflammatory response to surgery; too soon for infection. Above 100.4, think lung congestion or dehydration: pulmonary toilet and fluids.","After 48 hours: an elevated temp raises concern for infection (wound, lungs); check the WBC and neutrophils.","GI","Paralytic ileus and more","Abdominal discomfort is common; mobilization often helps most.","Paralytic ileus is most likely after bowel manipulation. After colon surgery, large intestine motility may take 2 to 7 days (small intestine sooner). Signs: distended, tender belly; absent or high-pitched bowel sounds.","No bowel sounds at 24 to 48 hours isn't necessarily an ileus; peristalsis may not have kicked in yet. Watch for bowel sounds and passing gas.","Ileus treatment: NG tube to low wall suction, NPO (\"don't feed a paralytic ileus\").","Treat nausea (antiemetic); advance the diet as tolerated (NPO, clear liquids, full liquids); document intake; ambulate; hydrate; monitor bowel sounds every shift; privacy.","Opioids cause constipation, and patients never develop tolerance to it: be proactive about stool softeners (ask the provider if none is ordered).","Urinary","Voiding and output","Get the Foley out as soon as possible; watch for UTI (in older patients, the first cue is often confusion).","Urine output greater than 30 mL/hour (rule of thumb). Urine output reflects renal perfusion: falling BP and cardiac output lower it.","Assess the urge to void, palpate the bladder, use the bladder scanner, normal voiding position, running water.","No void within 6 to 8 hours after surgery: get an order to catheterize (usually in-and-out).","Integument, Tubes & Drains","Wounds","Assess drainage (amount, color, odor, consistency) and erythema; purulent drainage is not expected.","Dehiscence: the wound opens. Evisceration: the insides come out. Full care in Week 5: Complications of Wounds","At risk: large or obese, older, coughing a lot.","Dressings clean, dry, intact; meticulous asepsis; nutrition is paramount (no wound heals without it).","Tubes, drains, catheters to know","Indwelling and suprapubic catheters; ostomies; G-tube (gastrostomy) to drainage; Tenckhoff (peritoneal dialysis) catheter; rectal tube/fecal management system; NG to low wall suction.","Penrose drain; closed suction drains (Hemovac, Jackson-Pratt); a T-tube; chest tube after pleural/lung surgery.","Know what each is for and monitor its output."]},{"s":"Fluid Volume Imbalances▾","a":"fluid-volume-imbalances","k":["Fluid Volume Imbalances▾","Review from 325","Oncotic (colloid osmotic) pressure: albumin, the primary colloid, has \"pulling power\" that keeps fluid in the intravascular space. Low albumin: fluid leaks into the interstitium and can third-space.","Hydrostatic pressure: \"pushing power.\" In heart failure, backed-up fluid raises it and pushes fluid into the interstitium.","Third spacing: fluid collecting where it serves no purpose (pleural effusion, peritoneum, pericardium). Pulmonary edema = fluid in the lungs; peripheral edema = in the ankles.","Causes — Too many IV fluids (older patients with heart failure; TACO); heart failure (backup, hydrostatic pressure); kidney failure (not excreting urine); primary polydipsia (rare, psychogenic); SIADH, Cushing syndrome, steroids (fluid rete…","Neuro — Headache, confusion, lethargy; seizures and coma (more from the hyponatremia) — Restless, drowsy, lethargic, confused; seizures and coma (from the hypernatremia)","Cardiovascular — JVD (backup from the right side shows first in the jugular vein), bounding pulse, increased BP, S3 gallop (\"lub-dub-ta\"), increased CVP (not tested) — Tachycardia (compensating for low stroke volume; BP holds, then eventual…","Respiratory — Pulmonary edema: very short of breath, crackles (can be life-threatening) — Increased respiratory rate","Skin — Peripheral edema (0 to 4+ scale; in dependent areas like the ankles), pitting — Dry mucous membranes, cold clammy skin, sluggish turgor, dry skin","Urine and weight — Polyuria (only if the kidneys work); muscle spasms; water weight gain — Decreased urine output; weak, dizzy; water weight loss; thirst (blunted in the elderly)","Labs — Hemodiluted: false low BUN, sodium, hematocrit — Hemoconcentrated: false high BUN, sodium, hematocrit","Treatment — Restrict fluids (for example 1,500 mL: heart failure, CKD, liver failure); watch every IV fluid and rate — Mild: drink more. Severe: blood products (PRBCs) or balanced IV solutions (normal saline, lactated Ringer's)","Nursing management","Daily weights are the key, most reliable measure: same scale, same clothing, same time of day. I&O is less reliable (urine not saved, unreported drinks).","Serum and urine osmolality (particles per solution: how concentrated).","Trend vital signs; respiratory status (pulmonary edema brings patients in); safety with orthostatic hypotension (assist when up); skin care (dry skin or pitting edema both risk breakdown).","Delegation to a UAP (NCLEX loves these): daily weights, vital signs, I&O (abnormal numbers come back to you to interpret), frequent oral care (helps on a fluid restriction), skin care and repositioning, elevating edematous extremities (prom…"]},{"s":"Electrolytes▾","a":"electrolytes","k":["Electrolytes▾","The two ranges to commit to memory: sodium 135 to 145 and potassium 3.5 to 5. You don't need to memorize calcium, magnesium, or phosphorus numbers; a question will imply whether they are high or low.","Sodium","The major extracellular cation. \"Salt sucks, and water follows salt\": sodium governs osmolality (most of the body's particles per solution). It also activates muscle and nerve cells.","Causes — Excess sodium intake (hypertonic IV fluids; tube feeding without free water). Inadequate water intake (the elderly's lost thirst; anorexia; depression). Excess water loss (insensible loss, diarrhea, osmotic diuretics, DKA, diabetes…","Manifestations — Mental status changes (cells shrink): irritability, trouble concentrating, confusion, seizures, coma. With fluid deficit: postural hypotension, tachycardia, weakness. — Mental status changes (cells swell): confusion (unsafe…","Treatment — Treat the cause: water lost = add water back (hypotonic IV fluid); excess sodium = remove sodium. Monitor serum sodium and osmolality. — Mild: fluid restriction (concentrates the serum). Severe: 3% (hypertonic) saline, three tim…","Sodium out of balance? Think mental status changes. And correct sodium slowly (over a couple of days): fast fluid shifts in brain cells can be life-threatening. Potassium can be corrected faster than sodium.","The four usual reasons for a fluid restriction: heart failure, kidney failure (end-stage CKD), hyponatremia, and severe liver disease.","Potassium","The major intracellular cation; regulates cell excitability and electrical status. Out of range? Think cardiac dysrhythmias (EKG changes, ventricular dysrhythmias): these patients go on telemetry.","Causes — IV fluids with KCl plus renal insufficiency; shifts out of the cell (acidosis, tissue breakdown, intense exercise); failure to eliminate (kidney disease with oliguria: the main reason seen in the hospital); adrenal insufficiency (A…","Manifestations — EKG changes and dysrhythmias; fatigue, irritability, muscle weakness, loss of tone, paresthesia, confusion. — EKG changes and dysrhythmias; muscle weakness, cramps, paresthesia. Hypokalemia precipitates digoxin toxicity (di…","Treatment — Have the provider stop potassium-containing IV fluids. Increase excretion: loop or thiazide diuretics (if the kidneys work), dialysis (anuric CKD), Veltassa or Kayexalate (take hours to days). Dangerously high (6.5 or 7): act no…","Potassium safety points","A patient on a loop or thiazide diuretic should usually have a potassium supplement ordered; if not, check that morning's potassium and ask. Never give a supplement without knowing the potassium.","IV fluids with potassium are labeled in red at UK (for example normal saline with 20 or 40 mEq KCl). Never bolus a potassium-containing bag: get plain 0.9% normal saline for the bolus.","Know high-potassium foods (select-all-that-apply favorites) to teach patients on potassium-losing diuretics, or to avoid when potassium is high.","Calcium","Mostly in bone (strength); needed for membrane potential and nerve excitability. Only 1% is in the cells: half protein-bound, half ionized (available).","Causes — Hyperparathyroidism (number one; the four parathyroids regulate serum calcium); less commonly cancers, including paraneoplastic secretion (for example, lung cancer). — Renal failure (number one); parathyroid deficiency or removal; …","Manifestations — \"Excess calcium is like a sedative\": reduced muscle and nerve excitability, confusion, psychosis, seizures, coma. — Tetany (excess nerve excitability, sustained muscle contraction); Chvostek's sign (tap at the mandible/chee…","Treatment — Mild: conservative (decrease calcium intake, weight-bearing, hydration). Severe: a bisphosphonate (pamidronate, the gold standard if from cancer), IV isotonic saline, calcitonin (short-term; increases renal calcium excretion). —…","Magnesium","Stabilizes cardiac and smooth muscle cells.","Causes — Renal failure (most common): a CKD patient taking Maalox for an upset stomach; too much IV magnesium sulfate (OB). — Malnutrition plus GI losses (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, wound drainage); chronic alcohol abuse.","Manifestations — Everything slowed down: lethargy, drowsiness, muscle weakness, decreased DTRs. — Everything revved up (like hypocalcemia): cramps, tremors, possible seizures, vertigo, hyperactive DTRs, Chvostek's and Trousseau's, increased…","Treatment — Avoid magnesium-containing drugs (Maalox, Mylanta); limit dietary magnesium (green vegetables, nuts, bananas, oranges, peanut butter, chocolate); force fluids if the kidneys work, dialysis if not; IV calcium gluconate if symptom…","Phosphorus","Calcium and phosphorus are always inversely related: calcium up, phosphorus down; phosphorus up, calcium down.","Causes — Kidney failure (number one; it also causes low calcium); phosphorus-containing laxatives; an underactive parathyroid. — Malnutrition (chronic alcohol abuse); lower GI losses.","Manifestations — Usually asymptomatic by itself: the symptoms (muscle spasms, paresthesia, tetany, Chvostek's, Trousseau's) come from the accompanying hypocalcemia. — CNS depression and muscle weakness, including respiratory muscles (life-t…","Treatment — Calcium-based phosphate binders (calcium carbonate); dialysis if severe. — Replace (IV or oral, over hours); watch calcium alongside phosphorus (often CKD patients)."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"medsurg1-week4-renal-urinary.html","t":"Renal & Urinary: UTI, Stones, AKI, CKD & Dialysis","secs":[{"s":"Renal & Urinary: UTI, Stones, AKI, CKD & Dialysis","a":"","k":["Med Surg I Review · Week 4","Urinary Labs","Urinary Tract Infection","Pyelonephritis","Kidney Stones (Nephrolithiasis)","Urinary Diversions","Acute Kidney Injury (AKI)","Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD)","Dialysis","Self-Test Flashcards","Med Surg I (NUR 334) review content. Built from the nine NUR 334 Week 4 voiceovers: urinary labs and diagnostics (two parts), UTI, pyelonephritis, nephrolithiasis, urinary diversion, acute kidney injury, CKD and conservative treatment, and …"]},{"s":"Urinary Labs▾","a":"urinary-labs","k":["Urinary Labs▾","Commit to memory: creatinine 0.5 to 1.2 (the most reliable indicator of kidney function) and BUN 10 to 20.","Serum creatinine — 0.5 to 1.2 — From muscle breakdown and protein metabolism; released at a consistent rate; specific to the kidneys. Low only matters in muscle atrophy or malnutrition.","BUN (blood urea nitrogen) — 10 to 20 — Not specific to the kidneys. Also raised by high-protein or high-nitrogen tube feeds or diets, a GI bleed (digested blood is protein-rich), and dehydration (false high); lowered by overhydration (false…","Potassium — 3.5 to 5 — Kidneys are the main excretory organ: kidney disease = hyperkalemia. One of the first electrolytes to become abnormal; watched almost daily.","Total calcium — 9 to 10.5 — CKD decreases calcium reabsorption: low calcium, renal osteodystrophy (bone porosity, fracture risk).","Phosphorus — 3 to 4.5 — Inversely related to calcium; typically high in kidney disease.","Magnesium — 1.3 to 2.1 — Excreted by the kidneys; elevated in CKD.","Elevated BUN? Check the creatinine next. High creatinine too: probably the kidneys. Normal creatinine: look for another cause, such as a GI bleed (is the H&H dropping?).","Urinalysis & Urine Color","The UA","First morning urine (most concentrated); analyze within the hour.","Should have no bilirubin, glucose, ketones, protein, RBCs, or WBCs. Glucose: think diabetes (glucosuria, DKA). WBCs: suspect UTI. RBCs: hematuria.","Odor \"aromatic\"; color amber-yellow; pH 4.6 to 6.","Specific gravity 1.010 to 1.030 (tap water is 1.000). Well hydrated = low; dehydrated = high (1.035 means the patient needs to drink). Lewis's range differs slightly; exam questions will fall outside both ranges.","Color and clarity","Clear, slightly cloudy, cloudy, turbid (very cloudy, as in a UTI; it showed up on the HESI).","Light yellow (big water drinker) to amber or dark amber (not drinking).","Red: hematuria, or certain medications.","Brown, \"Coca-Cola\" urine: obstructive jaundice (bilirubin excreted in urine instead of the GI tract).","Urinary Diagnostics","Creatinine clearance (24-hour urine) — Approximates the GFR (blood filtered past the glomerulus per minute). A dropping clearance raises concern for CKD. — Discard the first void (start time), save all urine for 24 hours, keep it cold (on i…","Estimated GFR — A calculation from serum creatinine plus age, sex, weight, ethnicity; recommended by the National Kidney Foundation over 24-hour urines. — The same labs can put two people (for example, a 76-year-old woman vs a 28-year-old m…","Bladder scan — Non-invasive bedside measure of urine in the bladder. — Water-soluble gel.","Clean-catch urine — Confirms a suspected UTI and identifies the organism. — Sterile cup; clean the meatus (the urethra isn't sterile, the bladder is); void a second or two into the toilet, then catch midstream.","Cystoscopy — Scoping the bladder wall; lithotomy position. — Consent. Burning, pink-tinged urine, and frequency afterward are expected; bright red blood is not.","KUB (kidneys, ureters, bladder) — An X-ray of the lower abdomen and pelvis (like a chest X-ray for the lungs). — Bowel prep possible.","IVP (intravenous pyelogram) — IV dye outlines the renal pelvis: stones, tumors, cysts, blockages. — Bowel prep possible; check iodine/contrast allergy (anaphylaxis); warn about the flushed feeling (expected); force fluids after (nephrotoxic…","Retrograde pyelogram — Through a cystoscope, backward up the urinary system. — Used if the IVP doesn't visualize well, for contrast allergy, or elevated creatinine: the dye doesn't go systemic. Prep like an IVP; complications like a cystosc…","Renal biopsy — Tissue sample (for example, suspected kidney cancer). — Consent; check PT/INR, coags, platelets (very vascular); no aspirin or warfarin. Side-lying flexed position. After: pressure dressing, lie on the affected side, bed rest…","Non-contrast spiral CT (CT KUB) — The gold standard for renal colic (stone pain). — Quick, non-invasive; used more than IVP or retrograde."]},{"s":"Urinary Tract Infection▾","a":"urinary-tract-infection","k":["Urinary Tract Infection▾","Terms","Upper UTI: the kidney parenchyma, pelvis, ureters; more serious, systemic (fever, chills, flank pain / CVA tenderness).","Lower UTI: the bladder (cystitis); no systemic signs.","Pyelonephritis: inflammation, usually infection, of the renal parenchyma and collecting system.","Urosepsis: the infection in the blood; can be life-threatening.","CAUTI (catheter-associated UTI): almost a third of healthcare-associated infections. \"Catheters are bad\": if not truly needed, get them out; the nurse is often the one to ask.","Risk factors","Urinary stasis (neurogenic bladder, an obstructing stone, urethral stricture, tumors).","Females (shorter urethra); pregnancy.","Compromised immunity (aging, diabetes); severe constipation (impedes outflow).","Poor hygiene; habitually delaying urination (\"nurse's bladder\").","Lower UTI symptoms","Emptying: hesitancy, intermittency, post-void dribbling, retention, incomplete emptying, dysuria.","Storage: frequency, urgency, incontinence, nocturia, nocturnal enuresis.","The most common bacterial infection in women; usually gram-negative E. coli ascending the urethra.","Older adults often present with confusion, sometimes as the first sign.","Diagnosis","Bacteria and pyuria (WBCs) in the urine. A point-of-care dipstick: positive nitrites and leukocyte esterase (an enzyme in WBCs). Confirm with a urinalysis.","Clean catch or Foley sample for complicated, hospital-acquired, frequent, or non-responding UTIs.","Prevention teaching","Empty the bladder regularly and completely; don't rush.","Wipe front to back; drink lots of fluid (water is best).","Cranberry: no definitive evidence it improves outcomes (fine if it means they drink more).","Sexually active women: urinate before and especially after intercourse.","Avoid bladder irritants.","Hospital-acquired UTI prevention and care","Remove catheters ASAP or avoid them; aseptic technique.","Catheter in the vagina by mistake: leave it there, get a new sterile kit, and catheterize the urethra.","Hand washing, gloves, nail care; clean up incontinence quickly; offer regular toileting.","Fluids unless contraindicated (heart failure, kidney failure, advanced liver disease).","Comfort: warm bath or shower (no bubble baths), local heat.","Take the full antibiotic course; should feel better within a couple of days, otherwise follow up (culture and sensitivity)."]},{"s":"Pyelonephritis▾","a":"pyelonephritis","k":["Pyelonephritis▾","Presentation and diagnosis","An upper UTI involving the parenchyma; more systemic than cystitis.","From mild fatigue to a sudden onset of chills, fever, vomiting, and CVA tenderness (flank pain).","UTI diagnostics plus a CBC with diff (shift to the left: neutrophils and bands), imaging, blood cultures if it may be in the blood.","Treatment","Mild: outpatient; fluids; NSAIDs or antipyretics (acetaminophen); broad-spectrum antibiotic, then narrowed to the culture. Cultures repeated after 14 to 21 days; may be on antibiotics for weeks.","Sicker: hospitalized with IV hydration and IV antibiotics, switched to oral once nausea, vomiting, and dehydration improve.","CVA tenderness","Costovertebral angle (not cerebrovascular accident): just below the last rib on each side of the back, where the kidneys sit (kidneys aren't palpable).","The provider lays one hand there and strikes it with the other fist. With pyelonephritis it is exceedingly tender; the patient will jump. An unexpected finding that points to an upper UTI."]},{"s":"Kidney Stones (Nephrolithiasis)▾","a":"kidney-stones-nephrolithiasis","k":["Kidney Stones (Nephrolithiasis)▾","Diagnosis and types","History and physical; non-contrast spiral CT (gold standard), ultrasound, KUB, or IVP.","Urinalysis for blood, crystals, pH; analyze any stone retrieved.","Five types: calcium oxalate (most common), calcium phosphate, uric acid, cystine, struvite.","Pain","One of the strongest pain sensations known: severe visceral pain, \"renal colic\" (waxes and wanes, spasms); nausea and vomiting that don't improve after vomiting.","It can cause hemodynamic compromise, so treating the pain may be the priority (unlike typical post-op pain).","Opioids are first-line (sometimes a PCA), plus non-drug measures. Some patients are admitted just for uncontrolled pain.","Treatment and teaching","Small stones: pass spontaneously at home, straining all urine to catch the stone. Larger stones: a stent, or removal. Antibiotics if infected; watch for obstruction.","Lithotripsy (outpatient): shock waves pulverize the stone. Internal (cystoscope, percutaneous, laser) or external (non-invasive). Afterward hematuria is expected; a stent placed to pass fragments comes out in 1 to 2 weeks; decreased urine o…","Daily hydration (water). Forcing fluids once a stone is present hasn't been shown to push it out.","Diet by stone type: calcium oxalate = low-oxalate diet (dark roughage, spinach, cocoa, nuts), not a low-calcium diet; uric acid = low-purine diet (gout patients can form uric acid stones).","Immobile or sedentary patients are at risk: mobilize, turn, fluids; sit (women) or stand (men) to void regularly for full emptying.","Nephrostomy tube","Surgically placed directly into the renal pelvis through the flank when a ureter is totally obstructed; drains to an external bag, bypassing the ureter, bladder, and urethra. Not long-term.","Pain or drainage around the site: suspect a blockage. Irrigation requires an order; meticulous aseptic technique (high infection risk); only a tiny amount of sterile saline (it's the renal pelvis, not the bladder)."]},{"s":"Urinary Diversions▾","a":"urinary-diversions","k":["Urinary Diversions▾","Usually after a cystectomy (bladder removal, often for bladder cancer): the kidneys and ureters work, but there is no reservoir.","Ileal conduit (incontinent) — Ureters are anastomosed to a snippet of ileum brought to the skin as a stoma; urine flows out all the time. — The former gold standard; still chosen with significant comorbidities or shorter life expectancy. Pe…","Continent cutaneous reservoir (Kock, Indiana, Miami pouch) — An internal pouch with a stoma and sphincter; the patient self-catheterizes. — No bag. Catheterize every few hours at first, extending to every 4 to 6 hours. Largely replaced by t…","Orthotopic neobladder (continent, to the urethra) — A reservoir made from intestine (usually ileum), connected to the native urethra. — The newest and preferred: most like normal voiding. No urge to void (no nerve supply): relax the sphinct…","Pre-op: fears, body image, teaching; a wound ostomy continence nurse. Post-op: NPO with an NG to suction for a couple of days to rest the gut; skin integrity risk."]},{"s":"Acute Kidney Injury (AKI)▾","a":"acute-kidney-injury-aki","k":["Acute Kidney Injury (AKI)▾","What it is","A sudden, rapidly progressive decline in kidney function (hours to days), with or without less urine. A syndrome, from slight deterioration to severe impairment.","Decreased GFR (less than 90), decreased urine output, BUN above 20, creatinine elevated.","Potentially reversible (CKD is not), but high mortality, especially in the ICU; usually in patients with other comorbidities.","Often follows prolonged hypotension, hypovolemia, or a nephrotoxic agent.","Urine output thresholds","Less than 30 mL/hour (the instructor's preferred \"magic number\"), or less than 400 mL in 24 hours: loose definitions of oliguria.","Example: 50 mL in 2 hours = notify the provider (after ruling out something you can fix, like a kinked Foley).","Waiting 24 hours may let the AKI worsen.","Prerenal (the most common) — Decreased perfusion to the kidneys: hypotension, hypovolemia. May develop within a day or two.","Intrarenal — Inside the kidney; most commonly acute tubular necrosis (mentioned only here).","Postrenal — Obstruction after the kidneys, for example a swollen prostate backing up urine.","The oliguric phase (the focus in this course)","Phases: oliguric, diuretic, recovery. An AKI that doesn't recover can become irreversible CKD.","Oliguric: less than 400 mL/24 hours; begins about a day after a hypotensive event; lasts 1 to 3 weeks.","Fluid retention (fluid volume excess), dilutional hyponatremia (confusion), hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis, rising BUN (waste accumulation).","Treatment: find and stop the cause; correct fluid and electrolytes (especially hyperkalemia); diuretics and fluid restriction; daily weights; manage BP; prevent infection; nutrition; avoid nephrotoxic drugs.","Lowering Potassium","Low-potassium diet — Prevents recurrence; doesn't help much with an acute elevation.","GI potassium binders (cation-exchange resins) — Kayexalate (sodium polystyrene sulfonate) PO or enema; newer Veltassa (patiromer) and Lokelma (sodium zirconium cyclosilicate). Remove potassium through the GI tract, but not fast (not for an …","IV calcium gluconate — Raises the threshold for excitation to protect against dysrhythmias; doesn't lower the potassium. Temporary.","Insulin and dextrose — Moves potassium back into the cells. Temporary.","Sodium bicarbonate — Corrects acidosis and shifts potassium into the cells. Temporary.","Dialysis — The most effective way to remove potassium; with the binders, the only treatments that actually remove it from the body."]},{"s":"Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD)▾","a":"chronic-kidney-disease-ckd","k":["Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD)▾","1 — 90 or more (normal) — Asymptomatic; slow progression. Catching it here would be ideal.","2 — Starting to drop — Asymptomatic except maybe hypertension; most don't know they have CKD.","3a and 3b — Moderate drop (3b worse) — Hypertension, otherwise often asymptomatic; evaluate and treat complications. The critical point to catch patients.","4 — 15 to 29 — Manifestations appear; diagnosis usually happens here (the kidneys have huge reserve). Just short of dialysis or transplant.","5 — Less than 15 — Full kidney failure; uremic (waste in the blood); likely starting dialysis.","End-stage manifestations","Fluid and electrolytes lost: edema, hyperkalemia, high phosphorus, high magnesium, metabolic acidosis.","Not hungry, malnourished; itch \"like crazy\"; CNS changes (confusion).","No erythropoietin: severe anemia. Decreased vitamin D activation: hypocalcemia and renal osteodystrophy.","Immunosuppressed (toxic accumulation, immune cell dysfunction, chronic inflammation, dialysis stress); elevated lipids (often on statins).","The two biggest causes: high blood pressure and diabetes. Prevention = early detection and controlling both.","Nursing problems and care","Excess fluid volume: monitor, daily weights (more reliable than I&O), fluid restriction.","Malnutrition: nausea and vomiting, weight trends, serum protein, H&H; desirable allowed foods.","Risk for injury (brittle bones): calcium and vitamin D supplements; watch electrolytes.","Grieving and lifestyle change: community support, family.","Infection risk: aseptic line care (dialysis access, peritoneal catheter).","Activity intolerance (anemia): erythropoietin, monitoring the H&H.","Conservative therapy (not dialysis)","Control BP and glucose.","Contrast dye: avoid it, or protect the kidneys with acetylcysteine beforehand (also the acetaminophen-overdose antidote) and forced fluids.","Avoid nephrotoxic drugs: NSAIDs and aminoglycosides. (Acetaminophen is hard on the liver.)","Treat fluid overload (less PO intake, diuretics if the kidneys work), hyperkalemia (anticipate orders near 6), acidosis, mineral and bone disorders, hypertension, anemia, dyslipidemia, malnutrition.","Diet and fluids","Protein restriction before dialysis (BUN and creatinine are protein end products), even though the patient is malnourished: a conundrum. Once on dialysis, protein can increase and restrictions loosen. (You won't be asked to choose between t…","Typically low potassium, sodium, magnesium, and phosphorus, plus a fluid restriction (sometimes today's allowance = yesterday's urine output). Renal shakes and tube feeds (\"nephroshake,\" Novasource Renal): high calorie, high protein, low po…","Teaching: thirst alternatives (mouth swabs); medication rationales and side effects; signs of electrolyte imbalance (muscle weakness with hyperkalemia); weigh daily and report a gain of more than 4 pounds in 2 days (fluid), rising BP, short…"]},{"s":"Dialysis▾","a":"dialysis","k":["Dialysis▾","How it works","For stage 5 when conservative measures aren't enough (or a kidney transplant). About 9 in 10 dialysis patients get hemodialysis; about 1 in 10 get peritoneal dialysis.","Osmosis: water moves from lower to higher particle concentration. Diffusion: particles move from higher to lower concentration. Both aim to equalize across a semipermeable membrane: outside the body in hemodialysis; the peritoneum in PD.","The nephrologist sets the dialysate concentrations from the labs: to pull potassium off, the dialysate has less potassium than the blood.","Schedule — About 4 to 5 hours (also given as 4 to 6), 3 days a week (Mon/Wed/Fri or Tue/Thu/Sat), at a clinic — CAPD: 4 to 5 (also given as 4 to 6) manual exchanges every day; automated PD: a cycler at night (cycles of about an hour each)","Access — AV fistula (preferred), AV graft, or a temporary external catheter — Tenckhoff catheter placed surgically into the peritoneum","Advantages — Fast: can pull off serious potassium and fluid in one session — At home, no fancy machines (CAPD), more mobility, fewer diet restrictions, more like continuous filtering, easier on the heart","Disadvantages — Needs vascular access; may need heparin; stricter diet and fluid limits (Sunday night before a Monday session can feel miserable) — Peritonitis; requires daily commitment; body image","Unique complication — Disequilibrium syndrome (often the first few sessions): disorientation, headache, agitation, nausea, vomiting, even seizures, from rapid fluid shifts — Peritonitis is the number one complication (cloudy dialysate); als…","Hemodialysis Access","AV fistula","A surgeon anastomoses an artery to a vein, most often in the forearm; permanent.","Needs about 6 weeks to mature before use (a temporary access is used meanwhile). If it survives the first 6 months, it can last up to 20 years.","Assess every shift: palpate a thrill (a vibration like a cat purring) and auscultate a bruit (\"whoosh\"); both are expected and confirm patency. A lost bruit = notify the provider.","Sign over the bed: no BPs or IV sticks in that arm.","Check distal perfusion (cap refill, numbness, tingling, radial and ulnar pulses, motor function, pain), especially right after surgery.","AV graft and temporary catheters","AV graft: synthetic loop under the skin; usable sooner; but a foreign body, so infection risk. For small, weak veins or when access is needed within 6 weeks.","Tunneled, cuffed IJ catheter (provider-placed; about 1 to 3 weeks), or a femoral catheter (bed rest, minimal leg movement; removed ASAP).","A temporary dialysis catheter (the transcript calls it a \"u-doll\" catheter; often for AKI in the ICU; about 2 weeks): looks like a central line but is not; never run medications or fluids through it.","Peritoneal Dialysis","1. Fill (inflow) — About 2 liters of dialysate into the peritoneal space; about 10 minutes.","2. Dwell — Prescribed time when diffusion and osmosis happen across the peritoneum; the patient can walk around.","3. Drain — The bag on the floor on a clean towel; drains by gravity in about 15 to 30 minutes.","PD nursing points","Tenckhoff site heals in a week or two; then no dressing, shower and pat dry; daily catheter care; teach infection signs.","Aseptic technique every exchange (4 or 5 times a day, so contamination risk is high). Repeated peritonitis can force a switch to HD.","Not all dialysate draining back? Turn the patient side to side. Cloudy dialysate may mean peritonitis.","Dialysis nursing and drugs","Trend vital signs, I&O, daily weights, labs; daily catheter and fistula care; know the complications.","Drugs not excreted by the kidneys can build to toxic levels; drugs that cross the membrane are dialyzed out (subtherapeutic after). Give dialyzable drugs after dialysis; doses may be adjusted (\"renal dosing\" by pharmacy)."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"medsurg1-week5-integumentary-hiv-burns.html","t":"HIV, Skin Disorders, Surgical Wounds & Burns","secs":[{"s":"HIV, Skin Disorders, Surgical Wounds & Burns","a":"","k":["Med Surg I Review · Week 5","HIV Labs & Diagnostics","HIV & AIDS: Nursing Care","Skin Lesions & Color","Skin Cancer","Skin Infections, Reactions & Benign Disorders","Surgical Wound Management","Complications of Wounds","Burns","Self-Test Flashcards","Med Surg I (NUR 334) review content. Built from the seven NUR 334 Week 5 voiceovers: HIV labs and diagnostics, HIV, skin cancer, skin infections and benign disorders, surgical wound management, complications of wounds, and burns."]},{"s":"HIV Labs & Diagnostics▾","a":"hiv-labs-diagnostics","k":["HIV Labs & Diagnostics▾","Screening","Routine testing for adults and pregnant women: at least once for ages 13 to 75 without risk factors.","High risk: test yearly. Men who have sex with men, IV drug users, people who exchange sex for money or drugs, sex partners of people with HIV, and people whose partner's HIV status is unknown.","Consent is voluntary. Opt out: a patient can decline, and we document them as opted out.","Confidentiality; counseling and referral to care if positive.","The test to remember","Fourth generation (HIV antibody AND antigen) is the preferred test. The antigen comes before antibodies, so it can turn positive within 2 to 3 weeks (older antibody-only tests: up to a month or longer).","Identifies early infection in up to 80% of patients; can be a rapid test.","ELISA tests antibodies only; Western blot is sometimes the second test.","HIV RNA (viral load): qualitative (positive or negative) and quantitative (progression, response to treatment).","Reading the Results","Positive — Positive test followed by a second positive. A positive always gets a confirmatory test.","Negative — Tests negative. No second test needed.","Indeterminate — Positive at first, then negative on the confirmatory test.","Window period: the time between exposure and when the test gives an accurate positive. During it the patient is still infectious but tests negative. Most people develop antibodies around week 3 to 4 after exposure.","Eclipse period: from exposure to when the viral load peaks; the patient is infectious at the end of it.","Monitoring Progression","CD4 count — Normal 500 to 1400; want it more than 500. Less than 200 = AIDS. — How healthy the immune system is; progression; risk for opportunistic infections; when to increase prophylactic treatment.","Viral load (HIV RNA) — Undetectable — Best indicator of how active the virus is; viral burden; response to drugs. Untreated HIV produces billions of copies a day.","Both checked every 3 to 4 months (stable untreated patients or patients on antiretroviral therapy).","Undetectable does not mean absent; it just can't be measured. Suppressing HIV to undetectable is the goal of medication therapy.","Also monitor WBC, platelets and H&H (all blood cells can be affected), liver function tests (hepatitis is a common comorbidity), and resistance to medications.","Not tested (instructor said you don't need to memorize): the \"out of 100 people with HIV\" statistics, the UpToDate testing algorithm slide, and the antibody-timing variation slide."]},{"s":"HIV & AIDS: Nursing Care▾","a":"hiv-aids-nursing-care","k":["HIV & AIDS: Nursing Care▾","AIDS = either an opportunistic infection develops or the CD4 drops below 200 (usually around the same time). Opportunistic infections are ones normal people don't get; they appear with immunosuppression.","1. Early / acute infection — Rapid replication; not detected on lab tests; no symptoms, but infectious. The patient then seroconverts: antibodies detectable, \"the worst flu ever,\" highly infectious, and most people don't think to get tested…","2. Latency — Body fights effectively; asymptomatic or mild symptoms; virus still producing; steady drop in CD4.","3. AIDS — Viral load increases, CD4 low; opportunistic diseases around a CD4 of 200.","Lecture conflict, kept as stated: the HIV labs voiceover gives the latency period as 10 to 20 years; the HIV voiceover gives 3 to 12 years.","Assessment","AIDS dementia complex: cognitive changes with advanced HIV.","Oral manifestations are common as the CD4 drops: do an oral assessment.","Infections can be fungal, viral, bacterial or cancerous.","Transmission: sexual, parenteral, mother to baby.","Collaborative goals","Monitor progression and immune function.","Start and monitor antiretroviral therapy (ART).","Prevent, detect and treat opportunistic infections.","Manage symptoms and ART complications; prevent transmission.","Be comfortable with sensitive subjects: often the nurse is the one who brings them up.","Occupational Exposure & PEP","Post-exposure prophylaxis: if drug therapy is indicated, start it within 1 to 2 hours, and definitely within 3 days. Follow-up HIV testing at 6 and 12 weeks, then 6 months.","Blood is the highest risk at work; other body fluids can carry HIV. Universal precautions.","Needlestick from a person with HIV: a very small risk (heard as \"02 percent\" in the transcript; the exact figure is garbled).","Mucous membranes and non-intact skin also count as exposure. The regimen depends on how severe the exposure was and the source's status; it may be no drug therapy or combination therapy.","ART Adherence & Teaching","Promoting adherence","Non-adherence is a real danger: a patient who doesn't feel sick is less likely to take a daily regimen.","Patient is motivated before getting the prescription.","Social support is a primary priority for a positive outcome.","Negotiate the plan (autonomy); a simple plan; anticipate side effects; establish trust.","Teaching the patient with AIDS","They are immunosuppressed: avoid crowds and places with poor sanitation, and foods that can carry bacteria.","Avoid cleaning pet litter boxes; keep the home clean; no sick visitors.","Continue ART; CD4 and viral load every 3 to 4 months.","Express empathy; be judgment free.","PrEP (pre-exposure prophylaxis)","Candidates: anal or vaginal sex in the last 6 months and a partner with HIV (especially an unknown or detectable viral load), inconsistent condom use, or an STI diagnosed in the last 6 months; people who inject drugs with an injection partn…","Daily medication. Used as prescribed it prevents HIV from sex by 99%, and 74% for IV drug users.","Does not protect against other STIs: still use condoms. Insurance and Medicaid cover it."]},{"s":"Skin Lesions & Color▾","a":"skin-lesions-color","k":["Skin Lesions & Color▾","Macule — Flat, not elevated — Freckle, petechiae, measles, flat mole, vitiligo","Papule — Raised — Wart, elevated mole, lipoma, basal cell carcinoma","Plaque — — — Psoriasis","Pustule — Contains purulent drainage (pus) — Impetigo, acne","Vesicle — Contains yellow plasma-like fluid — Herpes zoster (shingles), second-degree burn blister","Wheal — Edematous, irregular shape, lasts only a few hours — Bug bite, rash; angioedema (face and lip swelling)","Atrophy — Secondary lesion — Aged skin, striae (stretch marks)","Excoriation — Top layer of epidermis missing; like an abrasion — Excoriation over a wound","Fissure — Small crack in the epidermis — Athlete's foot, eczema, chapping","Scaling — Dead epidermal skin that sheds — Drug reaction, peeling sunburn","Scar — Connective tissue replacing normal skin — —","Ulcer — Loss of epidermis and dermis; a crater — Pressure ulcer, canker","Petechiae look like freckles but are small hemorrhages (for example, thin blood from thrombocytopenia); a larger bruised area is ecchymosis.","Cyanosis — Hypoxia; a late sign of decreased oxygen. Heart and lung problems, or being very cold.","Pallor — Decreased hemoglobin in the area. Check the conjunctiva for the line of demarcation (anemia).","Loss of pigment — Vitiligo, an autoimmune disease; an assessment finding.","Yellow-orange — Jaundice: too much bilirubin (liver failure). Seen most in the eyes (sclera), mucous membranes and skin.","Erythema — Red: increased oxyhemoglobin from dilation or increased blood flow; infection (\"the incision was erythemic\").","Tan-brown — Increased melanin: suntan, pregnancy."]},{"s":"Skin Cancer▾","a":"skin-cancer","k":["Skin Cancer▾","The most diagnosed cancer, partly because skin is visible (early detection). Classified as non-melanoma or melanoma.","Risk factors: fair skin, blonde or red hair, blue eyes; sun history; living near the equator or at high altitude; family or personal history; outdoor occupation; indoor tanning.","Basal cell carcinoma — Locally invasive, from basal cells. Erythematous, pearly, defined borders, barely elevated plaques, maybe a depression in the middle.","Squamous cell carcinoma — From keratinized epidermal cells; can be aggressive; scaling.","Malignant melanoma — From melanocytes; genetic and environmental factors (runs in families). Treatment depends on depth; depth correlates with survival. Poor prognosis unless diagnosed and treated early; can metastasize to any organ.","ABCDE of melanoma: Asymmetry · Border irregular (ragged, notched, blurred) · Color uneven · Diameter changing (usually increasing; most are larger than a pea) · Evolving (a mole that changed over weeks or months: notify the provider ASAP).","Biopsy confirms melanoma. Nursing: skin integrity care, anxiety and coping, biopsy site care, and annual dermatology checkups."]},{"s":"Skin Infections, Reactions & Benign Disorders▾","a":"skin-infections-reactions-benign-disorders","k":["Skin Infections, Reactions & Benign Disorders▾","Bacterial: impetigo and cellulitis","Risks: excess moisture, obesity, dermatitis, systemic steroid or antibiotic use, chronic disease such as diabetes. Usually staph and strep.","If exudate (drainage) is present, the drainage is infectious. Good hygiene and infection control.","Cellulitis: inflammation of subcutaneous tissue; hot, tender, red, diffuse borders; chills, malaise, fever.","Treat with moist heat, immobilize, elevate; IV antibiotics if admitted. Untreated: gangrene, possible limb loss.","Viral: harder to treat","Herpes simplex, shingles, HPV, warts.","Shingles (herpes zoster): reactivated varicella zoster; vesicles; burning pain and neuralgia along a dermatome; incidence rises with age; contagious to people who haven't had the virus.","Shingles vaccine: advised for anyone older than 60, one time.","HPV: warts anywhere; usually preventable with a vaccine.","Fungal: easy to diagnose","Candidiasis: mouth, vagina, skin.","Tinea is named by location: corporis = ringworm, cruris = jock itch, pedis = athlete's foot.","Skin, hair and nails most susceptible; antifungal cream or solution.","Infestations and bites","Pediculosis (lice), bedbugs, ticks, scabies. Allergy to the venom usually causes the problem.","Prevent: avoid them, repellents, meticulous hygiene (hair, skin, clothing, bed, sexual partners), routine skin checks; tick check after the woods.","Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN): rare but life-threatening. Usually a severe adverse reaction to a medication (or to infection). Acute destruction of skin epithelium and mucous membranes (peeling lips); fever, …","Contact dermatitis is the benign end of the dermatitis spectrum (example: a reaction to rubber boots).","Psoriasis","Chronic autoimmune disease; usually develops at 15 to 35; runs in families.","Plaque psoriasis is most common: distinct red, scaly lesions on knees, elbows, hands, lower back; painful and itchy.","4 out of 10 develop psoriatic arthritis. Not life-threatening; body image disturbance.","Care for Skin Disorders","Interprofessional therapy","Phototherapy; radiation for squamous or basal cell; laser.","Antibiotics if bacterial. Steroids decrease inflammation but use judiciously (side effects, including skin atrophy).","Antihistamines for urticaria or pruritus; immunomodulators suppress an overactive immune system.","Punch biopsy or scraping (for example, malignant melanoma).","Nursing tips","Compresses: room-temperature tap water; assess for maceration (white, puffy epidermis from a wet dressing).","Tepid baths: too hot a bath adds to itching.","Apply lotion while skin is still damp (seals in moisture).","Pruritus: cool environment, hydration, antipruritic creams, systemic antihistamines (diphenhydramine), avoid scratching."]},{"s":"Surgical Wound Management▾","a":"surgical-wound-management","k":["Surgical Wound Management▾","What slows healing","Age and loss of skin turgor; fragile skin, worst with long-term systemic steroids.","Decreased circulation and oxygenation (peripheral vascular disease).","Slower regeneration, less nutrient absorption and collagen, impaired immunity, dehydration.","Leukopenia; existing infection; chemo, anti-inflammatories, steroids.","Low hemoglobin, obesity, smoking, chronic disease, malnutrition.","Assessment","Three parts of wound management: assess, clean, protect.","Color: red/pink = healthy regeneration; yellow = maybe purulent; black = eschar (hinders healing).","Length x width x depth (cm); sinus tracts or tunnels; redness or swelling.","Primary intention: edges well approximated (butted together; staples, sutures, adhesive). Pulling apart = not approximated.","Drains and tubing; pain at the site.","Measuring & Charting","First measurement head to toe, second side to side, third depth.","Tunneling: a tunnel under the skin, probed with a cotton-tipped applicator. Undermining: slips under the lip around the wound.","Charted by the clock, 12 o'clock at the patient's head. Example: \"full thickness, red wound bed, 7 x 5 x 3 cm, 3 cm tunneling at 7 o'clock, 2 cm undermining at 3 to 5 o'clock.\"","Drainage","Note amount (scant, moderate, large, copious; if weighed, 1 gram = 1 mL), odor, consistency, color, and the skin around the wound and drain.","Serous — Watery, clear, slight yellow tint (inside of a blister) — A clean wound","Sanguineous — Red; serum plus RBCs — Bright = active bleeding; darker = older bleeding","Serosanguineous — Watery, pale pink (serum and blood) — Often a new wound; the most common term","Purulent — Thick; yellow, tan, green or brown; WBCs, debris, bacteria — Infection","Nursing Interventions","Nutrition is the priority. Without adequate nutrition a wound will not heal. Choosing between impaired wound and imbalanced nutrition in a malnourished patient, imbalanced nutrition comes first: all the asepsis in the world won't heal it. H…","Adequate hydration. Suture and staple removal on the provider's order.","Premedicate for dressing changes so the analgesic is peaking during the change.","Antimicrobials as ordered; document descriptively (paint a picture so the wound can be trended).","Wound care nurse specialists are your best friends.","Dressings","Gauze (4 x 4) / wet to dry — Wet gauze placed in the wound and pulled off once dry: mechanical debridement of the top layer until granulation tissue forms.","Transparent — Self-adhesive (Tegaderm).","Hydrocolloid — Occlusive; swells in the presence of exudate (DuoDERM).","Hydrogel — Mostly water; gels on contact with exudate, promoting autolytic debridement; rehydrates and fills dead space; may need a secondary occlusive dressing. For infected deep wounds or necrotic tissue, not heavily draining ones. Moist …","Alginate — Non-adherent; conforms to the wound shape and absorbs exudate.","Collagen — Powders, pastes, granules, gels.","Wound vac — Vacuum-assisted closure: foam in the wound bed, occlusive dressing, negative pressure. Decreases swelling, enhances healing; good for dehisced wounds; can infuse antibiotics. Drainage counts as output."]},{"s":"Complications of Wounds▾","a":"complications-of-wounds","k":["Complications of Wounds▾","Adhesions, contractions, hemorrhage, dehiscence, evisceration, fistula, infection, excessive granulation tissue, keloids. Starred in lecture: hemorrhage, dehiscence and evisceration, infection.","Hemorrhage","Greatest risk up to 48 hours after injury or surgery.","Clot coming loose, slipped sutures, vessel damage.","Internal bleeding: swelling and distension, sanguineous drainage, subtle vital sign changes; first: increased heart rate (compensating for decreased stroke volume).","Hematoma: local blood collection, blue or red bruise.","Apply direct pressure, notify the provider, monitor vitals closely.","Infection","Infection does not happen within 24 to 48 hours: a low-grade temp then isn't infection yet.","Shows 2 to 11 days after surgery or injury: pain, erythema, swelling, purulent drainage, fever, chills, foul odor, raised vitals, leukocytosis.","Risks: impaired circulation, malnutrition, chronic disease, poor wound care.","Aseptic technique, nutrition, rest; antibiotics after cultures are drawn (broad spectrum, then tailored to C&S).","Dehiscence and evisceration","Dehiscence: separation of the wound, usually a few underlying layers. Evisceration (the worst): viscera visible; can follow abdominal incisions; needs immediate surgery.","Signs: a sudden large increase in sanguineous or serosanguineous drainage after straining; the patient felt something \"pop\" or \"give way.\"","Precipitated by coughing, sneezing, vomiting, hiccuping, straining for a BM.","Risks: older age, obesity, abdominal cancer, vomiting, infection, poor suturing, dehydration, malnutrition, increased pressure.","Evisceration or severe dehiscence: notify the provider immediately and stay with the patient. Cover the wound and any organs with sterile dressings soaked in sterile normal saline. Do not reinsert organs. Position with hips and knees bent. …"]},{"s":"Burns▾","a":"burns","k":["Burns▾","Thermal — Most common: flame, flash, scald, touching a hot object.","Chemical — Acid, alkali, chemicals.","Inhalation — Inhaled heat burns the nose, mouth, esophagus, lung parenchyma: compromises the airway.","Electrical — Electrical energy converted to heat.","Radiation — Transfer of radiant energy.","Extreme temperature — Frostbite; children and older adults at risk (less ability to generate heat).","Severity depends on depth, extent, location, age and other health problems. Severe burns go to a burn center (the ABA referral criteria are not needed).","Three depths: superficial partial thickness, deep partial thickness, full thickness (formerly first to fourth degree). Full thickness may be painless (nerves destroyed).","Rule of nines (quick initial estimate of extent): head 9 (front 4.5, back 4.5); each arm 9; anterior leg 9, posterior leg 9; trunk and back 9 x 2 (as stated). The instructor won't ask you to calculate it on her test, but has seen it on HESI…","The other chart on the slide is more accurate because it accounts for age and body proportion; the rule of nines is easier to do on the fly.","Emergent (Resuscitative) Phase","Stop the burning, ABCs (patent airway, breathing, blood pressure), assess the burn, transfer if criteria are met, stabilize. Lasts up to 72 hours from the burn; hemodynamically labile. Ends when fluid mobilizes and diuresis begins.","Main concern: massive fluid and electrolyte shift from the huge increase in capillary permeability, leading to hypovolemic shock and massive edema. Instructor takeaway for burns: excruciating pain and massive fluid and electrolyte shifts.","Cardiovascular — Shock; increased blood viscosity, clot risk. Circumferential burns: edema impairs circulation, so an escharotomy (scalpel through the eschar) restores distal perfusion.","Pulmonary — Upper and lower airway if fire was inhaled.","Urinary — Acute renal failure from decreased renal blood flow (shock) and myoglobin and hemoglobin blocking the tubules.","Nursing management","1. Airway. 2. Fluids.","Aggressive resuscitation: at least two large-bore IVs or a central line. Crystalloids such as LR; colloids, typically albumin, to raise cardiac output. (Fluid formulas: not needed.)","Wound care: debride necrotic tissue, escharotomy, fasciotomy (opening fascia for distal circulation). Keep the room warm (lost skin barrier). Goal: permanent skin coverage.","Drug and nutrition therapy","IV opioids and sedatives around the clock.","Tetanus shot if none in the last 10 years.","Topical antimicrobials (Silvadene); systemic antibiotics. Sepsis is the leading cause of death in burns.","VTE prophylaxis (increased viscosity).","Metabolism is extremely high: enteral (tube) feedings if the gut works.","Acute & Rehab Phases","Acute phase","Begins with mobilization of extracellular fluid and diuresis; ends when partial-thickness wounds heal and full-thickness wounds are grafted. Weeks to months.","Partial thickness: eschar removed, re-epithelialization. Full thickness: surgical debridement and skin grafts.","Split-thickness graft harvested with a dermatome (often the thigh), may be meshed; donor site covered with a hydrophilic foam dressing.","Keep monitoring labs: fluid and electrolytes still in flux. PT, OT.","Rehab phase","Goal: return to a functional role in society; reconstructive surgery.","Prevent contractures and hypertrophic scarring: PT, OT, scheduled range of motion, pressure garments (keep scars flat).","Emotional needs: anger, anxiety, depression, fear, guilt, helplessness."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"medsurg1-week6-gi.html","t":"Gastrointestinal: Upper GI, Bleeds, IBD, Ostomies & More","secs":[{"s":"Gastrointestinal: Upper GI, Bleeds, IBD, Ostomies & More","a":"","k":["Med Surg I Review · Week 6","GI Labs & Diagnostics","GI Drugs","GERD & Hiatal Hernia","Peptic Ulcer Disease","Upper GI Bleed","Bariatric Surgery","Inflammatory Bowel Disease","Bowel Ostomies","Colorectal Cancer","Diverticulosis & Diverticulitis","Intestinal Obstruction","Self-Test Flashcards","Med Surg I (NUR 334) review content. Built from the ten NUR 334 Week 6 voiceovers: GI labs and diagnostics, GERD and hiatal hernia, peptic ulcer disease, upper GI bleed, bariatric surgery, Crohn's and ulcerative colitis, bowel ostomies, col…"]},{"s":"GI Labs & Diagnostics▾","a":"gi-labs-diagnostics","k":["GI Labs & Diagnostics▾","Upper GI / small bowel follow-through — Patient swallows contrast (barium or Gastrografin); fluoroscopy and X-rays from mouth to small intestine. Films about every 30 minutes until contrast reaches the terminal ileum. Gastrografin, not bari…","Barium swallow / modified barium swallow — A closer look at the esophagus. For dysphagia after a stroke, non-cardiac chest pain, painful swallowing, reflux. Modified = different consistencies (thin liquids, honey-thick, applesauce). — Same …","Barium enema (lower GI) — Barium into the lower half. — Bowel clear of stool: enemas till clear, clear liquids the night before, NPO; cathartics after; white stool teaching.","Gastric emptying study — Nuclear imaging for gastroparesis (delayed emptying). Patient eats a cooked egg with a trace of radioactive material; scans at 0, 1, 2, 4 hours. — Only a trace of radioactivity, no danger; must lie flat during scann…","CT / MRI — CT: with or without contrast. MRI: radiofrequency waves. — CT contrast: ask about iodine or shellfish allergy (clinically not really relevant anymore). No MRI with metal or pregnancy.","EGD (esophagogastroduodenoscopy) — Scope the upper GI: tumors, inflammation, hiatal hernia, ulcers, obstruction, bleeding. Can be therapeutic (cauterize a bleed or varices). Conscious sedation; throat anesthetized. — NPO 8 hours; consent. A…","Colonoscopy — Scope the colon after a bowel prep; sedated; air used, so gas afterward. — A good prep matters: leftover stool means repeating the test. Watch for perforation and bleeding: blood, vital sign changes, tender abdomen.","Stool tests — Occult blood; culture (bacteria, including C. diff); ova and parasites. — Occult blood: avoid red meat 24 to 48 hours if ordered (evidence is debatable)."]},{"s":"GI Drugs▾","a":"gi-drugs","k":["GI Drugs▾","Antacids — — — Quick but short-lived; 1 to 3 hours after meals and at bedtime. Treats symptoms only.","H2 receptor blockers — \"-tidine\": cimetidine, famotidine — Decrease gastric acid. OTC or prescription.","Proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) — \"-prazole\": omeprazole, lansoprazole, esomeprazole, pantoprazole — Given IV in the hospital a lot.","Cytoprotective agent — Sucralfate — Coats the stomach. Focus on generics (NCLEX does).","Prokinetic — Metoclopramide — Enhances gastric emptying: gastroparesis, reflux after meals, and poor appetite (for example, during chemo)."]},{"s":"GERD & Hiatal Hernia▾","a":"gerd-hiatal-hernia","k":["GERD & Hiatal Hernia▾","GERD: backward flow of acidic stomach contents into the esophagus through a loose lower esophageal sphincter (LES). Heartburn is the most common symptom; regurgitation; respiratory symptoms (night coughing spells).","Complications and diagnosis","Esophagitis; Barrett's esophagus; cough and bronchospasm.","Barium swallow or EGD, but often just a high-dose PPI for about two weeks: if symptoms respond, that's the diagnosis and invasive tests aren't needed.","Lifestyle teaching","Identify and avoid triggers. Quit smoking: LES strength improves immediately.","Avoid fatty foods; small meals, no fluids with meals (drink between); no late-night snacking.","Sit up 2 to 3 hours after meals; raise the head of the bed on 4 to 6 inch blocks (\"gravity is not reflux's friend\").","Weight loss if large (eases intra-abdominal pressure); medications.","LINX device — Endoscopic. A ring of rare-earth magnets around the LES: increases closure but lets food pass.","Nissen fundoplication — Surgical (laparoscopic or open). The fundus is wrapped and sutured into a collar around the LES (\"a big bear hug\"). Laparoscopic preferred over a big incision when there's a choice.","Hiatal hernia","Part of the stomach herniates up through the diaphragm (diaphragmatic or esophageal hernia). Not reflux: a herniated area above the LES.","Often structural: increased abdominal pressure (pregnancy, obesity).","May be asymptomatic; symptoms and treatment similar to GERD.","Barrett's esophagus","Long-term esophagitis that changes cells (metaplasia). Can follow GERD or hiatal hernia; not everyone gets it.","Rare; confirmed by biopsy.","30 to 40 fold increased risk of esophageal cancer."]},{"s":"Peptic Ulcer Disease▾","a":"peptic-ulcer-disease","k":["Peptic Ulcer Disease▾","Erosion of GI mucosa from hydrochloric acid and pepsin. Causes: H. pylori and drugs, number one being NSAIDs. NSAIDs plus H. pylori = \"double trouble.\" Also alcohol, smoking, family history. Stress aggravates an existing ulcer but hasn't be…","Commit to memory: NSAIDs (ibuprofen, naproxen) = GI upset and kidney problems. Acetaminophen (not an NSAID) = hepatotoxicity.","Types","Gastric: usually the antrum. Duodenal: just past the pyloric sphincter; 80% of ulcers are duodenal.","Most ulcers have H. pylori (the percentage was garbled in the transcript as \"90 to 90%\").","Acute: fewer layers, shorter, resolves. Chronic: 4 times more common, more severe, long-term.","Diagnosis","EGD.","Urea breath test and stool test: non-invasive H. pylori tests. Biopsy is the gold standard (invasive).","CBC (H&H for bleeding) and stool for occult blood (may be dark and tarry or invisible).","Complications: HOP","Hemorrhage (most common) — A creeping heart rate (70, 80, 86, 95: investigate). Redder NG aspirate. Pain may decrease at first (blood neutralizes gastric contents). — NG to low wall suction to decompress; then as for an upper GI bleed.","Obstruction — Most likely near the pyloric sphincter; gradual onset. — NG to low wall suction until resolved; irrigate per protocol.","Perforation — Sudden, severe pain radiating to the back, not relieved by anything; rigid, board-like abdomen; shallow fast respirations; weak, rapid pulse; absent bowel sounds (listen 5 minutes); rebound tenderness. — Bacterial peritonitis …","Collaborative care","Rest; quit smoking; no special diet (individualized).","Stop aspirin and NSAIDs for 4 to 6 weeks; if aspirin must continue, give it with a PPI.","H. pylori: antibiotics plus PPIs; maybe sucralfate.","Refractory PUD: Billroth I or II reconstruction after vagotomy (cuts the vagus nerve to decrease HCl) and antrectomy. Know it exists, not the differences.","Dumping syndrome","After part of the stomach is removed: PUD surgery, gastrectomy, fundoplication, Roux-en-Y.","A hyperosmolar (concentrated sugar) bolus dumps into the small intestine; fluid shifts out of the vascular space into the gut: diarrhea, dizziness, sweating, palpitations soon after eating.","Later, postprandial hypoglycemia about 2 hours after eating (insulin surge).","Self-limiting: a few months up to a year.","Dumping syndrome diet: six small meals, never a big one · no fluids with meals · avoid concentrated simple sugars · protein and fat encouraged · rest after eating."]},{"s":"Upper GI Bleed▾","a":"upper-gi-bleed","k":["Upper GI Bleed▾","Hematemesis, bright red (frank) — Recent or ongoing bleed.","Coffee-ground emesis — Blood partly digested: older bleeding or bleeding has stopped. Malodorous.","Melena — Black, tarry, malodorous stool; up to 8 hours of digestion.","Occult blood — Not visible; positive guaiac. Never expected unless a GI bleed is already diagnosed.","Causes","Ulcers: 50% of upper GI bleeds. The two most common causes of bleeding ulcers: H. pylori and NSAIDs. Also aspirin and steroids.","Stress-related mucosal disease (SRMD): in 70 to 90% of ICU patients, so ICU patients get a PPI (IV pantoprazole).","Esophageal varices (portal hypertension from end-stage liver disease): bleeding can be uncontrollable. A Blakemore tube (balloon pressure on the bleed) is an emergency, ICU-bound measure.","Nursing","Vital signs vs baseline; orthostatic hypotension; signs of hypovolemia: rising HR, insatiable thirst, cool clammy skin, restlessness (always investigate).","Rigid, board-like abdomen = perforation; distended with guarding = peritonitis.","Labs: H&H and BUN (a GI bleed raises BUN).","18-gauge IV (anticipate PRBCs, fluid resuscitation).","NG lavage (effectiveness questionable, but on standardized exams).","Endoscopy with hemostasis is first line: nothing else helps until the bleed is stopped.","BUN rising with a GI bleed covered again here — review the BUN vs creatinine logic in Week 4: Renal & Urinary if needed"]},{"s":"Bariatric Surgery▾","a":"bariatric-surgery","k":["Bariatric Surgery▾","Currently the only treatment with lasting weight loss in severe obesity. Criteria: BMI 40 or higher, or 35 with one or more severe obesity-related complications (hypertension, diabetes, heart failure, sleep apnea). Extensive psychological, …","Sleeve gastrectomy — Restrictive — Greater curvature removed and stapled: a tubular stomach. Permanent.","Intragastric balloon — Restrictive — Minimally invasive, temporary: a saline balloon of about 400 to 700 mL promotes satiety. Usually not left more than 6 months (leak risk).","Roux-en-Y gastric bypass — Restrictive and malabsorptive — Gold standard and most common. Pouch only 15 mL (a medicine cup is 30). Rest of the stomach disconnected, not removed. Stay 2 to 3 days; 60 to 70% weight loss in 1 to 2 years.","Outcomes","Better: glucose tolerance, diabetes, blood pressure, cholesterol and triglycerides, GERD, sleep apnea.","Adverse: malabsorption of vitamins and iron, so lifelong vitamin supplementation; dumping syndrome.","Post-op care","Control pain to enhance mobility.","Vigilant for wound infection, dehiscence and evisceration (higher risk with obesity).","NG to wall suction at first; then water and sugar-free liquids in tiny amounts, high-protein liquids, pureed, then solids.","Long-term: the DASH diet, in smaller portions."]},{"s":"Inflammatory Bowel Disease▾","a":"inflammatory-bowel-disease","k":["Inflammatory Bowel Disease▾","Location — Mouth to anus; most often the terminal ileum (and colon) — Colon only; starts in the rectum and moves up","Pattern — Skip lesions, cobblestone — Uniform and continuous","Depth — Transmural: more likely to perforate — Mucosal / submucosal","Bleeding — Sometimes — More common","Malabsorption — Yes (small intestine) — Not usually (colon absorbs water)","Complications — Abscesses, fistulas, strictures, perforation — Perforation from toxic megacolon (inflammation in deep layers; colon stops working, enlarges, can rupture)","Surgery — Usually for strictures; not curative; recurrence very high — Removing the colon is curative","Both: chronic intestinal inflammation with remissions and flares; cause unknown; autoimmune; no cure (except colectomy for UC). Diarrhea, weight loss, abdominal pain, fever, fatigue. Colon cancer risk increased in both (the lecture said \"no…","During a flare: rest the bowel. NPO with IV hydration, control inflammation, treat infection, correct malnutrition, reduce stress.","Nutrition","Malnutrition: poor intake, high metabolic rate, steroids, malabsorption, protein-losing enteropathy (proteins leak into the GI tract).","When eating resumes: high calorie, high protein, low residue (low fiber keeps the gut calm), plus vitamin and iron supplements.","No universal triggers: keep a food diary. Quit smoking.","Drugs","Sulfasalazine (GI anti-inflammatory) still the drug of choice; aminosalicylates decrease inflammation.","Antimicrobials, steroids (great anti-inflammatories, many side effects), immunosuppressants, immunomodulators (TNF).","UC: drug therapy induces remission 80% of the time; 1 in 5 eventually need surgery. Crohn's: most need surgery at some point.","Specific IBD drugs: not on this instructor's test (they are in pathopharm).","TPN","If the gut works, use it. A comatose patient with a working gut gets tube feedings, not TPN. TPN is for a gut that can't be used (severe flare, severe vomiting, recovering bowel resection).","Total parenteral nutrition is IV and nutritionally complete; positive nitrogen balance while the bowel rests. Not ideal long-term: an unused gut atrophies.","Very hypertonic (stated as D10 or D20, and later D15, D20, D25): needs a central line and a dedicated line. Lipids (milky) run separately.","Blood sugar checks every 6 hours; may need insulin coverage.","Start slowly and stop slowly (for example 10, 20, 30, 50 mL/hr up; 50, 40, 30, 20 down) so the pancreas can adapt.","PPN (partial) exists but is rarely used.","Central lines covered again here — review in Week 2: Hematology if needed","IBD Surgery & Teaching","Total proctocolectomy with ileal pouch-anal reservoir (J pouch) — Most common for UC; two stages. Colon removed, J pouch made, temporary ileostomy while it heals; a couple of months later a takedown and stool passes through the rectum again…","Total proctocolectomy with permanent ileostomy — Very liquid, caustic stool (no water reabsorption).","Continent ileostomy (Kock pouch) — No bag; the patient self-catheterizes the stoma; dressing over it between times.","Crohn's: resection with anastomosis — Unhealthy section removed and healthy bowel sewn together.","Kock pouch for urine covered in Week 4: Urinary Diversions — this is the stool version","Teach rest, diet, perianal care with diarrhea, meds and side effects, signs of recurrence, stress-reducing activities.","After colon surgery, call the provider for new drainage, wound opening or redness, a change in the stoma, no BM or gas for more than 24 hours, increased pain, vomiting, swelling, high ostomy output, dark or no urine, fever, or unable to tak…"]},{"s":"Bowel Ostomies▾","a":"bowel-ostomies","k":["Bowel Ostomies▾","Location tells you the output. Ileostomy: right lower quadrant, very liquid (no time for water reabsorption), caustic, oozes almost continuously, always needs a bag. Left lower quadrant (descending or sigmoid) colostomy: almost like a norma…","End stoma — Bowel cut; the proximal end brought out as one stoma. — Distal portion removed (permanent) or sewn closed and left in as a Hartmann's pouch, so it can be reconnected later (a takedown, a provider decision).","Loop stoma — A loop brought to the skin, slit on the anterior wall, held by a plastic rod. — Proximal opening = stool; distal opening = mucus. Usually temporary.","Double-barrel stoma — Bowel divided; both ends brought out as two separate stomas (no rod). — Proximal = stool; distal = mucous fistula. Usually temporary.","Stoma care","Know whether it's temporary or permanent before you go in.","Assess color, swelling, bleeding.","Mild soap and warm water; skin barrier, then a snug pouch cut to the exact stoma size so no skin touches the drainage.","Empty the bag often; release gas; don't make faces. Ostomy nurses are a great resource.","Other points","A colostomy near the rectum (solid stool) may be irrigated like an enema through the stoma, every day or two.","Old stool and mucus may still pass from the anus.","Teaching may go slowly: a patient not yet willing to look at the stoma at discharge isn't uncommon."]},{"s":"Colorectal Cancer▾","a":"colorectal-cancer","k":["Colorectal Cancer▾","Risks and signs","Red or processed meat, overweight, inactivity, alcohol, smoking, few fruits and vegetables, IBD history, genetics.","Insidious: often found advanced. Anemia (low H&H), rectal bleeding, abdominal pain, change in bowel habits, obstruction or perforation.","Rectal: discomfort, blood in stool. Descending: bowel change, bright red stool. Ascending: subtler (anemia).","Screening","Colonoscopy is the gold standard: average risk starts at 45, every 10 years; earlier and more often with strong family history or IBD. Remove precancerous polyps.","Fecal occult blood or stool DNA test: less favorable but acceptable, yearly.","Abdominoperineal resection (APR) — Tumor and part of the peritoneum removed; permanent colostomy (descending).","Low anterior resection (LAR) — Two steps with a temporary ostomy and Hartmann's pouch, then reconnected: sphincter preserved, normal control of defecation."]},{"s":"Diverticulosis & Diverticulitis▾","a":"diverticulosis-diverticulitis","k":["Diverticulosis & Diverticulitis▾","Diverticulosis (the condition)","Outpouchings of the intestinal mucosa. Usually asymptomatic; maybe pain, bloating, gas, bowel changes.","Prevent: high fiber, low fat, low red meat, activity. With it: keep eating high fiber.","No need to avoid nuts and seeds (no evidence) unless they cause a flare.","Weight reduction; avoid raised intra-abdominal pressure (constipation).","Diverticulitis (the flare)","Left lower quadrant pain, palpable mass, signs of infection (an older adult may only be confused).","NPO to rest the gut; if severe, admit for IV fluids and antibiotics.","Watch for abscess, bleeding, peritonitis (a ruptured diverticulum spills stool into the peritoneum).","Complications may need resection and a temporary ostomy."]},{"s":"Intestinal Obstruction▾","a":"intestinal-obstruction","k":["Intestinal Obstruction▾","Mechanical (most obstructions)","Mostly small intestine: adhesions, hernias, strictures.","Surgical adhesions are the number one cause, days to years post-op.","Non-mechanical: paralytic ileus","About 1 in 10. Peristalsis stops, most often after the bowel has been manipulated.","NG to low wall suction and wait for peristalsis, or the patient keeps vomiting.","Care: decompress (NG to low wall suction), correct fluids and electrolytes, pain control, remove the obstruction if needed (bowel resection with end-to-end anastomosis: no stoma)."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"medsurg1-week7-cancer-hepatobiliary.html","t":"Cancer, Liver, Gallbladder & Pancreas","secs":[{"s":"Cancer, Liver, Gallbladder & Pancreas","a":"","k":["Med Surg I Review · Week 7","Cancer: Prevention, Diagnosis & Treatment","Managing Cancer Treatment Side Effects","Hepatobiliary Labs & Diagnostics","Hepatitis","Cirrhosis","Cholelithiasis & Cholecystitis","Pancreatitis","Self-Test Flashcards","Med Surg I (NUR 334) review content. Built from the seven NUR 334 Week 7 voiceovers: cancer overview, cancer treatment side effects, hepatobiliary labs and diagnostics, hepatitis, cirrhosis, cholelithiasis, and pancreatitis."]},{"s":"Cancer: Prevention, Diagnosis & Treatment▾","a":"cancer-prevention-diagnosis-treatment","k":["Cancer: Prevention, Diagnosis & Treatment▾","Prevention and early detection","Avoid carcinogens: excess alcohol, poor diet, tobacco, too much sun.","Diet, exercise, rest; regular exams and screenings; teach self-exams (breast, testicular).","Diagnosis","History; cytology (Pap, sputum); tissue biopsy; labs; chest X-ray; mammogram.","Tumor markers (PSA, CEA): made by tumor cells; used for diagnosis or progression.","Bone marrow exam for cancers that start in the marrow (leukemia, lymphoma).","CAUTION, the seven warning signs: Change in bowel or bladder habits · A sore that does not heal · Unusual bleeding or discharge · Thickening or lump in the breast or elsewhere · Indigestion or difficulty swallowing · Obvious change in a war…","Goals: cure; if not, control; if not, palliative care. Surgery (about half of patients): prophylactic (double mastectomy for a high-risk gene), biopsy, cure, control, debulking (remove part of a tumor for comfort or to help chemo and radiat…","Chemotherapy","Giving chemo","The mainstay of cancer therapy: solid tumors and blood cancers. Systemic.","Must be chemo certified; very toxic; protect patient and nurse; two-RN double check.","Central venous catheters preferred (not required).","Premedicate for nausea and vomiting (and anticipatory nausea and vomiting); anti-inflammatory and anti-allergy meds.","Check WBC, platelets, H&H first: hold chemo if WBCs or platelets are too low.","Nadir","The point after chemo when counts are at their lowest; they then regenerate.","Chemo every two weeks: about day 7, 8, 9 the patient feels worst; counts should recover by the next visit, or treatment is delayed.","Teach: you won't feel well then; limit exposure to other people.","Central venous catheters and ports covered again here — review in Week 2: Hematology if needed","Extravasation (a vesicant infiltrating tissue) can cause necrosis and limb loss. Prevent: watch the site closely, a patent, free-flowing IV with good blood return. Pain is the cardinal symptom, but not always. Blood return lost? Stop the ch…","Radiation","About 60% of patients with cancer get radiation. Local, precisely targeted (chemo is systemic); alone or with chemo or surgery.","External (teletherapy) — Most common; visits for weeks, under half an hour each. — No. No risk to anyone when they leave.","Internal, sealed (brachytherapy) — Radioactive seeds implanted (prostate, breast, lung). — Emits radiation while the implant is active; excretions are not radioactive.","Internal, unsealed (brachytherapy) — Swallowed, for example iodine-131 for thyroid cancer. — Yes: excretions are radioactive (urine, stool, vomit, sweat).","Sealed implant precautions","Time, distance, shielding. Wear your own dosimeter (tracks exposure; no protection; don't share).","Private room, sign on the door.","No pregnant women, no children under 16. Visitors 30 minutes a day, 6 feet from the source.","Dislodged source: forceps into the lead container in the room, never hands.","Save dressings and linens until the source is removed.","Skin teaching (radiated area)","Wash daily with mild soap and water, using the hand, not a washcloth; rinse well.","Don't wash off skin markings.","Pat dry. No powders, lotions or creams unless the radiologist prescribes them.","Soft, loose clothing; no direct sun or temperature extremes.","Dry vs wet desquamation (moist, incredibly tender)."]},{"s":"Managing Cancer Treatment Side Effects▾","a":"managing-cancer-treatment-side-effects","k":["Managing Cancer Treatment Side Effects▾","Chemo kills rapidly dividing cells: bone marrow, GI tract, hair. Radiation effects depend on where it's aimed (bowel: diarrhea; neck: swallowing and taste).","Greater than 2,500 — Where we like it.","Greater than 1,000 — Low but pretty safe.","Less than 1,000 — Neutropenic.","Less than 500 — Protective precautions.","In cancer care, neutrophils are an actual count (segs plus bands), not a differential percentage. Neutrophils answer acute bacterial infection, so neutropenia = infection risk; filgrastim (a WBC growth factor) may be given.","Neutropenic precautions","Isolation technique, screen visitors, no fresh flowers or plants, monitor for infection.","Diet: avoid raw fruits and vegetables that can't be peeled (berries); peelable ones are OK (oranges, bananas, apples, pineapple); wrapped food and flatware; no raw peanuts or seeds.","Platelets and anemia","Platelets normal 150,000 to 400,000. Serious bleeding may not show until below 50,000; transfuse usually below 20,000.","Anemia: fatigue. Monitor H&H; energy conservation but stay active; iron-rich diet. Epoetin if hemoglobin is less than 11; PRBCs if severe.","GI effects","Anorexia, taste changes (focus on liked foods), nausea and vomiting.","Weigh twice weekly; albumin and prealbumin; small, frequent, high-protein, high-calorie meals; supplements.","Diarrhea is often from localized radiation.","Stomatitis","Inflamed, ulcerated mouth (mucositis is the general term); extremely painful.","Thorough oral assessment; meticulous, gentle brushing and flossing; saline gargles; numbing swish-and-spit mixtures.","No alcohol mouthwash. Artificial saliva; soft, non-irritating foods.","Other effects: fatigue, hair loss (unpredictable; may come back thicker and curly), lung and heart damage, infertility in young people.","Support the right to complementary treatments while encouraging the prescribed plan; a calm, present, healing environment.","Psychosocial: hair or body-part loss, fear of recurrence (\"body vigilant\"), secondary cancers, disability and death. Hope is positive reappraisal; spirituality can help or hurt; social support is one of the most important things."]},{"s":"Hepatobiliary Labs & Diagnostics▾","a":"hepatobiliary-labs-diagnostics","k":["Hepatobiliary Labs & Diagnostics▾","Organ shortcuts: kidneys = BUN and creatinine · liver = AST and ALT · pancreas = amylase and lipase.","ALT, AST, alkaline phosphatase — ALT is most specific to the liver; AST and alk phos are nonspecific. Elevated AST and ALT point to liver disease (no need to memorize numbers).","Conjugated (direct) bilirubin — Conjugated by the liver, water soluble, found in urine; elevated in obstructive jaundice (dark urine).","Unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin — Not water soluble, not in urine; elevated with problems in or before the liver.","Serum ammonia — Normally converted to urea in the liver. Buildup crosses the blood-brain barrier: hepatic encephalopathy.","Serum protein / albumin — Albumin is made in the liver: low in liver disease.","Prothrombin time (PT) — In seconds. Liver disease: prolonged PT, bleeding tendency.","Hepatitis antigens / antibodies — The only way to tell the viral hepatitis types apart (genotypes not needed).","Amylase — Digests carbohydrates; 60 to 120. In acute pancreatitis it rises within 12 hours, peaks about 24, normal in 48 to 72.","Lipase — Digests fat; 0 to 160. Rises more slowly and stays elevated longer than amylase.","Not tested: the actual amylase and lipase values (\"the test question will just say that it's elevated\") and the LFT numbers.","Abdominal ultrasound — 95% accurate for gallstones; non-invasive. — NPO 8 hours for the gallbladder.","CT with contrast — Different depths. — Know the BUN and creatinine (contrast can be nephrotoxic); watch for reaction; warn about the warm flush; fluids after.","MRI — — — Not with metal implants or pregnancy.","HIDA scan — Nuclear medicine; done when gallstones are suspected but the ultrasound didn't show them. Shows patency of the common bile duct. — —","ERCP — Scope through the mouth to the duodenum, then backward into the pancreatic, hepatic and common bile ducts. — NPO, consent, sedation. After: vital signs for perforation or infection; most common complication is pancreatitis; nothing b…","Liver biopsy — Very vascular organ. — Before: coags (PT/INR), type and crossmatch, consent, baseline vitals; patient holds breath after expiration for the needle. After: frequent vitals (rising HR first with bleeding); lie on the right side…"]},{"s":"Hepatitis▾","a":"hepatitis","k":["Hepatitis▾","Often no symptoms: 30% of hepatitis B and as much as 80% of hepatitis C. Acute phase lasts 1 to 4 months: vague malaise, flu-like symptoms (myalgia, arthralgia), nausea and vomiting, RUQ discomfort, hepatomegaly, jaundice (icteric), itching…","Transmission — Fecal-oral — Infected blood, high-risk behaviors, perinatal — Similar to B","Symptoms — Mild, self-limiting — Asymptomatic to fulminant (severe liver failure) — Many have none","Chronic? — Never — A small number — Tends to linger","Vaccine — Yes: all children from 12 months, high-risk adults — Yes: three doses, all infants — None","Post-exposure — Vaccine or immune globulin (about 2 months' protection) within 2 weeks — Within 24 hours; no benefit after 7 days — —","Also — Typically no drug therapy — \"Leading cause of cancer\" (as stated). Drugs only after genotyping, for severe liver failure. — Leading indication for liver transplant. Drugs if chronic (most are); genotyped.","HIV post-exposure prophylaxis (within 1 to 2 hours) — compare in Week 5: HIV","Jaundice","Yellow sclera, skin or hard palate; always too much bilirubin; usually present when bilirubin is greater than 2.5. A symptom, not a disease.","Hemolytic: RBC breakdown (wrong blood; check urine for hematuria).","Obstructive: intrahepatic or extrahepatic (a gallstone in the common bile duct). Bilirubin can't reach the gut: clay-colored stools and dark urine.","Care and teaching","No specific treatment: rest, managed at home.","No special diet: well balanced, adequate calories; low fat if fat isn't tolerated; B complex and vitamin K.","Itching: diphenhydramine.","Stop alcohol; avoid hepatotoxic drugs: acetaminophen and isoniazid.","Hygiene; treated or bottled water abroad; don't share linens, utensils, glasses, needles, razors, nail clippers, toothbrushes; condoms; cover cuts. Hep C: never donate blood, organs or tissue. Notify contacts."]},{"s":"Cirrhosis▾","a":"cirrhosis","k":["Cirrhosis▾","Early (insidious): weight loss, weakness, GI upset, bowel changes, nausea and vomiting. Late: jaundice, low albumin, prolonged PT, and everything driven by portal hypertension.","Ascites — Portal hypertension plus low albumin (lost \"pulling power\"): fluid third-spaces into the peritoneum.","Bleeding risk — Lack of prothrombin: longer bleeding times.","Splenomegaly — Congestion from portal hypertension (spleen is LUQ; liver mostly RUQ).","Spider angiomas, caput medusae — Engorged vessels from portal hypertension.","Esophageal and anorectal varices — Backed-up, engorged veins; esophageal varices are very dangerous if they bleed.","Hepatic encephalopathy — Too much serum ammonia (worth memorizing): not converted to urea; crosses the blood-brain barrier; sedative. Reversible. Confusion, slowed responses, personality change, ataxia, up to hepatic coma.","Asterixis (\"liver flap\") — Wrists flexed, hands flap involuntarily: elevated ammonia.","Managing ascites and fluid","Rest first.","Fluid and sodium restriction (water follows salt).","Diuretics: spironolactone (potassium sparing) and furosemide (potassium losing), often both.","Albumin to raise oncotic pressure.","Abdominal girth with skin markings, but daily weight is more accurate.","Paracentesis and TIPS","Paracentesis when ascites impairs breathing. Consent, baseline vitals, have the patient void (avoid puncturing a full bladder), supine or high Fowler's. Often repeated in cirrhosis.","TIPS (transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt): a non-surgical stent that lowers portal hypertension, controlling ascites and variceal bleeding.","Nutrition","High calorie, high carbohydrate, moderate to low fat.","Restrict protein only with severe encephalopathy; otherwise protein's benefits outweigh the ammonia risk.","Low sodium with fluid excess and ascites.","Bleeding Precautions","Assess","Coags; oral cavity (gums bleed first); ecchymosis, purpura, petechiae.","Fall prevention: a fall risks head injury and subarachnoid hemorrhage.","Avoid","Aspirin; alcohol, spicy and bulky foods; injections.","Vigorous nose blowing and straining at stool (raises pressure and can rupture varices): stool softeners.","Hard toothbrushes, rectal temps, enemas. Hold pressure longer after an IM shot.","Bleeding esophageal varices are an emergency. Endoscopic sclerotherapy (injected sclerosing agent) or variceal banding (rubber bands around the base). For an active bleed: balloon tamponade (Blakemore tube) applies direct pressure; the pati…","Hepatic Encephalopathy Care","Goal: get the ammonia down.","Control GI bleeding: blood in the gut is nitrogen-rich and raises ammonia.","Avoid constipation. Lactulose titrated to about 2 to 4 stools a day to remove ammonia. Teach why, even if they've already had a BM today.","Monitor level of consciousness and GCS (EMV); safety precautions for confusion."]},{"s":"Cholelithiasis & Cholecystitis▾","a":"cholelithiasis-cholecystitis","k":["Cholelithiasis & Cholecystitis▾","Cholelithiasis = gallstones in the gallbladder; cholecystitis = inflamed gallbladder (acute or chronic). Risks: middle-aged female, fair skin, overweight, high-fat diet, oral contraceptives.","Manifestations","Episodic, vague upper abdominal pain radiating to the right shoulder, triggered by high-fat or large meals.","Nausea, vomiting, indigestion, belching, gas; fever with inflammation.","Jaundice, clay-colored stools, dark urine, steatorrhea: most common with chronic cholecystitis (a stone blocking the common bile duct).","Treatment","Non-surgical: avoid fatty foods; NPO during a flare; opioids, antiemetics, antispasmodics for biliary pain.","Rarely lithotripsy; uncommonly ERCP with sphincterotomy.","Most common: laparoscopic cholecystectomy.","After a lap chole","Remove the puncture-site bandages the day after surgery; may shower.","Call for redness, swelling, purulent or bile-colored drainage, increasing pain, nausea, vomiting, fever, chills.","Gradually increase activity; back to work in about a week.","Low-fat diet tolerated better for the first few weeks; then a regular diet.","Open cholecystectomy and T-tube","Subcostal incision healing by primary intention.","A T-tube keeps the common bile duct patent while inflammation goes down.","Rarely goes home with it: teach emptying the bile bag."]},{"s":"Pancreatitis▾","a":"pancreatitis","k":["Pancreatitis▾","Acute: premature activation of pancreatic enzymes that autodigest the pancreas. Gallstones and alcohol are the two most common causes. CT is the best imaging for pancreatitis and its complications.","Severe pain; nausea and vomiting that vomiting doesn't relieve — Distension and peritoneal irritation (visceral pain).","Low-grade temp, high WBC — Inflammation.","Jaundice — Hepatobiliary obstruction.","Paralytic ileus — Peritoneal irritation: NG to low wall suction until it resolves.","Cullen's and Turner's signs — Bluish discoloration from enzyme leak into subcutaneous tissue: Cullen's around the umbilicus, Turner's the other location. Severe disease, poor prognosis.","Hypovolemia, tachycardia — Inflammatory mediators increase vascular permeability.","High amylase and lipase — The pancreatic enzymes.","High triglycerides; hypocalcemia — Fat necrosis (and deposits).","Complications","Pseudocyst: cavity of necrotic products outside the pancreas; often resolves; worst case it perforates into the peritoneum (severe peritonitis).","Abscess: within the pancreas; often needs surgical drainage.","Acute care","Pain relief first (can cause hemodynamic compromise): IV opioids (morphine); knees bent / fetal position.","Labile vitals: monitor often; fluid balance; aggressive IV hydration.","NPO with NG to low wall suction during a flare to rest the pancreas.","Monitor stools for steatorrhea and blood sugar for hyperglycemia (endocrine function).","Quit alcohol. Diet after NPO: carbohydrates encouraged (less stimulating), fat restricted.","Chronic Pancreatitis","Progressive, destructive inflammation and fibrosis with remissions and flares. Intense pain (less tenderness than acute); a mass is likely a pseudocyst or abscess; ascites with possible respiratory compromise; steatorrhea (oily, floating, g…","Opioids given more cautiously (some can increase pain through spasms).","Weight loss can be significant: 4,000 to 6,000 calories a day PO, so TPN may be considered.","Prevent flares: avoid alcohol, caffeine, nicotine, spices; bland, low-fat, high-protein, high-carbohydrate, small high-calorie meals; rest after eating.","Pancreatic enzyme replacement therapy (PERT): pancrelipase","Standard of care to prevent malnutrition, malabsorption and weight loss. Contains amylase, lipase and protease.","Take with the first bite of every meal or snack. Sometimes ordered with an antacid or H2 blocker (pH).","Swallow without chewing; wipe the lips. Powder in applesauce or juice; not mixed with protein foods. Don't crush enteric-coated forms.","Record the number and consistency of stools: goal is fewer, less fatty stools."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Click a card to flip it and check your answer."]}]},{"f":"medsurg1-comprehensive-study-guide.html","t":"Med Surg I Comprehensive Study Guide","secs":[{"s":"Med Surg I Comprehensive Study Guide","a":"","k":["Med Surg I Review · Comprehensive","Part 1 — Respiratory","Part 2 — Hematology: Anemia, Polycythemia, Central Lines & Blood Products","Part 3 — Perioperative Care, Fluids & Electrolytes","Part 4 — Renal & Urinary: UTI, Stones, AKI, CKD & Dialysis","Part 5 — HIV, Skin Disorders, Surgical Wounds & Burns","Part 6 — Gastrointestinal: Upper GI, Bleeds, IBD, Ostomies & More","Part 7 — Cancer, Liver, Gallbladder & Pancreas","Self-Test Flashcards","All of the Med Surg I (NUR 334) review material in one long page, in the order it was taught: Part 1 respiratory, Part 2 hematology, Part 3 perioperative care, fluids and electrolytes, Part 4 renal and urinary, Part 5 HIV, skin, wounds and …"]},{"s":"Part 1 — Respiratory▾","a":"part-1-respiratory","k":["Part 1 — Respiratory▾","From Respiratory. Med Surg I (NUR 334) review content. Built from the NUR 334 Week 1 voiceovers (respiratory labs and diagnostics, upper respiratory problems, obstructive sleep apnea, trachs, pharyngitis, head and neck cancer, pneumonia, CO…","Respiratory Labs & Diagnostics","The NUR 334 instructor: every system starts with a labs-and-diagnostics voiceover, you are held accountable for the related nursing care (pre- and post-procedure) of each one, and you should be able to explain each test to a patient. ABGs a…","Pulse Oximetry","O2 sat","The amount of hemoglobin that is carrying oxygen.","Normal: 95% or greater (voiceover); 95 to 100 (live lecture). Less than 90% can indicate hypoxemia (live lecture).","A low sat could mean hypoventilation, atelectasis, a pneumothorax, or other lung issues.","Not invasive; intermittent (with vital signs, Q4) or continuous (fresh post-op). Commonly used to titrate oxygen.","Best on a finger without nail polish (or fungus); the earlobe or toe is sometimes used, but a finger probe belongs on a finger.","On oxygen, we don't love 100%: wean it (for example from 4 L to 2 L nasal cannula). The exception is hyperoxygenating before trach suctioning. 100% on room air is fine.","The bus analogy: the red blood cell is the bus, hemoglobin is the seats (iron is what the seats are made of), and oxygen is the passengers. The O2 sat is how full the buses are; the hematocrit is how much of the highway is made up of buses.","Sputum Studies","The instructor says to commit these three to memory.","Culture and sensitivity (C&S) — Bacteria. The culture identifies the pathogen; the sensitivity shows which antibiotic works. Results can take 48 to 72 hours.","Cytology — Cancer cells (malignancy or abnormal changes); ordered when lung cancer is suspected.","Acid-fast bacillus (AFB) — Active tuberculosis; the definitive diagnosis of TB.","Collecting sputum","From deep in the lungs, not saliva: have the patient cough (expectorate), don't let them just spit in the jar.","Sterile container; to the lab as soon as possible (not sitting on the bedside table all day).","The morning specimen is best: secretions have settled overnight and the patient hasn't eaten or drunk.","Suction the patient if they can't cough productively.","Imaging","Chest X-ray — A two-dimensional picture of the lungs (and heart and chest wall): pneumonia, masses, pleural effusions. — Remove metal between the neck and waist (bra straps, necklaces). Usually two views. The voiceover names the most common…","CT (CAT) scan — Structures in cross-section; better visualization. — May need sedation to lie still or for claustrophobia; hard table, loud clicking. Contrast is provider preference and is usually iodine-based: ask about contrast allergy (a…","MRI — Lesions hard to assess by CT; distinguishes vascular from non-vascular structures. — Contrast is not iodine-based. Closed MRI may need sedation for claustrophobia. Remove all metal.","PET scan — A radioactive tracer through the IV; looks at physiology and function (blood flow, oxygen uptake, sugar uptake), not structure. — Used a lot for cancer (has it spread?) and some brain disorders.","Shellfish and contrast: research says a shellfish (or iodine) allergy is no longer linked to contrast reactions, so in practice it isn't a contraindication, but NCLEX and standardized tests \"can be late to the game.\" An allergy to contrast …","CT with contrast and the kidneys: if the creatinine is elevated (for example, \"their creatinine's 2\"), let the provider know before the scan. Whether to proceed is the provider's risk-benefit call.","TB Testing","Skin test (Mantoux / PPD) and IGRA","The TB skin test is intradermal (needle at a 10 to 15 degree angle) and should raise a bleb. Read in 48 to 72 hours.","The IGRA (interferon gamma release assay; QuantiFERON-TB Gold) is a blood test; no return visit to read it. Neither test is better than the other.","Both are screening tests: they do not tell latent from active TB. Positive screen, then chest X-ray; positive chest X-ray, then sputum for AFB.","Bronchoscopy","What it does","Scoping (directly visualizing) the bronchi and airways.","Obtain a biopsy; remove secretions the patient can't expectorate (lavage); remove a foreign body; diagnose infection, tumors, bleeding.","Nursing care","Signed consent (invasive). Oropharynx anesthetized; sedation (conscious sedation or general).","NPO before: 8 to 12 hours.","NPO until the gag reflex returns.","Blood-tinged mucus is expected: document and continue to monitor; no need to call the provider.","After a biopsy, watch for bleeding (an unexplained rising heart rate) or a pneumothorax (abrupt shortness of air).","Lung Biopsy","Transbronchial — Through a bronchoscope, in the endoscopy suite.","Transthoracic needle aspiration (percutaneous) — A needle through the thorax, with CT (or ultrasound) guidance, in radiology.","Open lung biopsy — An incision, in the OR.","VATS (video-assisted thoracic surgery) — A camera, in the OR.","\"-scopy\" means scoping an orifice (bronchoscopy, colonoscopy, cystoscopy); endoscopy is the umbrella term. Break big words apart to figure them out.","Thoracentesis","Removing pleural fluid","The provider puts a large-bore needle into the pleural space for a pleural effusion (fluid where it doesn't belong; third spacing that reduces surface area for oxygen). \"-centesis\" removes fluid or gas (paracentesis is the abdominal version…","Can be diagnostic (analyze the fluid), therapeutic (relieve dyspnea), or both.","Before: consent; sit upright with elbows on the overbed table (opens the intercostal spaces); stay very still and don't talk.","After: a chest X-ray to rule out a pneumothorax; assess for hypoxia. Risks: pneumothorax, bleeding, infection.","Pulmonary Function Tests & Peak Flow","PFTs","Measure lung function with respect to time (seconds); usually done by respiratory therapy, nose pinched, mouth sealed on the device.","Total lung capacity: inhale as deep as you can and exhale as deep as you can.","FEV1 (forced expiratory volume in 1 second): a deep breath in, then push it out as fast as you can. It is lower in obstructive pulmonary disease, where getting air out is the problem.","Peak flow meter","More of an asthma device (peds content), carried by the patient to check their own FEV1.","They track their personal best; a drop from it may signal an early asthma attack.","Upper Respiratory Problems","Epistaxis (Nosebleed)","Causes","Irritation (dry air), trauma, infection, a foreign body (kids), tumors.","Hypertension; blood dyscrasias; anticoagulants; chemotherapy.","With a spontaneous nosebleed, ask about anticoagulants: they affect the cause and how fast it stops. Antidotes: protamine sulfate for IV heparin, vitamin K for warfarin.","Anterior (most common)","Upright, leaning forward (leaning back sends blood down the throat: aspiration risk). Keep calm.","Pinch the soft tissue just below the bony prominence; pinching the bone won't stop it. Breathe through the mouth.","Lateral pressure with ice to vasoconstrict; nasal tampon or packing if needed.","Discourage nose blowing.","Posterior (less common, more serious)","An emergency; usually needs hospitalization (and often a specialist). Hard to stop because you can't pinch it.","Balloon catheter (tamponade) or posterior packing; can impede breathing.","Humidification, oxygenation, bed rest, pain control, oral care.","Teaching: saline spray, humidification, avoid aspirin and NSAIDs, avoid strenuous activity.","Live-lecture priority: for a patient with nasal packing, the priority assessment is the oxygen saturation: packing can obstruct the airway. Pain and dressing drainage matter, but think ABCs first.","Acute Pharyngitis","Viral — The most common; 9 times out of 10 a sore throat is viral. — Symptomatic care: soothing treatments, analgesics, antipyretics. No antibiotics.","Bacterial (streptococcal, group A strep) — Only about 1 in 10 adults. Sudden sore throat, swollen red tender tonsils, lymphadenopathy, fever. Hard to tell from viral by symptoms, so a rapid strep test is done. — Requires antibiotics. Not ev…","Fungal (thrush) — Candida albicans; for instance in patients on antibiotics. — Nystatin (yellow solution) swish and spit or swish and swallow.","Obstructive Sleep Apnea (OSA)","What and who","Repetitive collapse of the upper airway during sleep (the mandible drops back and blocks it). Apnea = breathing stops; hypopnea = it slows considerably.","Primary risk factors: increased age, male, obesity (large neck circumference), and nasopharyngeal structural abnormalities. Smoking is a less established risk factor.","Signs: waking tired, loud snoring and noises (often reported by the partner), morning headaches, daytime sleepiness; witnessed apnea on the hospital monitor.","Complications: motor vehicle crashes, neuropsychiatric dysfunction (lost REM sleep), pulmonary pressure, hypertension, heart failure, metabolic syndrome.","Gold standard diagnosis: polysomnography (a sleep study).","Treatment","Weight reduction, exercise; avoid alcohol and smoking; sleep on the side, not the back; sleep hygiene (routine, off the phone and TV).","Oral appliances (keep the mandible pulled up) are preferred over CPAP for mild to moderate cases.","Surgery: tissue removal, jaw repositioning, or (in serious cases) a trach.","The big problem is adherence: an estimated 20 to 40% don't use their CPAP. If a CPAP patient isn't rested, ask whether they are actually using it.","Pressure — One continuous setting the whole time — Two settings: higher on inhalation, lower on exhalation (easier to push air out)","Main use — The mainstay for OSA; spontaneous breathing, keeps the airway open — COPD (trouble exhaling), neuromuscular disease, chest wall deformities, OSA","Oxygen — No supplemental O2 at home (sometimes added in the hospital) — Not stated","Cost and complexity — Simplest, works well, cheap — Fancier, more expensive; often a last step to prevent intubation","Trach Review","Parts","Flange (faceplate on the outside), outer cannula, inner cannula (inside the outer cannula; removed for cleaning), and the obturator (only for reinserting a trach that came out).","Shiley (white plastic): has a cuff; disposable inner cannula you throw away at trach care; every fresh post-op trach starts as a Shiley.","Jackson (metal): no cuff; the inner cannula is cleaned with a brush; used for long-term or permanent trachs.","The cuff and pilot balloon go together: inflated pilot balloon = inflated cuff. The cuff is inflated when the patient is on a ventilator (so all air goes from the vent to the patient) and typically deflated off the vent. A speaking valve re…","An oral ET tube is used only about a week to 10 days before a trach is placed (FYI). Trachs can be temporary (downsized, then removed) or permanent.","Trach suctioning and care are fair game on the exam.","The trach comes out: the obturator is always taped at the bedside, with a spare trach and a bag-valve device with a trach adapter. Take out the inner cannula, put the obturator in the outer cannula, slightly extend the neck, guide it into t…","Preventing thick secretions (live-lecture question)","The question asks about preventing thickening: changing the inner cannula treats it but doesn't prevent it; low Fowler's is too low for the airway; vital signs don't prevent it. Read what the question is actually asking before you look at t…","Head & Neck Cancer","Who and why it is devastating","Tobacco causes 85%; more men; usually over 50. Under 50, often linked to HPV.","Symptoms (hoarseness, dysphagia, a neck mass) come late; very few early symptoms means high mortality. Most have locally advanced disease at diagnosis.","Surgery is first-line (then radiation and chemo): vocal cord stripping, laryngectomy (removal of the voice box), tracheostomy, lymph node removal, neck dissection.","Huge impact on body image (and it's the airway).","After a laryngectomy","A permanent tracheostomy; the natural voice is lost.","Air no longer enters through the nose or mouth, only the stoma; sputum comes out the stoma.","The patient can eventually eat and drink (the esophagus is intact), but has tube feeding at first (no swallowing for about 10 days to 2 weeks while swelling settles).","Code on a laryngectomy patient: do not bag the mouth and nose. Bag the stoma, using a pediatric face mask that fits over it.","Artificial larynx (electrolarynx) — Probably the most common; held against the throat to vibrate. Usable right after surgery, easy, well understood (sounds a bit robotic).","Tracheoesophageal voice restoration — A valve placed in the area lets the patient talk.","Esophageal speech — The oldest; hands-free with no device, but hard to learn: air is sucked into the esophagus and \"burped\" out to form words.","Post-op care (including radical neck dissection)","Airway is number one; trach collar with O2 and humidification.","Cough regularly; suction (Yankauer and deep trach suctioning). A mucus plug can cause a code.","Blood-tinged sputum can be expected for the first couple of days.","Pulmonary toilet, stoma care, pain control (enough for good pulmonary toilet); Jackson-Pratt drain.","Often malnourished before surgery: tube feedings early. Physical and speech therapy; psychosocial support.","Pneumonia","An acute infection of the lung parenchyma (the functional unit, where the alveoli exchange O2 and CO2). Can be bacterial, viral, or fungal.","Risk factors","Older age; altered level of consciousness (weak cough, aspiration risk); can't expectorate secretions.","Immobility or bed rest; debilitating illness; malnutrition; smoking.","Tracheal intubation (on a ventilator in the ICU).","Picture it: an older man from a nursing home after a stroke, low albumin, a smoker.","Classifications","CAP (community-acquired): not hospitalized or living in long-term care within 14 days of symptoms.","HAP (hospital-acquired): begins 48 hours or more after admission, not present on admission; non-intubated patients.","VAP (ventilator-associated): on a ventilator in the ICU.","Viral — The most common; may be mild and self-limiting (usually resolves in 3 to 4 days) but can be life-threatening. Cough is scanty or non-productive.","Bacterial — Usually more serious; sometimes hospitalized. Cough is typically productive, possibly purulent.","Aspiration — Material from the mouth or stomach enters the trachea and lungs. Risk: decreased LOC, dysphagia (thin liquids), NG and feeding tubes (Dobhoff tubes are placed post-pyloric). Can be silent (no vomiting or coughing).","Opportunistic — Immunocompromised patients.","Manifestations and diagnosis","Often follows a simple respiratory infection (a \"post-influenza pneumonia\"), then fever, chills, cough, malaise, painful deep breaths and coughing, dyspnea, fatigue, myalgia; crackles.","In the elderly, a new onset of confusion can be one of the first signs of any infection.","Severe cases: respiratory distress and failure (patients with comorbidities tolerate it worst).","Chest X-ray: infiltrates (haziness where it should be clear).","CBC with diff: leukocytosis (high WBC). An acute bacterial infection may show a shift to the left: more neutrophils, including immature neutrophils released early.","Sputum C&S identifies the bacteria and the antibiotic; a viral infection grows no bacteria (and gets no antibiotics).","Don't mix these up (the instructor says exam options will test it): leukocytosis = increased WBCs; leukopenia = decreased WBCs.","Prevention","Pneumococcal vaccine for those at risk or of age; flu vaccine.","Don't smoke; balanced diet.","In the hospital: know who is at risk; incentive spirometer, turn-cough-deep breathe, ambulate.","Standard precautions; hand hygiene.","Care","Trend vital signs and pulse ox; listen to lungs at the start of the shift for a baseline.","Oxygen as needed to keep the sat above 95%.","Pulmonary toilet; the incentive spirometer (make sure they use it and document it). Chest physiotherapy.","Increase fluids to thin secretions (unless contraindicated, such as severe heart failure).","Ambulation balanced with energy conservation (cluster care).","Antibiotics if bacterial; analgesics; antipyretics.","Live-lecture question: what to do for a pneumonia patient","Appropriate: acetaminophen for fever (a higher temperature raises the cells' oxygen demand), notify the provider, check urine output (hydration), and encourage fluids.","Not appropriate: complete bed rest (it worsens pneumonia).","COPD","Obstructive pulmonary disease is an umbrella term for asthma, chronic bronchitis, and emphysema. Asthma is taught in peds, not med surg. COPD usually means emphysema and/or chronic bronchitis. It is preventable and treatable.","Emphysema","Alveolar damage: alveoli overinflated, stretched, destroyed.","Impaired gas exchange at the parenchyma.","Barrel chest, dyspnea, but not the daily sputum of bronchitis.","Chronic bronchitis","Inflammation with excessive mucus in the bronchi.","Coughing something up every single day.","Findings","The problem is getting air out, not in. Diagnosis: history, symptoms, spirometry.","Barrel chest: AP diameter closer to 1:1 instead of the normal 2:1 (on X-ray: widened AP diameter, flattened diaphragm).","Decreased or diminished breath sounds; wheezes, rhonchi, crackles (especially in an exacerbation); prolonged expiration.","Advanced disease: pursed-lip breathing (creates positive pressure to push air out; most patients already do it), neck vein distension and peripheral edema (from pulmonary hypertension backing things up), and cachexia (they burn so many calo…","Nursing problems: activity intolerance (sat may drop talking or eating), ineffective breathing pattern (usually too fast), ineffective airway clearance, impaired gas exchange, anxiety, poor nutrition.","Nursing implications","Smoking cessation: the single most effective and cost-effective intervention (Kentucky has many smokers).","Flu and other vaccines; catch respiratory infections early.","Inhaler therapy is the mainstay of self-management; adherence is critical (non-adherent patients end up with exacerbations). Long-acting beta agonists and long-acting muscarinic antagonists bronchodilate; inhaled steroids reduce inflammatio…","Home or hospital O2 with a lower sat target than the average patient.","Tripod position and pursed-lip breathing (they usually already do both); huff coughing; energy conservation and relaxation; high calorie needs; psychosocial support.","COPD Exacerbation","When they come into the hospital","A flare-up: worsening dyspnea, cough, or sputum beyond their usual day-to-day variation, needing a change in treatment. The most common cause is a respiratory infection (often after an upper respiratory infection).","Mainstay: oxygen for hypoxemia. Sat goal: 88 to 92%. 99 to 100% is too high.","Non-invasive positive pressure (BiPAP) when hypercapnic (rising CO2).","Bronchodilators (MDI at home; nebulizer in the hospital); chest physiotherapy; antibiotics if bacterial.","CO2 narcosis, and never withhold oxygen. A long-term CO2 retainer's drive to breathe is low oxygen (ours is high CO2). Too much oxygen (for example 6 L with a sat of 99%) can take that drive away. Only an ABG proves someone is a CO2 retaine…","Tuberculosis","Caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis. Resistant to isoniazid (INH) and rifampin = multi-drug resistant TB (MDR-TB). Risk: poor, underserved, and minority populations; IV drug users; poor sanitation; crowded living; immunosuppression.","Primary — Inhaled; the immune response encapsulates the organism. Usually asymptomatic. — No","Latent — A persistent immune response with no clinical signs. Positive skin or blood test; chest X-ray may or may not show a Ghon nodule. — No","Active (reactivated) — Develops in about 5 to 10% of latent cases, possibly years later; higher risk in older, immunosuppressed people. Sputum positive for AFB. Fever, night sweats, weight loss, productive cough, hemoptysis for longer than …","Reading the TB Skin Test","Measure induration (the raised area), not redness. Redness that isn't raised is not positive. The instructor wants you familiar with these cut-offs.","15 mm or more — Healthy people with no risk factors.","10 mm or more — Immigrants, drug users, nursing home residents, young children.","5 mm or more — The immunosuppressed: HIV, recent contact with TB, organ transplant.","After a positive screen","Positive skin test or IGRA, then a chest X-ray. Clear chest X-ray (no Ghon nodule) = latent TB. Nodule present = more testing (sputum for AFB) for active TB.","TB Treatment & Care","Drug therapy","The mainstay; months (6 or more) to years. Strict adherence is essential. Learn it as \"isoniazid,\" not just INH.","Directly observed therapy (DOT): supervised dosing to increase adherence and decrease MDR-TB, and to assess adverse effects.","Isoniazid (INH) — Hepatotoxicity (with acetaminophen, one of the two hepatotoxic drugs to know in med surg); peripheral neuropathy.","Rifampin — Red-orange secretions (bright red urine that isn't hematuria).","Ethambutol — Visual acuity changes; trouble telling colors apart.","In the hospital","Airborne precautions in a negative pressure room (air pushed outside, not to other rooms).","A fitted HEPA mask (fit testing).","At home","Preventive drugs for high-risk contacts (a spouse, for example); test contacts.","Cover mouth and nose when coughing; mask in crowds.","Sputum for AFB every couple of weeks: three negative cultures typically means no longer infectious.","Lung Cancer","Presentation and diagnosis","One of the most prevalent cancers in Kentucky. About 10% are found incidentally on an X-ray for something else.","Few early symptoms; often masked by a smoker's chronic cough. Most common symptom: a persistent cough (productive); also hemoptysis, weight loss, chest pain.","Chest X-ray: a mass (consolidation) or infiltrate (haziness). CT or MRI for metastasis.","Sputum cytology: only 20 to 30 of 100 patients with lung cancer test positive.","The definitive diagnosis is a biopsy: fine needle aspirate, bronchoscopy, or VATS (provider preference).","Problems: airway clearance, ineffective breathing, anxiety, malnutrition, fatigue (space activities, allow rest), knowledge deficit.","Smoking cessation; anxiety reduction and rapport.","Surgery: pneumonectomy (whole lung), lobectomy, or wedge resection (surgeon preference); plus radiation and/or chemo.","After a pneumonectomy, no breath sounds on that side (sounds near midline may be referred)."]},{"s":"Part 2 — Hematology: Anemia, Polycythemia, Central Lines & Blood Products▾","a":"part-2-hematology-anemia-polycythemia-central-lines-blood-products","k":["Part 2 — Hematology: Anemia, Polycythemia, Central Lines & Blood Products▾","From Hematology: Anemia, Polycythemia, Central Lines & Blood Products. Med Surg I (NUR 334) review content. Built from the eight NUR 334 Week 2 voiceovers: hematology labs and diagnostics, hematologic problems (RBCs), the anemias, nursing c…","Hematology Labs & Diagnostics","The CBC","RBC count — Not talked about much clinically; the H&H is used far more.","Hemoglobin and hematocrit (H&H, \"the crit\") — See the values to memorize below.","WBC count — Normal 5,000 to 10,000. A differential breaks it into neutrophils, lymphocytes, monocytes, eosinophils, and basophils as percentages that always add up to 100%.","Platelet count — Normal starts at 150,000 (the upper end \"depending on where you look\").","\"Never Let Monkeys Eat Bananas\" (neutrophils, lymphocytes, monocytes, eosinophils, basophils), carried forward from 325. The neutrophil is the most plentiful WBC and the first to arrive at an acute bacterial infection. Mature neutrophils ar…","Hemoglobin & Hematocrit","Commit these to memory \"forever\" (the instructor's words): Hemoglobin women 12 to 16, men 14 to 18. Hematocrit women 37 to 47%, men 42 to 52%.","Definitions","Hemoglobin: the iron-containing pigment of the RBC.","Hematocrit: the percentage of total blood volume made up of RBCs. The hematocrit is roughly three times the hemoglobin (a hemoglobin around 12 goes with a crit of 36).","A low crit is anemia (anemia doesn't cause it); the cause might be bleeding, bone marrow failure, dietary deficiency, renal disease. Too many RBCs = polycythemia.","Trend, don't read stand-alone values: a post-op H&H of 9.2 and 28.1 that was lower yesterday is trending up; keep an eye on it.","False lows and false highs","Fluid retention (heart failure, kidney failure): extra plasma lowers the percentage = a false low hematocrit, not true anemia.","Dehydration: less plasma raises the percentage = a false high hematocrit, not true polycythemia.","The bus analogy again: RBC = bus, hemoglobin = seats, iron = what the seats are made of, oxygen = passengers. O2 sat = how full the buses are (we want 95% or higher). Hematocrit = how much of the highway is buses.","RBC Morphology","Color, size, and shape; it helps the provider find the cause of an anemia. You don't need to memorize the MCV or MCHC numbers, just the terms.","MCV — Size (V = volume) — Normocytic (normal), microcytic (small, e.g. iron deficiency), macrocytic (large, e.g. the megaloblastic B12 and folic acid deficiencies; large but fragile)","MCHC — How concentrated the RBC is with hemoglobin — Normochromic (normal), hypochromic (low, pale)","Iron Studies & Other Anemia Tests","Serum iron — Low in iron deficiency anemia. About 70% of iron is in the hemoglobin of RBCs; the rest is stored as ferritin and bound to transferrin.","Serum transferrin — The protein that transports iron; decreased in iron deficiency anemia.","Serum ferritin — The protein that stores iron. Decreased ferritin is the most sensitive test for iron deficiency anemia.","Intrinsic factor antibodies — Intrinsic factor (made by the stomach's parietal cells) is needed to absorb B12. Autoimmune antibodies against it = pernicious anemia, the most common cause of B12 deficiency.","Serum B12 and folate — Assess the presence of each.","Stool for guaiac (occult blood) — For anemia of unknown origin; the GI tract (a GI bleed) is suspected first. Not needed if you can see frank blood.","EGD and colonoscopy — Scope for a bleed: esophagogastroduodenoscopy (top), colonoscopy (bottom); usually in the endoscopy suite.","Coombs test — Differentiates types of hemolytic anemia. Direct: suspected incompatible blood was hung. Indirect: screening before a transfusion (the \"type and screen\").","Bone marrow biopsy — For low RBCs, platelets, and WBCs without a known cause; the patient with aplastic anemia. Red marrow, usually the iliac crest. Informed consent, time-out, premedicate; gentle pressure after and assess the site for blee…","Transferrin transports; ferritin stores. These iron tests are for anemia of unknown origin, not for someone who bled in surgery yesterday.","Melena vs iron stools","Melena: dark, tarry, very malodorous, sticky stool from digested blood. Significant.","Stool on iron is also black, but that is expected. Teach the difference: blood is very malodorous and tacky.","Anemia","Blood cell terms","Leukopenia = low WBCs; neutropenia = low neutrophils (big trouble: the first responders are gone); leukocytosis = too many WBCs (infection).","Thrombocytopenia = low platelets (thrombocytes). Erythrocytes = RBCs. \"-penia\" = low or deficient.","Pancytopenia = low RBCs, WBCs, and platelets (aplastic anemia).","Causes of Anemia","Decreased RBC production — Not enough hemoglobin synthesis (iron deficiency); impaired DNA synthesis (B12 or folic acid: the megaloblastic anemias); decreased RBC precursors (aplastic anemia, chemotherapy, chronic disease). Chronic kidney f…","Blood loss — Acute (ruptured spleen, ruptured aneurysm, surgery): no time to compensate; quick cardiac instability and a rising heart rate. Chronic (heavy menstrual periods, GI bleeding such as a peptic ulcer or ulcerative colitis): insidio…","Increased RBC destruction (hemolysis) — Sickle cell anemia (peds); a hemolytic transfusion reaction from incompatible blood.","Post-op patients commonly come back with a low H&H; that is expected. GI bleeding is always on the list for anemia of unknown origin.","Severity & Why the Symptoms Happen","Mild — 10 to 14 — Maybe tired with exercise.","Moderate — 6 to 10 — Cardiopulmonary symptoms, possibly even at rest.","Severe — Less than 6 — Symptoms at rest; can be life-threatening with comorbidities. \"A hemoglobin of 6 should make your heart rate go up as the nurse.\"","Fatigue — Not enough oxygen to the muscles, so not enough energy.","Pallor (palms, nail beds, conjunctiva) — Peripheral vasoconstriction sends blood to the vital organs (heart, lungs).","Tachycardia, thumping in the chest — Cardiac output rises to compensate.","Bone pain (sternum, iliac crest) — Erythropoietin rises and the marrow works to make more RBCs.","Angina, MI, worsening heart failure — Cardiac muscle hypoxia (severe anemia, life-threatening).","Hypoxia, dyspnea, increased respiratory rate — Overall poor oxygenation.","Write this down: CO = HR × SV. Cardiac output (blood ejected from the left ventricle per minute) = heart rate × stroke volume (blood ejected per beat). On med surg you measure it with vital signs: blood pressure is an indirect measure of ca…","The Types of Anemia","Iron deficiency — Poor intake (anorexia, can't afford food), poor gut absorption, blood loss (menstruation), hemolysis. — Chronic may be asymptomatic. Most common symptom: pallor (lost line of demarcation in the conjunctiva). Glossitis (red…","B12 (cobalamin) deficiency — Pernicious anemia (autoimmune; no intrinsic factor; the most common cause); bowel surgery or malabsorption; strict vegans; alcohol abuse; gastrectomy (lost parietal cells, not autoimmune). — Insidious GI signs (…","Folate deficiency — Usually decreased intake: pregnancy, alcohol abuse. — Looks a lot like B12 but without the neurologic problems; assess neuro signs to tell them apart. — Stated as \"microcytic normochromic, just like the other megaloblast…","Aplastic anemia — Usually idiopathic; sometimes thought autoimmune. — Pancytopenia: anemic (fatigue), leukopenic/neutropenic (infection risk), thrombocytopenic (bleeding risk). A whole different ballgame; diagnosed with a bone marrow biopsy…","Lecture oddity, left as stated: the folate slide is read as \"microcytic normochromic, just like the other megaloblastic anemias,\" while earlier the same lectures call the megaloblastic (B12 and folic acid) anemias macrocytic.","Nursing Care of the Anemic Patient","Nursing problems","Activity intolerance (probably the most common): fine at rest, but heart rate up, palpitations, anxiety, and short of breath with activity. Fatigue is being tired even lying in bed.","Nutrition (not taking in or not absorbing nutrients).","Aplastic anemia: risk for bleeding (thrombocytopenia), infection (neutropenia), and fatigue (anemia), all in one patient.","Ongoing care","Trend labs; watch for hypoxemia; vital signs and O2 sat; neuro checks (B12).","Oxygen for the very anemic patient (fewer buses need all the help they can get).","Rest periods.","Keep them warm, but no heating pads (paresthesia risk).","Possible PRBC transfusion.","Treat the cause: nutrition, stop alcohol, supplements, find and stop the bleeding.","Medications","Iron (ferrous sulfate) — PO is inexpensive and easy; once daily is acceptable (newer evidence). Absorbed in the duodenum; best in an acidic environment, so before meals, but take with food for the first couple of weeks if it causes nausea (…","Vitamin B12 (cobalamin, cyanocobalamin) — PO, IM, subcutaneous, or intranasal. IM is preferred with severe neurologic problems. With some intrinsic factor present, PO megadoses (typically 1,000 mcg, of which only 2.4 mcg is absorbed) work a…","Folic acid — By mouth; no real adverse effects; a big vitamin in pregnancy.","Epoetin (synthetic erythropoietin) — For chronic kidney disease and cancer patients; often given with iron (\"the buses and the wheels\"). IV or subcutaneous about three times a week. Black box warnings; discontinued when the hemoglobin is ab…","Food teaching (a favorite select-all-that-apply topic)","Iron-rich foods; most books say give vitamin C with iron (the literature is mixed).","B12 is only in animal products: strict vegans need supplements.","Folic acid: green leafy vegetables, legumes, grains, orange juice, nuts.","Polycythemia","An abnormally high H&H and RBC count. Three classifications.","Relative — Decreased plasma volume or dehydration: the false high hematocrit; not a true increase in RBCs.","Primary (polycythemia vera) — A genetic change during the person's lifetime. Not preventable; never goes away.","Secondary — Compensation for tissue hypoxia: COPD (the most common cause overall) or a lifetime at high altitude. \"My body compensates by making more buses.\"","Manifestations","Thicker, more blood: hypertension, headache, trouble concentrating.","Ruddy (dusky red) complexion; possible cyanosis.","Clots or hemorrhage: angina, MI, TIA, stroke. The most common serious acute complication is a stroke.","Hypermetabolic: night sweats, weight loss.","Pruritus (itching) worsened by a hot bath is classic; pain in the fingers and toes.","Collaborative care","Phlebotomy is the mainstay for chronic polycythemia: 300 to 500 mL every other day at first, eventually every few months.","Goal: hematocrit below 42 in women, below 45 in men.","Hydration (the blood is so viscous).","Anticoagulants (clot risk); mobility matters but clot risk is high.","Antihistamines (diphenhydramine) for serious itching.","Ongoing evaluation and support.","Central Venous Catheters","Why a central line","TPN: very hypertonic (10 to 20% dextrose instead of 5%); peripheral veins can't handle it.","Chemotherapy: often vesicant (serious tissue damage if it infiltrates) and long-term.","Long-term antibiotics (for example, weeks for endocarditis).","Loss of peripheral access.","Non-tunneled (\"deep line\") — Provider or acute care NP; subclavian or internal jugular (IJ); tip usually in the superior vena cava. — Up to 5 ports (usually 2 or 3); 7 to 10 inches; multiple lumens can run incompatible infusions. Chest X-ra…","Tunneled (Hickman, Groshong, Broviac) — Surgeon, in the OR; tunneled through subcutaneous tissue, exits lower than it enters. — Stable; long-term frequent infusions; cancer floor and dialysis access. No needle sticks, but a prolonged break …","Implanted port (Port-a-Cath, power port) — Surgeon, in the OR; under the skin of the chest wall. — IV therapy up to a year or more; no external tubing when not accessed (less body-image impact). Accessed with a Huber needle (up to about 2,0…","PICC (peripherally inserted central catheter) — Specially trained PICC nurses at the bedside; usually the antecubital area, threaded so the tip lies in the superior vena cava. — A central line (the tip location decides it). Not tunneled; ab…","Complications of central lines","Bleeding at the site, air embolus, pneumothorax.","CLABSI (central line-associated bloodstream infection): a huge share of hospital-acquired infections; can cause sepsis and death.","CHG-impregnated dressings help prevent CLABSI; a StatLock keeps the catheter from moving (wiggling enlarges the insertion hole).","Removing a non-tunneled CVC or PICC (RN)","Check the INR first (especially on anticoagulants).","Patient supine or Trendelenburg (PICC: lying down, hand below heart level).","Valsalva (bear down or hum) during removal so air isn't sucked into the vessel.","PICC: withdraw slowly, inch by inch (too fast can spasm the vein).","Pressure 3 to 5 minutes; occlusive dressing.","Measure the catheter against the charted length so nothing is left in.","Nursing students at UK HealthCare may not provide direct CVC care (CLABSI bundle): no accessing or flushing ports, no pushing through a central line, no hanging primary fluids, no dressing changes. The one exception: hanging a secondary med…","Blood Products","Blood Types","Compatibility","Two antigen sets: ABO (A, B, both, or neither) and Rh (have it or don't).","You can only receive blood with antigens you already have; a foreign antigen causes a hemolytic reaction.","O negative = universal donor (no A, B, or Rh). AB positive = universal recipient.","Examples: B positive can receive O negative, O positive, B negative, B positive. A negative can receive only O negative and A negative.","You don't have to memorize the chart, but you must understand it well enough to answer, for example, a select-all-that-apply on which types an AB negative patient can receive. (Most common types, O positive and A positive, are not on the te…","When PRBCs Are Given","Less than 6 — Transfusion recommended except in extenuating circumstances.","6 to 7 — Transfusion probably given; is the patient symptomatic (low BP, compensating high heart rate)?","7 to 8 — Appropriate for patients undergoing surgery or with heart disease.","8 to 10 — Usually not, unless serious comorbidities: oncology, risk for MI or ACS, active bleeding.","Greater than 10 — Usually not, except in exceptional cases.","Before & During a Transfusion","Know the H&H and confirm the order — An informed nurse checks the hemoglobin when PRBCs are ordered.","Type and crossmatch within the last 72 hours — Type = their blood type; crossmatch = mixing a sample of the unit with their blood to check for hemolysis (the indirect Coombs). Redone every 72 hours.","Positive ID, signed consent, teaching — Premedicate if ordered (acetaminophen for fever risk, diphenhydramine for itching risk).","IV access: 20 gauge or larger (18 ideal) — Lewis says 22; the instructor doesn't accept 22 (\"we're gonna say 20 or higher\"). Smaller gauges can break down RBCs (hemolysis).","Blood (Y) tubing with a filter; normal saline only — Never dextrose or lactated Ringer's (hemolysis). Prime with saline, then blood; flush with saline at the end to get every last drop.","Have everything ready before getting the blood — Once it's on the floor you have 30 minutes to start it; if you can't, return it to the blood bank (never the med fridge).","Final ID check at the bedside — Textbook: two RNs (UK uses barcode scanning as the second check).","Stay with the patient the first 15 to 30 minutes — Cannot be delegated (and a student can't be the one to stay). Teach them to report itching, chills, shortness of breath, back pain, or feeling \"weird\"; a cold IV site is expected.","Vital signs at 15 minutes, then hourly, and at the end — If tolerated, increase the rate; aim for about 2 hours (250 to 350 mL per unit of PRBCs).","Never let it hang more than 4 hours — Bacterial proliferation. If not finished by 4 hours: take it down, document, tell the provider.","Know the post-transfusion orders — Repeat H&H? A diuretic (furosemide) between units for fluid-overload risk? A second unit?","The two time limits to commit to memory: 30 minutes to start once the blood reaches the floor, and 4 hours maximum hang time.","Other Blood Products","Packed RBCs (PRBCs) — The severely anemic (\"only the buses\") — No plasma, WBCs, or platelets; 250 to 350 mL; type and crossmatch required.","Platelets — Thrombocytopenia (often chemo patients); usually when the count is below 20,000 — 30 to 60 mL per bag (sometimes a \"six pack\"); stored at room temperature, good 1 to 5 days; agitate the bag (they clump); give as fast as tolerate…","Fresh frozen plasma (FFP) — Bleeding risk from lack of clotting factors (liver disease, hemophilia, von Willebrand's); rarely as a volume expander — About 250 mL; rich in clotting factors (prothrombin); ABO compatibility required; stored up…","Albumin — Low albumin (for example, liver patients with fluid shifted into the third space) — A colloid with \"pulling power\" that keeps or pulls fluid into the intravascular space. 5% equals plasma; 25% is hypertonic. Compatibility not a fa…","Transfusion Reactions","Suspect a reaction? Stop the transfusion first. Then hang a new bag and tubing of normal saline at the same IV site (don't just switch to the saline on the blood tubing; that tubing is full of blood). Keep the line open, assess and take vit…","Acute hemolytic (the most life-threatening) — Wrong (incompatible) blood. Immediate: facial flushing, fever, headache, low back pain (kidneys), hematuria, dyspnea, dropping BP, cardiac arrest. — Support the BP (it is shock; colloids such as…","Febrile non-hemolytic (the vast majority of fevers) — A gradual temperature rise (not immediate); no back pain, no hematuria, no hemodynamic compromise. — Notify the provider; probably acetaminophen and restart. — Leukocyte-reduced products…","Mild allergic (the most common type) — Itching, flushing, urticaria (hives). Sensitization to donor WBCs. — Stop, notify; diphenhydramine or a steroid; usually restart slowly. — Antihistamines and steroids beforehand; washed RBCs.","Severe allergic (anaphylactic) — Anxiety, urticaria, bronchospasm, respiratory and cardiovascular compromise, low BP, possible cardiac arrest. — Treat like a code: CPR, oxygen, epinephrine, antihistamines, steroids, maybe a beta-2 agonist. …","Bacterial sepsis — Rapid chills and high fever, vomiting, diarrhea, marked hypotension. — Stop and return the blood; cultures; notify; antibiotics, IV fluids, vasopressors (likely ICU). — Infuse within 4 hours; aseptic technique.","TACO (transfusion-associated circulatory overload) — Often an older patient with heart failure, blood given too fast: lung congestion, crackles at the bases, dyspnea, increased respirations, headache, distended neck veins. — The only one wh…","Leukocyte-reduced products","Leukocytes collected with whole blood can cause febrile non-hemolytic and allergic reactions.","Pre-storage leuko-reduction lowers that risk; most blood at UK is leukocyte-reduced.","Autologous donation vs autotransfusion","Autologous donation: donating your own blood before a planned surgery. Less common now (expensive, often wasted).","Autotransfusion: collecting blood lost during surgery (for example, open-heart) and giving it back. No reaction risk; it's their blood."]},{"s":"Part 3 — Perioperative Care, Fluids & Electrolytes▾","a":"part-3-perioperative-care-fluids-electrolytes","k":["Part 3 — Perioperative Care, Fluids & Electrolytes▾","From Perioperative Care, Fluids & Electrolytes. Med Surg I (NUR 334) review content. Built from the eleven NUR 334 Week 3 voiceovers: perioperative overview and pre-op (two parts), intra-op, post-op (two parts), fluid volume imbalances, and…","Perioperative Overview","Pre-op — The anesthesiologist assesses and introduces; care is planned with the patient. The surgical site is marked (preventing wrong-site surgery). Very anxiety-producing for the patient.","Intra-op — From being wheeled into the OR until transfer to the PACU. Nursing roles: scrub nurse, circulating nurse, RN first assist.","Post-op — The PACU: airway first, recovery from general anesthesia, pain management, close vital signs (patients can be hemodynamically unstable and change quickly). Then back to the ICU, step-down, or med surg unit.","Purposes and settings","Purposes: diagnosis, cure, palliation (treating symptoms), prevention, exploration, cosmetic improvement.","Ambulatory: home the same day. Same-day admit: surgery in the morning, admitted overnight (usually one night). Inpatient: in the hospital before and after.","Timing","Elective: not urgent; improves quality of life (cataracts). Low risk, but not no risk.","Semi-elective: more time-sensitive, but not needed within 24 hours (gallstones, fibroid bleeding).","Urgent: within 24 hours of diagnosis (hip fracture, an appendix about to rupture).","Emergency: can't be delayed, ideally within 2 hours (unstable GI bleed, subdural hematoma).","Pre-op","The Pre-op History","Steps in the interview","Review what is documented before the interview and just double-check it (asking the same questions repeatedly is annoying). Can happen in advance (a phone call) or on the day.","Confirm understanding of the procedure (teach-back: \"tell me about the procedure you're going to have\").","Previous surgeries and anesthetics; family history of anesthesia problems (malignant hyperthermia is familial).","All medications: prescription, over-the-counter, herbals.","Allergies and intolerances, including latex (see below).","Illicit drug use: not optional to ask, even if sensitive.","Tobacco: quitting 6 weeks before surgery is worth it (smoking compromises oxygen binding); quitting any time before is better than not at all.","Possibility of pregnancy.","End with: \"Is there anything else you'd like to share with me?\"","Latex allergy clues: allergy to kiwis, bananas, avocados, chestnuts, or a poinsettia plant; daily latex exposure; past reactions to balloons or condoms; a history of allergies and asthma. Latex allergy can be anaphylactic.","Review of Systems","Respiratory — Risk of atelectasis, infection, prolonged mechanical ventilation (a heavy smoker with COPD is harder to wean), bronchospasm.","Cardiac — Don't increase myocardial oxygen demand.","Hearing, vision, cognition — Know the baseline: is post-op confusion their baseline or the anesthesia?","Kidneys — Creatinine for kidney function.","Pregnancy — Is or has been pregnant.","Liver — Clotting and metabolism problems (affects how much medication).","Integument — Pressure ulcers, rashes, breakdown.","Musculoskeletal and mobility — Patients may be put in extreme positions during surgery.","Diabetes, thyroid — May need medication adjustments.","Steroids — Raise blood sugar, delay wound healing, impair skin integrity.","Fluids and electrolytes — Recent nausea, vomiting, diarrhea. Older patients have a narrow margin of fluid safety: a patient's age in a test question (an 82-year-old) usually plays into the answer.","Nutrition — Obesity; malnutrition shown in lab values.","The Joint Commission requires a history and physical (H&P) in the chart before the OR. Diagnostic results must be in the chart too. Teach the patient what they'll see and feel (a cold room, warm blankets, bright lights) and the process rule…","Informed Consent","The surgeon's job","Full disclosure: diagnosis, purpose, risks (even common non-serious and serious uncommon ones), alternatives, the risk of no treatment, who performs it, short- and long-term costs, hospital stay, pain, recovery, loss of function, scarring.","The patient must show comprehension; signing is voluntary.","The nurse's job","Witness the signature and act as the patient advocate.","You can answer follow-up questions, but if the patient expresses uncertainty about the procedure, explore it, and the next best action is to let the provider know they are having second thoughts.","Minors, unconscious, or mentally incompetent patients: a legal representative; an emancipated minor can consent. A true medical emergency can override consent when life is at stake.","Day of Surgery & the Pre-op Checklist","Fasting and checklist","No more blanket \"NPO after midnight.\" Typically liquids are held a few hours and food 8 or more hours, but follow the surgeon's orders (don't memorize the table; gut surgery may mean longer NPO).","Checklist: height and weight; baseline vital signs; H&P in the chart; signed consent; type and crossmatch; latest labs (H&H, coags/PT-INR); surgical site marked; ID and allergy bands; NPO since when; recent glucose; skin prepped (CHG wipes …","Valuables (dentures, hearing aids, wigs, glasses): send them with family, or secure them per protocol.","Intra-op","Circulating nurse — Not scrubbed, gowned, or gloved (but masked and capped); stays in the unsterile area, \"runs the show,\" records nursing care.","Scrub nurse — Scrubbed in, gowned and gloved; stays inside the sterile field.","RN first assist — A nursing role assisting the surgeon (at UK, often medical students assist).","CRNA — Certified registered nurse anesthetist; extensive schooling after nursing school plus ICU experience.","The four intra-op concepts: identification, safety, positioning, asepsis","At least two patient identifiers.","Site marking by the surgeon (and often the patient's initials) when there is a left and right or multiple structures (fingers, toes, spinal levels); as close to the incision as possible and visible after draping.","Time-out: the whole team stops before the incision to verify the patient, site, level, procedure, position, and implants or special equipment.","Positioning (supine, prone, lithotomy, side-lying) with secured extremities and padding: improper positioning over many hours can cause muscle strain, joint damage, pressure ulcers, and nerve damage.","A meticulously sterile field.","General — Inhaled and/or injected drugs: loss of all sensation and consciousness, with amnesia. Phases: pre-induction, induction, maintenance, emergence. Given by an anesthesiologist or CRNA.","Regional — Injected near nerves to block an area: nerve block, Bier block, spinal, epidural.","Local — Temporary loss of feeling by inhibiting nerve endings in one part of the body.","Monitored anesthesia care (MAC) — Formerly \"conscious sedation\": no inhaled agents; conscious but sedated (a colonoscopy, for example).","Intra-op nursing concerns: infection, positioning injury, and imbalanced body temperature (patients can become hypothermic in the OR).","Malignant Hyperthermia","Malignant hyperthermia: an inherited muscle disorder triggered by certain volatile anesthetics; rare, but high mortality if untreated (\"all hands on deck\"). The most common initial sign is an unexpected rise in end-tidal CO2 (hypercarbia), …","Not predictable","Familial tendency, but some patients have no family history.","Over 50% had uneventful general anesthetics before, so past uneventful surgeries don't rule it out.","Post-op","PACU basics","All orders must be rewritten after surgery; pre-op orders don't carry over. Read every new order.","PACU handoff: patient history, intra-op management, vital signs, lab results, estimated blood loss.","Initial PACU assessment: airway, breathing, circulation, pain, level of consciousness, voiding, bowel sounds, safety needs.","Possible problems: neuro/psych, GI (delayed gastric emptying, paralytic ileus), urinary (trouble voiding, infection with a Foley), respiratory (especially with lung history or smoking), cardiovascular, integument (dehiscence, hematoma, infe…","Respiratory","Problems","Hypoxia from shallow breathing, anesthesia, obesity, respiratory depression (especially first-time narcotics), laryngeal spasm.","Atelectasis and pneumonia.","Prevention","Pulmonary toilet; head of bed elevated; vital signs and O2 sat; suction if needed.","Effective coughing with splinting for abdominal or thoracic incisions (a pillow; CABG patients get a red heart pillow). Coughing after these surgeries is mandatory.","Early mobilization.","Around-the-clock pain dosing so they can cough and move.","Cardiovascular","Decreased cardiac output and VTE","Monitor vital signs (BP is the indirect measure of cardiac output), skin temperature, and color (peripheral perfusion). Trend and compare to baseline.","DVT (most often elderly, obese, immobilized) can become a PE; together called VTE. Prevent with leg and ankle exercises (dorsiflex, plantar flex, circles: they mimic walking's muscle pump), SCDs, early ambulation, phlebitis assessment.","TED hose must fit correctly (an ill-fitting pair caused a stage 2 pressure ulcer); SCDs are used more now.","BP less than 90 or greater than 160 — Notify the provider (rule of thumb).","Hypotension with a normal pulse and warm pink skin — Keep an eye on it. (A baseline of 90/60 may be normal for that patient if asymptomatic.)","Hypotension with a fast pulse or cold, clammy skin — Possible impending hypovolemic shock: immediate intervention.","Heart rate greater than 100 (for example 110) — Assess why: pain (morphine just given?), anxiety, or fluid volume deficit? Treatment depends on the cause. Normal is 60 to 100.","Narrowing pulse pressure (120/80 = 40, now 110/80 = 30) — Can indicate hemodynamic compromise.","Gradual BP trends over several readings; any rhythm change — Noteworthy; report rhythm changes.","Neurologic & Psychological","Emergence delirium and more","A restless patient always means think hypoxia first (do they need more oxygen?); also pain, anesthetic agents, the ET tube.","Post-op delirium is more common in the elderly. Post-op depression exists (lack of sleep, lack of home support, body image, pain): assess mood and allow time to talk.","Assess LOC, orientation, memory, following commands, grips, moving extremities, pupils; always compare to baseline (was that weak left grip there before?).","Pain & Temperature","Pain","Post-op pain is continuous and predictable: around-the-clock dosing, not waiting for the patient to ask.","Pain increases as anesthesia wears off. Opioids and non-opioids first for severe pain, supplemented by non-drug measures.","Watch every patient on opioids for respiratory depression. Document outcomes.","Temperature","Often cold after surgery: warm blankets; check the temperature.","First 48 hours: a low-grade temp is usually expected, the inflammatory response to surgery; too soon for infection. Above 100.4, think lung congestion or dehydration: pulmonary toilet and fluids.","After 48 hours: an elevated temp raises concern for infection (wound, lungs); check the WBC and neutrophils.","GI","Paralytic ileus and more","Abdominal discomfort is common; mobilization often helps most.","Paralytic ileus is most likely after bowel manipulation. After colon surgery, large intestine motility may take 2 to 7 days (small intestine sooner). Signs: distended, tender belly; absent or high-pitched bowel sounds.","No bowel sounds at 24 to 48 hours isn't necessarily an ileus; peristalsis may not have kicked in yet. Watch for bowel sounds and passing gas.","Ileus treatment: NG tube to low wall suction, NPO (\"don't feed a paralytic ileus\").","Treat nausea (antiemetic); advance the diet as tolerated (NPO, clear liquids, full liquids); document intake; ambulate; hydrate; monitor bowel sounds every shift; privacy.","Opioids cause constipation, and patients never develop tolerance to it: be proactive about stool softeners (ask the provider if none is ordered).","Urinary","Voiding and output","Get the Foley out as soon as possible; watch for UTI (in older patients, the first cue is often confusion).","Urine output greater than 30 mL/hour (rule of thumb). Urine output reflects renal perfusion: falling BP and cardiac output lower it.","Assess the urge to void, palpate the bladder, use the bladder scanner, normal voiding position, running water.","No void within 6 to 8 hours after surgery: get an order to catheterize (usually in-and-out).","Integument, Tubes & Drains","Wounds","Assess drainage (amount, color, odor, consistency) and erythema; purulent drainage is not expected.","Dehiscence: the wound opens. Evisceration: the insides come out. Full care in Week 5: Complications of Wounds","At risk: large or obese, older, coughing a lot.","Dressings clean, dry, intact; meticulous asepsis; nutrition is paramount (no wound heals without it).","Tubes, drains, catheters to know","Indwelling and suprapubic catheters; ostomies; G-tube (gastrostomy) to drainage; Tenckhoff (peritoneal dialysis) catheter; rectal tube/fecal management system; NG to low wall suction.","Penrose drain; closed suction drains (Hemovac, Jackson-Pratt); a T-tube; chest tube after pleural/lung surgery.","Know what each is for and monitor its output.","Fluid Volume Imbalances","Review from 325","Oncotic (colloid osmotic) pressure: albumin, the primary colloid, has \"pulling power\" that keeps fluid in the intravascular space. Low albumin: fluid leaks into the interstitium and can third-space.","Hydrostatic pressure: \"pushing power.\" In heart failure, backed-up fluid raises it and pushes fluid into the interstitium.","Third spacing: fluid collecting where it serves no purpose (pleural effusion, peritoneum, pericardium). Pulmonary edema = fluid in the lungs; peripheral edema = in the ankles.","Causes — Too many IV fluids (older patients with heart failure; TACO); heart failure (backup, hydrostatic pressure); kidney failure (not excreting urine); primary polydipsia (rare, psychogenic); SIADH, Cushing syndrome, steroids (fluid rete…","Neuro — Headache, confusion, lethargy; seizures and coma (more from the hyponatremia) — Restless, drowsy, lethargic, confused; seizures and coma (from the hypernatremia)","Cardiovascular — JVD (backup from the right side shows first in the jugular vein), bounding pulse, increased BP, S3 gallop (\"lub-dub-ta\"), increased CVP (not tested) — Tachycardia (compensating for low stroke volume; BP holds, then eventual…","Respiratory — Pulmonary edema: very short of breath, crackles (can be life-threatening) — Increased respiratory rate","Skin — Peripheral edema (0 to 4+ scale; in dependent areas like the ankles), pitting — Dry mucous membranes, cold clammy skin, sluggish turgor, dry skin","Urine and weight — Polyuria (only if the kidneys work); muscle spasms; water weight gain — Decreased urine output; weak, dizzy; water weight loss; thirst (blunted in the elderly)","Labs — Hemodiluted: false low BUN, sodium, hematocrit — Hemoconcentrated: false high BUN, sodium, hematocrit","Treatment — Restrict fluids (for example 1,500 mL: heart failure, CKD, liver failure); watch every IV fluid and rate — Mild: drink more. Severe: blood products (PRBCs) or balanced IV solutions (normal saline, lactated Ringer's)","Nursing management","Daily weights are the key, most reliable measure: same scale, same clothing, same time of day. I&O is less reliable (urine not saved, unreported drinks).","Serum and urine osmolality (particles per solution: how concentrated).","Trend vital signs; respiratory status (pulmonary edema brings patients in); safety with orthostatic hypotension (assist when up); skin care (dry skin or pitting edema both risk breakdown).","Delegation to a UAP (NCLEX loves these): daily weights, vital signs, I&O (abnormal numbers come back to you to interpret), frequent oral care (helps on a fluid restriction), skin care and repositioning, elevating edematous extremities (prom…","Electrolytes","The two ranges to commit to memory: sodium 135 to 145 and potassium 3.5 to 5. You don't need to memorize calcium, magnesium, or phosphorus numbers; a question will imply whether they are high or low.","Sodium","The major extracellular cation. \"Salt sucks, and water follows salt\": sodium governs osmolality (most of the body's particles per solution). It also activates muscle and nerve cells.","Causes — Excess sodium intake (hypertonic IV fluids; tube feeding without free water). Inadequate water intake (the elderly's lost thirst; anorexia; depression). Excess water loss (insensible loss, diarrhea, osmotic diuretics, DKA, diabetes…","Manifestations — Mental status changes (cells shrink): irritability, trouble concentrating, confusion, seizures, coma. With fluid deficit: postural hypotension, tachycardia, weakness. — Mental status changes (cells swell): confusion (unsafe…","Treatment — Treat the cause: water lost = add water back (hypotonic IV fluid); excess sodium = remove sodium. Monitor serum sodium and osmolality. — Mild: fluid restriction (concentrates the serum). Severe: 3% (hypertonic) saline, three tim…","Sodium out of balance? Think mental status changes. And correct sodium slowly (over a couple of days): fast fluid shifts in brain cells can be life-threatening. Potassium can be corrected faster than sodium.","The four usual reasons for a fluid restriction: heart failure, kidney failure (end-stage CKD), hyponatremia, and severe liver disease.","Potassium","The major intracellular cation; regulates cell excitability and electrical status. Out of range? Think cardiac dysrhythmias (EKG changes, ventricular dysrhythmias): these patients go on telemetry.","Causes — IV fluids with KCl plus renal insufficiency; shifts out of the cell (acidosis, tissue breakdown, intense exercise); failure to eliminate (kidney disease with oliguria: the main reason seen in the hospital); adrenal insufficiency (A…","Manifestations — EKG changes and dysrhythmias; fatigue, irritability, muscle weakness, loss of tone, paresthesia, confusion. — EKG changes and dysrhythmias; muscle weakness, cramps, paresthesia. Hypokalemia precipitates digoxin toxicity (di…","Treatment — Have the provider stop potassium-containing IV fluids. Increase excretion: loop or thiazide diuretics (if the kidneys work), dialysis (anuric CKD), Veltassa or Kayexalate (take hours to days). Dangerously high (6.5 or 7): act no…","Potassium safety points","A patient on a loop or thiazide diuretic should usually have a potassium supplement ordered; if not, check that morning's potassium and ask. Never give a supplement without knowing the potassium.","IV fluids with potassium are labeled in red at UK (for example normal saline with 20 or 40 mEq KCl). Never bolus a potassium-containing bag: get plain 0.9% normal saline for the bolus.","Know high-potassium foods (select-all-that-apply favorites) to teach patients on potassium-losing diuretics, or to avoid when potassium is high.","Calcium","Mostly in bone (strength); needed for membrane potential and nerve excitability. Only 1% is in the cells: half protein-bound, half ionized (available).","Causes — Hyperparathyroidism (number one; the four parathyroids regulate serum calcium); less commonly cancers, including paraneoplastic secretion (for example, lung cancer). — Renal failure (number one); parathyroid deficiency or removal; …","Manifestations — \"Excess calcium is like a sedative\": reduced muscle and nerve excitability, confusion, psychosis, seizures, coma. — Tetany (excess nerve excitability, sustained muscle contraction); Chvostek's sign (tap at the mandible/chee…","Treatment — Mild: conservative (decrease calcium intake, weight-bearing, hydration). Severe: a bisphosphonate (pamidronate, the gold standard if from cancer), IV isotonic saline, calcitonin (short-term; increases renal calcium excretion). —…","Magnesium","Stabilizes cardiac and smooth muscle cells.","Causes — Renal failure (most common): a CKD patient taking Maalox for an upset stomach; too much IV magnesium sulfate (OB). — Malnutrition plus GI losses (nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, wound drainage); chronic alcohol abuse.","Manifestations — Everything slowed down: lethargy, drowsiness, muscle weakness, decreased DTRs. — Everything revved up (like hypocalcemia): cramps, tremors, possible seizures, vertigo, hyperactive DTRs, Chvostek's and Trousseau's, increased…","Treatment — Avoid magnesium-containing drugs (Maalox, Mylanta); limit dietary magnesium (green vegetables, nuts, bananas, oranges, peanut butter, chocolate); force fluids if the kidneys work, dialysis if not; IV calcium gluconate if symptom…","Phosphorus","Calcium and phosphorus are always inversely related: calcium up, phosphorus down; phosphorus up, calcium down.","Causes — Kidney failure (number one; it also causes low calcium); phosphorus-containing laxatives; an underactive parathyroid. — Malnutrition (chronic alcohol abuse); lower GI losses.","Manifestations — Usually asymptomatic by itself: the symptoms (muscle spasms, paresthesia, tetany, Chvostek's, Trousseau's) come from the accompanying hypocalcemia. — CNS depression and muscle weakness, including respiratory muscles (life-t…","Treatment — Calcium-based phosphate binders (calcium carbonate); dialysis if severe. — Replace (IV or oral, over hours); watch calcium alongside phosphorus (often CKD patients)."]},{"s":"Part 4 — Renal & Urinary: UTI, Stones, AKI, CKD & Dialysis▾","a":"part-4-renal-urinary-uti-stones-aki-ckd-dialysis","k":["Part 4 — Renal & Urinary: UTI, Stones, AKI, CKD & Dialysis▾","From Renal & Urinary: UTI, Stones, AKI, CKD & Dialysis. Med Surg I (NUR 334) review content. Built from the nine NUR 334 Week 4 voiceovers: urinary labs and diagnostics (two parts), UTI, pyelonephritis, nephrolithiasis, urinary diversion, a…","Urinary Labs","Commit to memory: creatinine 0.5 to 1.2 (the most reliable indicator of kidney function) and BUN 10 to 20.","Serum creatinine — 0.5 to 1.2 — From muscle breakdown and protein metabolism; released at a consistent rate; specific to the kidneys. Low only matters in muscle atrophy or malnutrition.","BUN (blood urea nitrogen) — 10 to 20 — Not specific to the kidneys. Also raised by high-protein or high-nitrogen tube feeds or diets, a GI bleed (digested blood is protein-rich), and dehydration (false high); lowered by overhydration (false…","Potassium — 3.5 to 5 — Kidneys are the main excretory organ: kidney disease = hyperkalemia. One of the first electrolytes to become abnormal; watched almost daily.","Total calcium — 9 to 10.5 — CKD decreases calcium reabsorption: low calcium, renal osteodystrophy (bone porosity, fracture risk).","Phosphorus — 3 to 4.5 — Inversely related to calcium; typically high in kidney disease.","Magnesium — 1.3 to 2.1 — Excreted by the kidneys; elevated in CKD.","Elevated BUN? Check the creatinine next. High creatinine too: probably the kidneys. Normal creatinine: look for another cause, such as a GI bleed (is the H&H dropping?).","Urinalysis & Urine Color","The UA","First morning urine (most concentrated); analyze within the hour.","Should have no bilirubin, glucose, ketones, protein, RBCs, or WBCs. Glucose: think diabetes (glucosuria, DKA). WBCs: suspect UTI. RBCs: hematuria.","Odor \"aromatic\"; color amber-yellow; pH 4.6 to 6.","Specific gravity 1.010 to 1.030 (tap water is 1.000). Well hydrated = low; dehydrated = high (1.035 means the patient needs to drink). Lewis's range differs slightly; exam questions will fall outside both ranges.","Color and clarity","Clear, slightly cloudy, cloudy, turbid (very cloudy, as in a UTI; it showed up on the HESI).","Light yellow (big water drinker) to amber or dark amber (not drinking).","Red: hematuria, or certain medications.","Brown, \"Coca-Cola\" urine: obstructive jaundice (bilirubin excreted in urine instead of the GI tract).","Urinary Diagnostics","Creatinine clearance (24-hour urine) — Approximates the GFR (blood filtered past the glomerulus per minute). A dropping clearance raises concern for CKD. — Discard the first void (start time), save all urine for 24 hours, keep it cold (on i…","Estimated GFR — A calculation from serum creatinine plus age, sex, weight, ethnicity; recommended by the National Kidney Foundation over 24-hour urines. — The same labs can put two people (for example, a 76-year-old woman vs a 28-year-old m…","Bladder scan — Non-invasive bedside measure of urine in the bladder. — Water-soluble gel.","Clean-catch urine — Confirms a suspected UTI and identifies the organism. — Sterile cup; clean the meatus (the urethra isn't sterile, the bladder is); void a second or two into the toilet, then catch midstream.","Cystoscopy — Scoping the bladder wall; lithotomy position. — Consent. Burning, pink-tinged urine, and frequency afterward are expected; bright red blood is not.","KUB (kidneys, ureters, bladder) — An X-ray of the lower abdomen and pelvis (like a chest X-ray for the lungs). — Bowel prep possible.","IVP (intravenous pyelogram) — IV dye outlines the renal pelvis: stones, tumors, cysts, blockages. — Bowel prep possible; check iodine/contrast allergy (anaphylaxis); warn about the flushed feeling (expected); force fluids after (nephrotoxic…","Retrograde pyelogram — Through a cystoscope, backward up the urinary system. — Used if the IVP doesn't visualize well, for contrast allergy, or elevated creatinine: the dye doesn't go systemic. Prep like an IVP; complications like a cystosc…","Renal biopsy — Tissue sample (for example, suspected kidney cancer). — Consent; check PT/INR, coags, platelets (very vascular); no aspirin or warfarin. Side-lying flexed position. After: pressure dressing, lie on the affected side, bed rest…","Non-contrast spiral CT (CT KUB) — The gold standard for renal colic (stone pain). — Quick, non-invasive; used more than IVP or retrograde.","Urinary Tract Infection","Terms","Upper UTI: the kidney parenchyma, pelvis, ureters; more serious, systemic (fever, chills, flank pain / CVA tenderness).","Lower UTI: the bladder (cystitis); no systemic signs.","Pyelonephritis: inflammation, usually infection, of the renal parenchyma and collecting system.","Urosepsis: the infection in the blood; can be life-threatening.","CAUTI (catheter-associated UTI): almost a third of healthcare-associated infections. \"Catheters are bad\": if not truly needed, get them out; the nurse is often the one to ask.","Risk factors","Urinary stasis (neurogenic bladder, an obstructing stone, urethral stricture, tumors).","Females (shorter urethra); pregnancy.","Compromised immunity (aging, diabetes); severe constipation (impedes outflow).","Poor hygiene; habitually delaying urination (\"nurse's bladder\").","Lower UTI symptoms","Emptying: hesitancy, intermittency, post-void dribbling, retention, incomplete emptying, dysuria.","Storage: frequency, urgency, incontinence, nocturia, nocturnal enuresis.","The most common bacterial infection in women; usually gram-negative E. coli ascending the urethra.","Older adults often present with confusion, sometimes as the first sign.","Diagnosis","Bacteria and pyuria (WBCs) in the urine. A point-of-care dipstick: positive nitrites and leukocyte esterase (an enzyme in WBCs). Confirm with a urinalysis.","Clean catch or Foley sample for complicated, hospital-acquired, frequent, or non-responding UTIs.","Prevention teaching","Empty the bladder regularly and completely; don't rush.","Wipe front to back; drink lots of fluid (water is best).","Cranberry: no definitive evidence it improves outcomes (fine if it means they drink more).","Sexually active women: urinate before and especially after intercourse.","Avoid bladder irritants.","Hospital-acquired UTI prevention and care","Remove catheters ASAP or avoid them; aseptic technique.","Catheter in the vagina by mistake: leave it there, get a new sterile kit, and catheterize the urethra.","Hand washing, gloves, nail care; clean up incontinence quickly; offer regular toileting.","Fluids unless contraindicated (heart failure, kidney failure, advanced liver disease).","Comfort: warm bath or shower (no bubble baths), local heat.","Take the full antibiotic course; should feel better within a couple of days, otherwise follow up (culture and sensitivity).","Pyelonephritis","Presentation and diagnosis","An upper UTI involving the parenchyma; more systemic than cystitis.","From mild fatigue to a sudden onset of chills, fever, vomiting, and CVA tenderness (flank pain).","UTI diagnostics plus a CBC with diff (shift to the left: neutrophils and bands), imaging, blood cultures if it may be in the blood.","Treatment","Mild: outpatient; fluids; NSAIDs or antipyretics (acetaminophen); broad-spectrum antibiotic, then narrowed to the culture. Cultures repeated after 14 to 21 days; may be on antibiotics for weeks.","Sicker: hospitalized with IV hydration and IV antibiotics, switched to oral once nausea, vomiting, and dehydration improve.","CVA tenderness","Costovertebral angle (not cerebrovascular accident): just below the last rib on each side of the back, where the kidneys sit (kidneys aren't palpable).","The provider lays one hand there and strikes it with the other fist. With pyelonephritis it is exceedingly tender; the patient will jump. An unexpected finding that points to an upper UTI.","Kidney Stones (Nephrolithiasis)","Diagnosis and types","History and physical; non-contrast spiral CT (gold standard), ultrasound, KUB, or IVP.","Urinalysis for blood, crystals, pH; analyze any stone retrieved.","Five types: calcium oxalate (most common), calcium phosphate, uric acid, cystine, struvite.","Pain","One of the strongest pain sensations known: severe visceral pain, \"renal colic\" (waxes and wanes, spasms); nausea and vomiting that don't improve after vomiting.","It can cause hemodynamic compromise, so treating the pain may be the priority (unlike typical post-op pain).","Opioids are first-line (sometimes a PCA), plus non-drug measures. Some patients are admitted just for uncontrolled pain.","Treatment and teaching","Small stones: pass spontaneously at home, straining all urine to catch the stone. Larger stones: a stent, or removal. Antibiotics if infected; watch for obstruction.","Lithotripsy (outpatient): shock waves pulverize the stone. Internal (cystoscope, percutaneous, laser) or external (non-invasive). Afterward hematuria is expected; a stent placed to pass fragments comes out in 1 to 2 weeks; decreased urine o…","Daily hydration (water). Forcing fluids once a stone is present hasn't been shown to push it out.","Diet by stone type: calcium oxalate = low-oxalate diet (dark roughage, spinach, cocoa, nuts), not a low-calcium diet; uric acid = low-purine diet (gout patients can form uric acid stones).","Immobile or sedentary patients are at risk: mobilize, turn, fluids; sit (women) or stand (men) to void regularly for full emptying.","Nephrostomy tube","Surgically placed directly into the renal pelvis through the flank when a ureter is totally obstructed; drains to an external bag, bypassing the ureter, bladder, and urethra. Not long-term.","Pain or drainage around the site: suspect a blockage. Irrigation requires an order; meticulous aseptic technique (high infection risk); only a tiny amount of sterile saline (it's the renal pelvis, not the bladder).","Urinary Diversions","Usually after a cystectomy (bladder removal, often for bladder cancer): the kidneys and ureters work, but there is no reservoir.","Ileal conduit (incontinent) — Ureters are anastomosed to a snippet of ileum brought to the skin as a stoma; urine flows out all the time. — The former gold standard; still chosen with significant comorbidities or shorter life expectancy. Pe…","Continent cutaneous reservoir (Kock, Indiana, Miami pouch) — An internal pouch with a stoma and sphincter; the patient self-catheterizes. — No bag. Catheterize every few hours at first, extending to every 4 to 6 hours. Largely replaced by t…","Orthotopic neobladder (continent, to the urethra) — A reservoir made from intestine (usually ileum), connected to the native urethra. — The newest and preferred: most like normal voiding. No urge to void (no nerve supply): relax the sphinct…","Pre-op: fears, body image, teaching; a wound ostomy continence nurse. Post-op: NPO with an NG to suction for a couple of days to rest the gut; skin integrity risk.","Acute Kidney Injury (AKI)","What it is","A sudden, rapidly progressive decline in kidney function (hours to days), with or without less urine. A syndrome, from slight deterioration to severe impairment.","Decreased GFR (less than 90), decreased urine output, BUN above 20, creatinine elevated.","Potentially reversible (CKD is not), but high mortality, especially in the ICU; usually in patients with other comorbidities.","Often follows prolonged hypotension, hypovolemia, or a nephrotoxic agent.","Urine output thresholds","Less than 30 mL/hour (the instructor's preferred \"magic number\"), or less than 400 mL in 24 hours: loose definitions of oliguria.","Example: 50 mL in 2 hours = notify the provider (after ruling out something you can fix, like a kinked Foley).","Waiting 24 hours may let the AKI worsen.","Prerenal (the most common) — Decreased perfusion to the kidneys: hypotension, hypovolemia. May develop within a day or two.","Intrarenal — Inside the kidney; most commonly acute tubular necrosis (mentioned only here).","Postrenal — Obstruction after the kidneys, for example a swollen prostate backing up urine.","The oliguric phase (the focus in this course)","Phases: oliguric, diuretic, recovery. An AKI that doesn't recover can become irreversible CKD.","Oliguric: less than 400 mL/24 hours; begins about a day after a hypotensive event; lasts 1 to 3 weeks.","Fluid retention (fluid volume excess), dilutional hyponatremia (confusion), hyperkalemia, metabolic acidosis, rising BUN (waste accumulation).","Treatment: find and stop the cause; correct fluid and electrolytes (especially hyperkalemia); diuretics and fluid restriction; daily weights; manage BP; prevent infection; nutrition; avoid nephrotoxic drugs.","Lowering Potassium","Low-potassium diet — Prevents recurrence; doesn't help much with an acute elevation.","GI potassium binders (cation-exchange resins) — Kayexalate (sodium polystyrene sulfonate) PO or enema; newer Veltassa (patiromer) and Lokelma (sodium zirconium cyclosilicate). Remove potassium through the GI tract, but not fast (not for an …","IV calcium gluconate — Raises the threshold for excitation to protect against dysrhythmias; doesn't lower the potassium. Temporary.","Insulin and dextrose — Moves potassium back into the cells. Temporary.","Sodium bicarbonate — Corrects acidosis and shifts potassium into the cells. Temporary.","Dialysis — The most effective way to remove potassium; with the binders, the only treatments that actually remove it from the body.","Chronic Kidney Disease (CKD)","1 — 90 or more (normal) — Asymptomatic; slow progression. Catching it here would be ideal.","2 — Starting to drop — Asymptomatic except maybe hypertension; most don't know they have CKD.","3a and 3b — Moderate drop (3b worse) — Hypertension, otherwise often asymptomatic; evaluate and treat complications. The critical point to catch patients.","4 — 15 to 29 — Manifestations appear; diagnosis usually happens here (the kidneys have huge reserve). Just short of dialysis or transplant.","5 — Less than 15 — Full kidney failure; uremic (waste in the blood); likely starting dialysis.","End-stage manifestations","Fluid and electrolytes lost: edema, hyperkalemia, high phosphorus, high magnesium, metabolic acidosis.","Not hungry, malnourished; itch \"like crazy\"; CNS changes (confusion).","No erythropoietin: severe anemia. Decreased vitamin D activation: hypocalcemia and renal osteodystrophy.","Immunosuppressed (toxic accumulation, immune cell dysfunction, chronic inflammation, dialysis stress); elevated lipids (often on statins).","The two biggest causes: high blood pressure and diabetes. Prevention = early detection and controlling both.","Nursing problems and care","Excess fluid volume: monitor, daily weights (more reliable than I&O), fluid restriction.","Malnutrition: nausea and vomiting, weight trends, serum protein, H&H; desirable allowed foods.","Risk for injury (brittle bones): calcium and vitamin D supplements; watch electrolytes.","Grieving and lifestyle change: community support, family.","Infection risk: aseptic line care (dialysis access, peritoneal catheter).","Activity intolerance (anemia): erythropoietin, monitoring the H&H.","Conservative therapy (not dialysis)","Control BP and glucose.","Contrast dye: avoid it, or protect the kidneys with acetylcysteine beforehand (also the acetaminophen-overdose antidote) and forced fluids.","Avoid nephrotoxic drugs: NSAIDs and aminoglycosides. (Acetaminophen is hard on the liver.)","Treat fluid overload (less PO intake, diuretics if the kidneys work), hyperkalemia (anticipate orders near 6), acidosis, mineral and bone disorders, hypertension, anemia, dyslipidemia, malnutrition.","Diet and fluids","Protein restriction before dialysis (BUN and creatinine are protein end products), even though the patient is malnourished: a conundrum. Once on dialysis, protein can increase and restrictions loosen. (You won't be asked to choose between t…","Typically low potassium, sodium, magnesium, and phosphorus, plus a fluid restriction (sometimes today's allowance = yesterday's urine output). Renal shakes and tube feeds (\"nephroshake,\" Novasource Renal): high calorie, high protein, low po…","Teaching: thirst alternatives (mouth swabs); medication rationales and side effects; signs of electrolyte imbalance (muscle weakness with hyperkalemia); weigh daily and report a gain of more than 4 pounds in 2 days (fluid), rising BP, short…","Dialysis","How it works","For stage 5 when conservative measures aren't enough (or a kidney transplant). About 9 in 10 dialysis patients get hemodialysis; about 1 in 10 get peritoneal dialysis.","Osmosis: water moves from lower to higher particle concentration. Diffusion: particles move from higher to lower concentration. Both aim to equalize across a semipermeable membrane: outside the body in hemodialysis; the peritoneum in PD.","The nephrologist sets the dialysate concentrations from the labs: to pull potassium off, the dialysate has less potassium than the blood.","Schedule — About 4 to 5 hours (also given as 4 to 6), 3 days a week (Mon/Wed/Fri or Tue/Thu/Sat), at a clinic — CAPD: 4 to 5 (also given as 4 to 6) manual exchanges every day; automated PD: a cycler at night (cycles of about an hour each)","Access — AV fistula (preferred), AV graft, or a temporary external catheter — Tenckhoff catheter placed surgically into the peritoneum","Advantages — Fast: can pull off serious potassium and fluid in one session — At home, no fancy machines (CAPD), more mobility, fewer diet restrictions, more like continuous filtering, easier on the heart","Disadvantages — Needs vascular access; may need heparin; stricter diet and fluid limits (Sunday night before a Monday session can feel miserable) — Peritonitis; requires daily commitment; body image","Unique complication — Disequilibrium syndrome (often the first few sessions): disorientation, headache, agitation, nausea, vomiting, even seizures, from rapid fluid shifts — Peritonitis is the number one complication (cloudy dialysate); als…","Hemodialysis Access","AV fistula","A surgeon anastomoses an artery to a vein, most often in the forearm; permanent.","Needs about 6 weeks to mature before use (a temporary access is used meanwhile). If it survives the first 6 months, it can last up to 20 years.","Assess every shift: palpate a thrill (a vibration like a cat purring) and auscultate a bruit (\"whoosh\"); both are expected and confirm patency. A lost bruit = notify the provider.","Sign over the bed: no BPs or IV sticks in that arm.","Check distal perfusion (cap refill, numbness, tingling, radial and ulnar pulses, motor function, pain), especially right after surgery.","AV graft and temporary catheters","AV graft: synthetic loop under the skin; usable sooner; but a foreign body, so infection risk. For small, weak veins or when access is needed within 6 weeks.","Tunneled, cuffed IJ catheter (provider-placed; about 1 to 3 weeks), or a femoral catheter (bed rest, minimal leg movement; removed ASAP).","A temporary dialysis catheter (the transcript calls it a \"u-doll\" catheter; often for AKI in the ICU; about 2 weeks): looks like a central line but is not; never run medications or fluids through it.","Peritoneal Dialysis","1. Fill (inflow) — About 2 liters of dialysate into the peritoneal space; about 10 minutes.","2. Dwell — Prescribed time when diffusion and osmosis happen across the peritoneum; the patient can walk around.","3. Drain — The bag on the floor on a clean towel; drains by gravity in about 15 to 30 minutes.","PD nursing points","Tenckhoff site heals in a week or two; then no dressing, shower and pat dry; daily catheter care; teach infection signs.","Aseptic technique every exchange (4 or 5 times a day, so contamination risk is high). Repeated peritonitis can force a switch to HD.","Not all dialysate draining back? Turn the patient side to side. Cloudy dialysate may mean peritonitis.","Dialysis nursing and drugs","Trend vital signs, I&O, daily weights, labs; daily catheter and fistula care; know the complications.","Drugs not excreted by the kidneys can build to toxic levels; drugs that cross the membrane are dialyzed out (subtherapeutic after). Give dialyzable drugs after dialysis; doses may be adjusted (\"renal dosing\" by pharmacy)."]},{"s":"Part 5 — HIV, Skin Disorders, Surgical Wounds & Burns▾","a":"part-5-hiv-skin-disorders-surgical-wounds-burns","k":["Part 5 — HIV, Skin Disorders, Surgical Wounds & Burns▾","From HIV, Skin Disorders, Surgical Wounds & Burns. Med Surg I (NUR 334) review content. Built from the seven NUR 334 Week 5 voiceovers: HIV labs and diagnostics, HIV, skin cancer, skin infections and benign disorders, surgical wound managem…","HIV Labs & Diagnostics","Screening","Routine testing for adults and pregnant women: at least once for ages 13 to 75 without risk factors.","High risk: test yearly. Men who have sex with men, IV drug users, people who exchange sex for money or drugs, sex partners of people with HIV, and people whose partner's HIV status is unknown.","Consent is voluntary. Opt out: a patient can decline, and we document them as opted out.","Confidentiality; counseling and referral to care if positive.","The test to remember","Fourth generation (HIV antibody AND antigen) is the preferred test. The antigen comes before antibodies, so it can turn positive within 2 to 3 weeks (older antibody-only tests: up to a month or longer).","Identifies early infection in up to 80% of patients; can be a rapid test.","ELISA tests antibodies only; Western blot is sometimes the second test.","HIV RNA (viral load): qualitative (positive or negative) and quantitative (progression, response to treatment).","Reading the Results","Positive — Positive test followed by a second positive. A positive always gets a confirmatory test.","Negative — Tests negative. No second test needed.","Indeterminate — Positive at first, then negative on the confirmatory test.","Window period: the time between exposure and when the test gives an accurate positive. During it the patient is still infectious but tests negative. Most people develop antibodies around week 3 to 4 after exposure.","Eclipse period: from exposure to when the viral load peaks; the patient is infectious at the end of it.","Monitoring Progression","CD4 count — Normal 500 to 1400; want it more than 500. Less than 200 = AIDS. — How healthy the immune system is; progression; risk for opportunistic infections; when to increase prophylactic treatment.","Viral load (HIV RNA) — Undetectable — Best indicator of how active the virus is; viral burden; response to drugs. Untreated HIV produces billions of copies a day.","Both checked every 3 to 4 months (stable untreated patients or patients on antiretroviral therapy).","Undetectable does not mean absent; it just can't be measured. Suppressing HIV to undetectable is the goal of medication therapy.","Also monitor WBC, platelets and H&H (all blood cells can be affected), liver function tests (hepatitis is a common comorbidity), and resistance to medications.","Not tested (instructor said you don't need to memorize): the \"out of 100 people with HIV\" statistics, the UpToDate testing algorithm slide, and the antibody-timing variation slide.","HIV & AIDS: Nursing Care","AIDS = either an opportunistic infection develops or the CD4 drops below 200 (usually around the same time). Opportunistic infections are ones normal people don't get; they appear with immunosuppression.","1. Early / acute infection — Rapid replication; not detected on lab tests; no symptoms, but infectious. The patient then seroconverts: antibodies detectable, \"the worst flu ever,\" highly infectious, and most people don't think to get tested…","2. Latency — Body fights effectively; asymptomatic or mild symptoms; virus still producing; steady drop in CD4.","3. AIDS — Viral load increases, CD4 low; opportunistic diseases around a CD4 of 200.","Lecture conflict, kept as stated: the HIV labs voiceover gives the latency period as 10 to 20 years; the HIV voiceover gives 3 to 12 years.","Assessment","AIDS dementia complex: cognitive changes with advanced HIV.","Oral manifestations are common as the CD4 drops: do an oral assessment.","Infections can be fungal, viral, bacterial or cancerous.","Transmission: sexual, parenteral, mother to baby.","Collaborative goals","Monitor progression and immune function.","Start and monitor antiretroviral therapy (ART).","Prevent, detect and treat opportunistic infections.","Manage symptoms and ART complications; prevent transmission.","Be comfortable with sensitive subjects: often the nurse is the one who brings them up.","Occupational Exposure & PEP","Post-exposure prophylaxis: if drug therapy is indicated, start it within 1 to 2 hours, and definitely within 3 days. Follow-up HIV testing at 6 and 12 weeks, then 6 months.","Blood is the highest risk at work; other body fluids can carry HIV. Universal precautions.","Needlestick from a person with HIV: a very small risk (heard as \"02 percent\" in the transcript; the exact figure is garbled).","Mucous membranes and non-intact skin also count as exposure. The regimen depends on how severe the exposure was and the source's status; it may be no drug therapy or combination therapy.","ART Adherence & Teaching","Promoting adherence","Non-adherence is a real danger: a patient who doesn't feel sick is less likely to take a daily regimen.","Patient is motivated before getting the prescription.","Social support is a primary priority for a positive outcome.","Negotiate the plan (autonomy); a simple plan; anticipate side effects; establish trust.","Teaching the patient with AIDS","They are immunosuppressed: avoid crowds and places with poor sanitation, and foods that can carry bacteria.","Avoid cleaning pet litter boxes; keep the home clean; no sick visitors.","Continue ART; CD4 and viral load every 3 to 4 months.","Express empathy; be judgment free.","PrEP (pre-exposure prophylaxis)","Candidates: anal or vaginal sex in the last 6 months and a partner with HIV (especially an unknown or detectable viral load), inconsistent condom use, or an STI diagnosed in the last 6 months; people who inject drugs with an injection partn…","Daily medication. Used as prescribed it prevents HIV from sex by 99%, and 74% for IV drug users.","Does not protect against other STIs: still use condoms. Insurance and Medicaid cover it.","Skin Lesions & Color","Macule — Flat, not elevated — Freckle, petechiae, measles, flat mole, vitiligo","Papule — Raised — Wart, elevated mole, lipoma, basal cell carcinoma","Plaque — — — Psoriasis","Pustule — Contains purulent drainage (pus) — Impetigo, acne","Vesicle — Contains yellow plasma-like fluid — Herpes zoster (shingles), second-degree burn blister","Wheal — Edematous, irregular shape, lasts only a few hours — Bug bite, rash; angioedema (face and lip swelling)","Atrophy — Secondary lesion — Aged skin, striae (stretch marks)","Excoriation — Top layer of epidermis missing; like an abrasion — Excoriation over a wound","Fissure — Small crack in the epidermis — Athlete's foot, eczema, chapping","Scaling — Dead epidermal skin that sheds — Drug reaction, peeling sunburn","Scar — Connective tissue replacing normal skin — —","Ulcer — Loss of epidermis and dermis; a crater — Pressure ulcer, canker","Petechiae look like freckles but are small hemorrhages (for example, thin blood from thrombocytopenia); a larger bruised area is ecchymosis.","Cyanosis — Hypoxia; a late sign of decreased oxygen. Heart and lung problems, or being very cold.","Pallor — Decreased hemoglobin in the area. Check the conjunctiva for the line of demarcation (anemia).","Loss of pigment — Vitiligo, an autoimmune disease; an assessment finding.","Yellow-orange — Jaundice: too much bilirubin (liver failure). Seen most in the eyes (sclera), mucous membranes and skin.","Erythema — Red: increased oxyhemoglobin from dilation or increased blood flow; infection (\"the incision was erythemic\").","Tan-brown — Increased melanin: suntan, pregnancy.","Skin Cancer","The most diagnosed cancer, partly because skin is visible (early detection). Classified as non-melanoma or melanoma.","Risk factors: fair skin, blonde or red hair, blue eyes; sun history; living near the equator or at high altitude; family or personal history; outdoor occupation; indoor tanning.","Basal cell carcinoma — Locally invasive, from basal cells. Erythematous, pearly, defined borders, barely elevated plaques, maybe a depression in the middle.","Squamous cell carcinoma — From keratinized epidermal cells; can be aggressive; scaling.","Malignant melanoma — From melanocytes; genetic and environmental factors (runs in families). Treatment depends on depth; depth correlates with survival. Poor prognosis unless diagnosed and treated early; can metastasize to any organ.","ABCDE of melanoma: Asymmetry · Border irregular (ragged, notched, blurred) · Color uneven · Diameter changing (usually increasing; most are larger than a pea) · Evolving (a mole that changed over weeks or months: notify the provider ASAP).","Biopsy confirms melanoma. Nursing: skin integrity care, anxiety and coping, biopsy site care, and annual dermatology checkups.","Skin Infections, Reactions & Benign Disorders","Bacterial: impetigo and cellulitis","Risks: excess moisture, obesity, dermatitis, systemic steroid or antibiotic use, chronic disease such as diabetes. Usually staph and strep.","If exudate (drainage) is present, the drainage is infectious. Good hygiene and infection control.","Cellulitis: inflammation of subcutaneous tissue; hot, tender, red, diffuse borders; chills, malaise, fever.","Treat with moist heat, immobilize, elevate; IV antibiotics if admitted. Untreated: gangrene, possible limb loss.","Viral: harder to treat","Herpes simplex, shingles, HPV, warts.","Shingles (herpes zoster): reactivated varicella zoster; vesicles; burning pain and neuralgia along a dermatome; incidence rises with age; contagious to people who haven't had the virus.","Shingles vaccine: advised for anyone older than 60, one time.","HPV: warts anywhere; usually preventable with a vaccine.","Fungal: easy to diagnose","Candidiasis: mouth, vagina, skin.","Tinea is named by location: corporis = ringworm, cruris = jock itch, pedis = athlete's foot.","Skin, hair and nails most susceptible; antifungal cream or solution.","Infestations and bites","Pediculosis (lice), bedbugs, ticks, scabies. Allergy to the venom usually causes the problem.","Prevent: avoid them, repellents, meticulous hygiene (hair, skin, clothing, bed, sexual partners), routine skin checks; tick check after the woods.","Stevens-Johnson syndrome and toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN): rare but life-threatening. Usually a severe adverse reaction to a medication (or to infection). Acute destruction of skin epithelium and mucous membranes (peeling lips); fever, …","Contact dermatitis is the benign end of the dermatitis spectrum (example: a reaction to rubber boots).","Psoriasis","Chronic autoimmune disease; usually develops at 15 to 35; runs in families.","Plaque psoriasis is most common: distinct red, scaly lesions on knees, elbows, hands, lower back; painful and itchy.","4 out of 10 develop psoriatic arthritis. Not life-threatening; body image disturbance.","Care for Skin Disorders","Interprofessional therapy","Phototherapy; radiation for squamous or basal cell; laser.","Antibiotics if bacterial. Steroids decrease inflammation but use judiciously (side effects, including skin atrophy).","Antihistamines for urticaria or pruritus; immunomodulators suppress an overactive immune system.","Punch biopsy or scraping (for example, malignant melanoma).","Nursing tips","Compresses: room-temperature tap water; assess for maceration (white, puffy epidermis from a wet dressing).","Tepid baths: too hot a bath adds to itching.","Apply lotion while skin is still damp (seals in moisture).","Pruritus: cool environment, hydration, antipruritic creams, systemic antihistamines (diphenhydramine), avoid scratching.","Surgical Wound Management","What slows healing","Age and loss of skin turgor; fragile skin, worst with long-term systemic steroids.","Decreased circulation and oxygenation (peripheral vascular disease).","Slower regeneration, less nutrient absorption and collagen, impaired immunity, dehydration.","Leukopenia; existing infection; chemo, anti-inflammatories, steroids.","Low hemoglobin, obesity, smoking, chronic disease, malnutrition.","Three parts of wound management: assess, clean, protect.","Color: red/pink = healthy regeneration; yellow = maybe purulent; black = eschar (hinders healing).","Length x width x depth (cm); sinus tracts or tunnels; redness or swelling.","Primary intention: edges well approximated (butted together; staples, sutures, adhesive). Pulling apart = not approximated.","Drains and tubing; pain at the site.","Measuring & Charting","First measurement head to toe, second side to side, third depth.","Tunneling: a tunnel under the skin, probed with a cotton-tipped applicator. Undermining: slips under the lip around the wound.","Charted by the clock, 12 o'clock at the patient's head. Example: \"full thickness, red wound bed, 7 x 5 x 3 cm, 3 cm tunneling at 7 o'clock, 2 cm undermining at 3 to 5 o'clock.\"","Drainage","Note amount (scant, moderate, large, copious; if weighed, 1 gram = 1 mL), odor, consistency, color, and the skin around the wound and drain.","Serous — Watery, clear, slight yellow tint (inside of a blister) — A clean wound","Sanguineous — Red; serum plus RBCs — Bright = active bleeding; darker = older bleeding","Serosanguineous — Watery, pale pink (serum and blood) — Often a new wound; the most common term","Purulent — Thick; yellow, tan, green or brown; WBCs, debris, bacteria — Infection","Nursing Interventions","Nutrition is the priority. Without adequate nutrition a wound will not heal. Choosing between impaired wound and imbalanced nutrition in a malnourished patient, imbalanced nutrition comes first: all the asepsis in the world won't heal it. H…","Adequate hydration. Suture and staple removal on the provider's order.","Premedicate for dressing changes so the analgesic is peaking during the change.","Antimicrobials as ordered; document descriptively (paint a picture so the wound can be trended).","Wound care nurse specialists are your best friends.","Dressings","Gauze (4 x 4) / wet to dry — Wet gauze placed in the wound and pulled off once dry: mechanical debridement of the top layer until granulation tissue forms.","Transparent — Self-adhesive (Tegaderm).","Hydrocolloid — Occlusive; swells in the presence of exudate (DuoDERM).","Hydrogel — Mostly water; gels on contact with exudate, promoting autolytic debridement; rehydrates and fills dead space; may need a secondary occlusive dressing. For infected deep wounds or necrotic tissue, not heavily draining ones. Moist …","Alginate — Non-adherent; conforms to the wound shape and absorbs exudate.","Collagen — Powders, pastes, granules, gels.","Wound vac — Vacuum-assisted closure: foam in the wound bed, occlusive dressing, negative pressure. Decreases swelling, enhances healing; good for dehisced wounds; can infuse antibiotics. Drainage counts as output.","Complications of Wounds","Adhesions, contractions, hemorrhage, dehiscence, evisceration, fistula, infection, excessive granulation tissue, keloids. Starred in lecture: hemorrhage, dehiscence and evisceration, infection.","Hemorrhage","Greatest risk up to 48 hours after injury or surgery.","Clot coming loose, slipped sutures, vessel damage.","Internal bleeding: swelling and distension, sanguineous drainage, subtle vital sign changes; first: increased heart rate (compensating for decreased stroke volume).","Hematoma: local blood collection, blue or red bruise.","Apply direct pressure, notify the provider, monitor vitals closely.","Infection","Infection does not happen within 24 to 48 hours: a low-grade temp then isn't infection yet.","Shows 2 to 11 days after surgery or injury: pain, erythema, swelling, purulent drainage, fever, chills, foul odor, raised vitals, leukocytosis.","Risks: impaired circulation, malnutrition, chronic disease, poor wound care.","Aseptic technique, nutrition, rest; antibiotics after cultures are drawn (broad spectrum, then tailored to C&S).","Dehiscence and evisceration","Dehiscence: separation of the wound, usually a few underlying layers. Evisceration (the worst): viscera visible; can follow abdominal incisions; needs immediate surgery.","Signs: a sudden large increase in sanguineous or serosanguineous drainage after straining; the patient felt something \"pop\" or \"give way.\"","Precipitated by coughing, sneezing, vomiting, hiccuping, straining for a BM.","Risks: older age, obesity, abdominal cancer, vomiting, infection, poor suturing, dehydration, malnutrition, increased pressure.","Evisceration or severe dehiscence: notify the provider immediately and stay with the patient. Cover the wound and any organs with sterile dressings soaked in sterile normal saline. Do not reinsert organs. Position with hips and knees bent. …","Burns","Thermal — Most common: flame, flash, scald, touching a hot object.","Chemical — Acid, alkali, chemicals.","Inhalation — Inhaled heat burns the nose, mouth, esophagus, lung parenchyma: compromises the airway.","Electrical — Electrical energy converted to heat.","Radiation — Transfer of radiant energy.","Extreme temperature — Frostbite; children and older adults at risk (less ability to generate heat).","Severity depends on depth, extent, location, age and other health problems. Severe burns go to a burn center (the ABA referral criteria are not needed).","Three depths: superficial partial thickness, deep partial thickness, full thickness (formerly first to fourth degree). Full thickness may be painless (nerves destroyed).","Rule of nines (quick initial estimate of extent): head 9 (front 4.5, back 4.5); each arm 9; anterior leg 9, posterior leg 9; trunk and back 9 x 2 (as stated). The instructor won't ask you to calculate it on her test, but has seen it on HESI…","The other chart on the slide is more accurate because it accounts for age and body proportion; the rule of nines is easier to do on the fly.","Emergent (Resuscitative) Phase","Stop the burning, ABCs (patent airway, breathing, blood pressure), assess the burn, transfer if criteria are met, stabilize. Lasts up to 72 hours from the burn; hemodynamically labile. Ends when fluid mobilizes and diuresis begins.","Main concern: massive fluid and electrolyte shift from the huge increase in capillary permeability, leading to hypovolemic shock and massive edema. Instructor takeaway for burns: excruciating pain and massive fluid and electrolyte shifts.","Cardiovascular — Shock; increased blood viscosity, clot risk. Circumferential burns: edema impairs circulation, so an escharotomy (scalpel through the eschar) restores distal perfusion.","Pulmonary — Upper and lower airway if fire was inhaled.","Urinary — Acute renal failure from decreased renal blood flow (shock) and myoglobin and hemoglobin blocking the tubules.","Nursing management","1. Airway. 2. Fluids.","Aggressive resuscitation: at least two large-bore IVs or a central line. Crystalloids such as LR; colloids, typically albumin, to raise cardiac output. (Fluid formulas: not needed.)","Wound care: debride necrotic tissue, escharotomy, fasciotomy (opening fascia for distal circulation). Keep the room warm (lost skin barrier). Goal: permanent skin coverage.","Drug and nutrition therapy","IV opioids and sedatives around the clock.","Tetanus shot if none in the last 10 years.","Topical antimicrobials (Silvadene); systemic antibiotics. Sepsis is the leading cause of death in burns.","VTE prophylaxis (increased viscosity).","Metabolism is extremely high: enteral (tube) feedings if the gut works.","Acute & Rehab Phases","Acute phase","Begins with mobilization of extracellular fluid and diuresis; ends when partial-thickness wounds heal and full-thickness wounds are grafted. Weeks to months.","Partial thickness: eschar removed, re-epithelialization. Full thickness: surgical debridement and skin grafts.","Split-thickness graft harvested with a dermatome (often the thigh), may be meshed; donor site covered with a hydrophilic foam dressing.","Keep monitoring labs: fluid and electrolytes still in flux. PT, OT.","Rehab phase","Goal: return to a functional role in society; reconstructive surgery.","Prevent contractures and hypertrophic scarring: PT, OT, scheduled range of motion, pressure garments (keep scars flat).","Emotional needs: anger, anxiety, depression, fear, guilt, helplessness."]},{"s":"Part 6 — Gastrointestinal: Upper GI, Bleeds, IBD, Ostomies & More▾","a":"part-6-gastrointestinal-upper-gi-bleeds-ibd-ostomies-more","k":["Part 6 — Gastrointestinal: Upper GI, Bleeds, IBD, Ostomies & More▾","From Gastrointestinal: Upper GI, Bleeds, IBD, Ostomies & More. Med Surg I (NUR 334) review content. Built from the ten NUR 334 Week 6 voiceovers: GI labs and diagnostics, GERD and hiatal hernia, peptic ulcer disease, upper GI bleed, bariatr…","GI Labs & Diagnostics","Upper GI / small bowel follow-through — Patient swallows contrast (barium or Gastrografin); fluoroscopy and X-rays from mouth to small intestine. Films about every 30 minutes until contrast reaches the terminal ileum. Gastrografin, not bari…","Barium swallow / modified barium swallow — A closer look at the esophagus. For dysphagia after a stroke, non-cardiac chest pain, painful swallowing, reflux. Modified = different consistencies (thin liquids, honey-thick, applesauce). — Same …","Barium enema (lower GI) — Barium into the lower half. — Bowel clear of stool: enemas till clear, clear liquids the night before, NPO; cathartics after; white stool teaching.","Gastric emptying study — Nuclear imaging for gastroparesis (delayed emptying). Patient eats a cooked egg with a trace of radioactive material; scans at 0, 1, 2, 4 hours. — Only a trace of radioactivity, no danger; must lie flat during scann…","CT / MRI — CT: with or without contrast. MRI: radiofrequency waves. — CT contrast: ask about iodine or shellfish allergy (clinically not really relevant anymore). No MRI with metal or pregnancy.","EGD (esophagogastroduodenoscopy) — Scope the upper GI: tumors, inflammation, hiatal hernia, ulcers, obstruction, bleeding. Can be therapeutic (cauterize a bleed or varices). Conscious sedation; throat anesthetized. — NPO 8 hours; consent. A…","Colonoscopy — Scope the colon after a bowel prep; sedated; air used, so gas afterward. — A good prep matters: leftover stool means repeating the test. Watch for perforation and bleeding: blood, vital sign changes, tender abdomen.","Stool tests — Occult blood; culture (bacteria, including C. diff); ova and parasites. — Occult blood: avoid red meat 24 to 48 hours if ordered (evidence is debatable).","GI Drugs","Antacids — — — Quick but short-lived; 1 to 3 hours after meals and at bedtime. Treats symptoms only.","H2 receptor blockers — \"-tidine\": cimetidine, famotidine — Decrease gastric acid. OTC or prescription.","Proton pump inhibitors (PPIs) — \"-prazole\": omeprazole, lansoprazole, esomeprazole, pantoprazole — Given IV in the hospital a lot.","Cytoprotective agent — Sucralfate — Coats the stomach. Focus on generics (NCLEX does).","Prokinetic — Metoclopramide — Enhances gastric emptying: gastroparesis, reflux after meals, and poor appetite (for example, during chemo).","GERD & Hiatal Hernia","GERD: backward flow of acidic stomach contents into the esophagus through a loose lower esophageal sphincter (LES). Heartburn is the most common symptom; regurgitation; respiratory symptoms (night coughing spells).","Complications and diagnosis","Esophagitis; Barrett's esophagus; cough and bronchospasm.","Barium swallow or EGD, but often just a high-dose PPI for about two weeks: if symptoms respond, that's the diagnosis and invasive tests aren't needed.","Lifestyle teaching","Identify and avoid triggers. Quit smoking: LES strength improves immediately.","Avoid fatty foods; small meals, no fluids with meals (drink between); no late-night snacking.","Sit up 2 to 3 hours after meals; raise the head of the bed on 4 to 6 inch blocks (\"gravity is not reflux's friend\").","Weight loss if large (eases intra-abdominal pressure); medications.","LINX device — Endoscopic. A ring of rare-earth magnets around the LES: increases closure but lets food pass.","Nissen fundoplication — Surgical (laparoscopic or open). The fundus is wrapped and sutured into a collar around the LES (\"a big bear hug\"). Laparoscopic preferred over a big incision when there's a choice.","Hiatal hernia","Part of the stomach herniates up through the diaphragm (diaphragmatic or esophageal hernia). Not reflux: a herniated area above the LES.","Often structural: increased abdominal pressure (pregnancy, obesity).","May be asymptomatic; symptoms and treatment similar to GERD.","Barrett's esophagus","Long-term esophagitis that changes cells (metaplasia). Can follow GERD or hiatal hernia; not everyone gets it.","Rare; confirmed by biopsy.","30 to 40 fold increased risk of esophageal cancer.","Peptic Ulcer Disease","Erosion of GI mucosa from hydrochloric acid and pepsin. Causes: H. pylori and drugs, number one being NSAIDs. NSAIDs plus H. pylori = \"double trouble.\" Also alcohol, smoking, family history. Stress aggravates an existing ulcer but hasn't be…","Commit to memory: NSAIDs (ibuprofen, naproxen) = GI upset and kidney problems. Acetaminophen (not an NSAID) = hepatotoxicity.","Types","Gastric: usually the antrum. Duodenal: just past the pyloric sphincter; 80% of ulcers are duodenal.","Most ulcers have H. pylori (the percentage was garbled in the transcript as \"90 to 90%\").","Acute: fewer layers, shorter, resolves. Chronic: 4 times more common, more severe, long-term.","Diagnosis","EGD.","Urea breath test and stool test: non-invasive H. pylori tests. Biopsy is the gold standard (invasive).","CBC (H&H for bleeding) and stool for occult blood (may be dark and tarry or invisible).","Complications: HOP","Hemorrhage (most common) — A creeping heart rate (70, 80, 86, 95: investigate). Redder NG aspirate. Pain may decrease at first (blood neutralizes gastric contents). — NG to low wall suction to decompress; then as for an upper GI bleed.","Obstruction — Most likely near the pyloric sphincter; gradual onset. — NG to low wall suction until resolved; irrigate per protocol.","Perforation — Sudden, severe pain radiating to the back, not relieved by anything; rigid, board-like abdomen; shallow fast respirations; weak, rapid pulse; absent bowel sounds (listen 5 minutes); rebound tenderness. — Bacterial peritonitis …","Collaborative care","Rest; quit smoking; no special diet (individualized).","Stop aspirin and NSAIDs for 4 to 6 weeks; if aspirin must continue, give it with a PPI.","H. pylori: antibiotics plus PPIs; maybe sucralfate.","Refractory PUD: Billroth I or II reconstruction after vagotomy (cuts the vagus nerve to decrease HCl) and antrectomy. Know it exists, not the differences.","Dumping syndrome","After part of the stomach is removed: PUD surgery, gastrectomy, fundoplication, Roux-en-Y.","A hyperosmolar (concentrated sugar) bolus dumps into the small intestine; fluid shifts out of the vascular space into the gut: diarrhea, dizziness, sweating, palpitations soon after eating.","Later, postprandial hypoglycemia about 2 hours after eating (insulin surge).","Self-limiting: a few months up to a year.","Dumping syndrome diet: six small meals, never a big one · no fluids with meals · avoid concentrated simple sugars · protein and fat encouraged · rest after eating.","Upper GI Bleed","Hematemesis, bright red (frank) — Recent or ongoing bleed.","Coffee-ground emesis — Blood partly digested: older bleeding or bleeding has stopped. Malodorous.","Melena — Black, tarry, malodorous stool; up to 8 hours of digestion.","Occult blood — Not visible; positive guaiac. Never expected unless a GI bleed is already diagnosed.","Causes","Ulcers: 50% of upper GI bleeds. The two most common causes of bleeding ulcers: H. pylori and NSAIDs. Also aspirin and steroids.","Stress-related mucosal disease (SRMD): in 70 to 90% of ICU patients, so ICU patients get a PPI (IV pantoprazole).","Esophageal varices (portal hypertension from end-stage liver disease): bleeding can be uncontrollable. A Blakemore tube (balloon pressure on the bleed) is an emergency, ICU-bound measure.","Nursing","Vital signs vs baseline; orthostatic hypotension; signs of hypovolemia: rising HR, insatiable thirst, cool clammy skin, restlessness (always investigate).","Rigid, board-like abdomen = perforation; distended with guarding = peritonitis.","Labs: H&H and BUN (a GI bleed raises BUN).","18-gauge IV (anticipate PRBCs, fluid resuscitation).","NG lavage (effectiveness questionable, but on standardized exams).","Endoscopy with hemostasis is first line: nothing else helps until the bleed is stopped.","Bariatric Surgery","Currently the only treatment with lasting weight loss in severe obesity. Criteria: BMI 40 or higher, or 35 with one or more severe obesity-related complications (hypertension, diabetes, heart failure, sleep apnea). Extensive psychological, …","Sleeve gastrectomy — Restrictive — Greater curvature removed and stapled: a tubular stomach. Permanent.","Intragastric balloon — Restrictive — Minimally invasive, temporary: a saline balloon of about 400 to 700 mL promotes satiety. Usually not left more than 6 months (leak risk).","Roux-en-Y gastric bypass — Restrictive and malabsorptive — Gold standard and most common. Pouch only 15 mL (a medicine cup is 30). Rest of the stomach disconnected, not removed. Stay 2 to 3 days; 60 to 70% weight loss in 1 to 2 years.","Outcomes","Better: glucose tolerance, diabetes, blood pressure, cholesterol and triglycerides, GERD, sleep apnea.","Adverse: malabsorption of vitamins and iron, so lifelong vitamin supplementation; dumping syndrome.","Post-op care","Control pain to enhance mobility.","Vigilant for wound infection, dehiscence and evisceration (higher risk with obesity).","NG to wall suction at first; then water and sugar-free liquids in tiny amounts, high-protein liquids, pureed, then solids.","Long-term: the DASH diet, in smaller portions.","Inflammatory Bowel Disease","Location — Mouth to anus; most often the terminal ileum (and colon) — Colon only; starts in the rectum and moves up","Pattern — Skip lesions, cobblestone — Uniform and continuous","Depth — Transmural: more likely to perforate — Mucosal / submucosal","Bleeding — Sometimes — More common","Malabsorption — Yes (small intestine) — Not usually (colon absorbs water)","Complications — Abscesses, fistulas, strictures, perforation — Perforation from toxic megacolon (inflammation in deep layers; colon stops working, enlarges, can rupture)","Surgery — Usually for strictures; not curative; recurrence very high — Removing the colon is curative","Both: chronic intestinal inflammation with remissions and flares; cause unknown; autoimmune; no cure (except colectomy for UC). Diarrhea, weight loss, abdominal pain, fever, fatigue. Colon cancer risk increased in both (the lecture said \"no…","During a flare: rest the bowel. NPO with IV hydration, control inflammation, treat infection, correct malnutrition, reduce stress.","Nutrition","Malnutrition: poor intake, high metabolic rate, steroids, malabsorption, protein-losing enteropathy (proteins leak into the GI tract).","When eating resumes: high calorie, high protein, low residue (low fiber keeps the gut calm), plus vitamin and iron supplements.","No universal triggers: keep a food diary. Quit smoking.","Drugs","Sulfasalazine (GI anti-inflammatory) still the drug of choice; aminosalicylates decrease inflammation.","Antimicrobials, steroids (great anti-inflammatories, many side effects), immunosuppressants, immunomodulators (TNF).","UC: drug therapy induces remission 80% of the time; 1 in 5 eventually need surgery. Crohn's: most need surgery at some point.","Specific IBD drugs: not on this instructor's test (they are in pathopharm).","TPN","If the gut works, use it. A comatose patient with a working gut gets tube feedings, not TPN. TPN is for a gut that can't be used (severe flare, severe vomiting, recovering bowel resection).","Total parenteral nutrition is IV and nutritionally complete; positive nitrogen balance while the bowel rests. Not ideal long-term: an unused gut atrophies.","Very hypertonic (stated as D10 or D20, and later D15, D20, D25): needs a central line and a dedicated line. Lipids (milky) run separately.","Blood sugar checks every 6 hours; may need insulin coverage.","Start slowly and stop slowly (for example 10, 20, 30, 50 mL/hr up; 50, 40, 30, 20 down) so the pancreas can adapt.","PPN (partial) exists but is rarely used.","IBD Surgery & Teaching","Total proctocolectomy with ileal pouch-anal reservoir (J pouch) — Most common for UC; two stages. Colon removed, J pouch made, temporary ileostomy while it heals; a couple of months later a takedown and stool passes through the rectum again…","Total proctocolectomy with permanent ileostomy — Very liquid, caustic stool (no water reabsorption).","Continent ileostomy (Kock pouch) — No bag; the patient self-catheterizes the stoma; dressing over it between times.","Crohn's: resection with anastomosis — Unhealthy section removed and healthy bowel sewn together.","Teach rest, diet, perianal care with diarrhea, meds and side effects, signs of recurrence, stress-reducing activities.","After colon surgery, call the provider for new drainage, wound opening or redness, a change in the stoma, no BM or gas for more than 24 hours, increased pain, vomiting, swelling, high ostomy output, dark or no urine, fever, or unable to tak…","Bowel Ostomies","Location tells you the output. Ileostomy: right lower quadrant, very liquid (no time for water reabsorption), caustic, oozes almost continuously, always needs a bag. Left lower quadrant (descending or sigmoid) colostomy: almost like a norma…","End stoma — Bowel cut; the proximal end brought out as one stoma. — Distal portion removed (permanent) or sewn closed and left in as a Hartmann's pouch, so it can be reconnected later (a takedown, a provider decision).","Loop stoma — A loop brought to the skin, slit on the anterior wall, held by a plastic rod. — Proximal opening = stool; distal opening = mucus. Usually temporary.","Double-barrel stoma — Bowel divided; both ends brought out as two separate stomas (no rod). — Proximal = stool; distal = mucous fistula. Usually temporary.","Stoma care","Know whether it's temporary or permanent before you go in.","Assess color, swelling, bleeding.","Mild soap and warm water; skin barrier, then a snug pouch cut to the exact stoma size so no skin touches the drainage.","Empty the bag often; release gas; don't make faces. Ostomy nurses are a great resource.","Other points","A colostomy near the rectum (solid stool) may be irrigated like an enema through the stoma, every day or two.","Old stool and mucus may still pass from the anus.","Teaching may go slowly: a patient not yet willing to look at the stoma at discharge isn't uncommon.","Colorectal Cancer","Risks and signs","Red or processed meat, overweight, inactivity, alcohol, smoking, few fruits and vegetables, IBD history, genetics.","Insidious: often found advanced. Anemia (low H&H), rectal bleeding, abdominal pain, change in bowel habits, obstruction or perforation.","Rectal: discomfort, blood in stool. Descending: bowel change, bright red stool. Ascending: subtler (anemia).","Screening","Colonoscopy is the gold standard: average risk starts at 45, every 10 years; earlier and more often with strong family history or IBD. Remove precancerous polyps.","Fecal occult blood or stool DNA test: less favorable but acceptable, yearly.","Abdominoperineal resection (APR) — Tumor and part of the peritoneum removed; permanent colostomy (descending).","Low anterior resection (LAR) — Two steps with a temporary ostomy and Hartmann's pouch, then reconnected: sphincter preserved, normal control of defecation.","Diverticulosis & Diverticulitis","Diverticulosis (the condition)","Outpouchings of the intestinal mucosa. Usually asymptomatic; maybe pain, bloating, gas, bowel changes.","Prevent: high fiber, low fat, low red meat, activity. With it: keep eating high fiber.","No need to avoid nuts and seeds (no evidence) unless they cause a flare.","Weight reduction; avoid raised intra-abdominal pressure (constipation).","Diverticulitis (the flare)","Left lower quadrant pain, palpable mass, signs of infection (an older adult may only be confused).","NPO to rest the gut; if severe, admit for IV fluids and antibiotics.","Watch for abscess, bleeding, peritonitis (a ruptured diverticulum spills stool into the peritoneum).","Complications may need resection and a temporary ostomy.","Intestinal Obstruction","Mechanical (most obstructions)","Mostly small intestine: adhesions, hernias, strictures.","Surgical adhesions are the number one cause, days to years post-op.","Non-mechanical: paralytic ileus","About 1 in 10. Peristalsis stops, most often after the bowel has been manipulated.","NG to low wall suction and wait for peristalsis, or the patient keeps vomiting.","Care: decompress (NG to low wall suction), correct fluids and electrolytes, pain control, remove the obstruction if needed (bowel resection with end-to-end anastomosis: no stoma)."]},{"s":"Part 7 — Cancer, Liver, Gallbladder & Pancreas▾","a":"part-7-cancer-liver-gallbladder-pancreas","k":["Part 7 — Cancer, Liver, Gallbladder & Pancreas▾","From Cancer, Liver, Gallbladder & Pancreas. Med Surg I (NUR 334) review content. Built from the seven NUR 334 Week 7 voiceovers: cancer overview, cancer treatment side effects, hepatobiliary labs and diagnostics, hepatitis, cirrhosis, chole…","Cancer: Prevention, Diagnosis & Treatment","Prevention and early detection","Avoid carcinogens: excess alcohol, poor diet, tobacco, too much sun.","Diet, exercise, rest; regular exams and screenings; teach self-exams (breast, testicular).","Diagnosis","History; cytology (Pap, sputum); tissue biopsy; labs; chest X-ray; mammogram.","Tumor markers (PSA, CEA): made by tumor cells; used for diagnosis or progression.","Bone marrow exam for cancers that start in the marrow (leukemia, lymphoma).","CAUTION, the seven warning signs: Change in bowel or bladder habits · A sore that does not heal · Unusual bleeding or discharge · Thickening or lump in the breast or elsewhere · Indigestion or difficulty swallowing · Obvious change in a war…","Goals: cure; if not, control; if not, palliative care. Surgery (about half of patients): prophylactic (double mastectomy for a high-risk gene), biopsy, cure, control, debulking (remove part of a tumor for comfort or to help chemo and radiat…","Chemotherapy","Giving chemo","The mainstay of cancer therapy: solid tumors and blood cancers. Systemic.","Must be chemo certified; very toxic; protect patient and nurse; two-RN double check.","Central venous catheters preferred (not required).","Premedicate for nausea and vomiting (and anticipatory nausea and vomiting); anti-inflammatory and anti-allergy meds.","Check WBC, platelets, H&H first: hold chemo if WBCs or platelets are too low.","Nadir","The point after chemo when counts are at their lowest; they then regenerate.","Chemo every two weeks: about day 7, 8, 9 the patient feels worst; counts should recover by the next visit, or treatment is delayed.","Teach: you won't feel well then; limit exposure to other people.","Extravasation (a vesicant infiltrating tissue) can cause necrosis and limb loss. Prevent: watch the site closely, a patent, free-flowing IV with good blood return. Pain is the cardinal symptom, but not always. Blood return lost? Stop the ch…","Radiation","About 60% of patients with cancer get radiation. Local, precisely targeted (chemo is systemic); alone or with chemo or surgery.","External (teletherapy) — Most common; visits for weeks, under half an hour each. — No. No risk to anyone when they leave.","Internal, sealed (brachytherapy) — Radioactive seeds implanted (prostate, breast, lung). — Emits radiation while the implant is active; excretions are not radioactive.","Internal, unsealed (brachytherapy) — Swallowed, for example iodine-131 for thyroid cancer. — Yes: excretions are radioactive (urine, stool, vomit, sweat).","Sealed implant precautions","Time, distance, shielding. Wear your own dosimeter (tracks exposure; no protection; don't share).","Private room, sign on the door.","No pregnant women, no children under 16. Visitors 30 minutes a day, 6 feet from the source.","Dislodged source: forceps into the lead container in the room, never hands.","Save dressings and linens until the source is removed.","Skin teaching (radiated area)","Wash daily with mild soap and water, using the hand, not a washcloth; rinse well.","Don't wash off skin markings.","Pat dry. No powders, lotions or creams unless the radiologist prescribes them.","Soft, loose clothing; no direct sun or temperature extremes.","Dry vs wet desquamation (moist, incredibly tender).","Managing Cancer Treatment Side Effects","Chemo kills rapidly dividing cells: bone marrow, GI tract, hair. Radiation effects depend on where it's aimed (bowel: diarrhea; neck: swallowing and taste).","Greater than 2,500 — Where we like it.","Greater than 1,000 — Low but pretty safe.","Less than 1,000 — Neutropenic.","Less than 500 — Protective precautions.","In cancer care, neutrophils are an actual count (segs plus bands), not a differential percentage. Neutrophils answer acute bacterial infection, so neutropenia = infection risk; filgrastim (a WBC growth factor) may be given.","Neutropenic precautions","Isolation technique, screen visitors, no fresh flowers or plants, monitor for infection.","Diet: avoid raw fruits and vegetables that can't be peeled (berries); peelable ones are OK (oranges, bananas, apples, pineapple); wrapped food and flatware; no raw peanuts or seeds.","Platelets and anemia","Platelets normal 150,000 to 400,000. Serious bleeding may not show until below 50,000; transfuse usually below 20,000.","Anemia: fatigue. Monitor H&H; energy conservation but stay active; iron-rich diet. Epoetin if hemoglobin is less than 11; PRBCs if severe.","GI effects","Anorexia, taste changes (focus on liked foods), nausea and vomiting.","Weigh twice weekly; albumin and prealbumin; small, frequent, high-protein, high-calorie meals; supplements.","Diarrhea is often from localized radiation.","Stomatitis","Inflamed, ulcerated mouth (mucositis is the general term); extremely painful.","Thorough oral assessment; meticulous, gentle brushing and flossing; saline gargles; numbing swish-and-spit mixtures.","No alcohol mouthwash. Artificial saliva; soft, non-irritating foods.","Other effects: fatigue, hair loss (unpredictable; may come back thicker and curly), lung and heart damage, infertility in young people.","Support the right to complementary treatments while encouraging the prescribed plan; a calm, present, healing environment.","Psychosocial: hair or body-part loss, fear of recurrence (\"body vigilant\"), secondary cancers, disability and death. Hope is positive reappraisal; spirituality can help or hurt; social support is one of the most important things.","Hepatobiliary Labs & Diagnostics","Organ shortcuts: kidneys = BUN and creatinine · liver = AST and ALT · pancreas = amylase and lipase.","ALT, AST, alkaline phosphatase — ALT is most specific to the liver; AST and alk phos are nonspecific. Elevated AST and ALT point to liver disease (no need to memorize numbers).","Conjugated (direct) bilirubin — Conjugated by the liver, water soluble, found in urine; elevated in obstructive jaundice (dark urine).","Unconjugated (indirect) bilirubin — Not water soluble, not in urine; elevated with problems in or before the liver.","Serum ammonia — Normally converted to urea in the liver. Buildup crosses the blood-brain barrier: hepatic encephalopathy.","Serum protein / albumin — Albumin is made in the liver: low in liver disease.","Prothrombin time (PT) — In seconds. Liver disease: prolonged PT, bleeding tendency.","Hepatitis antigens / antibodies — The only way to tell the viral hepatitis types apart (genotypes not needed).","Amylase — Digests carbohydrates; 60 to 120. In acute pancreatitis it rises within 12 hours, peaks about 24, normal in 48 to 72.","Lipase — Digests fat; 0 to 160. Rises more slowly and stays elevated longer than amylase.","Not tested: the actual amylase and lipase values (\"the test question will just say that it's elevated\") and the LFT numbers.","Abdominal ultrasound — 95% accurate for gallstones; non-invasive. — NPO 8 hours for the gallbladder.","CT with contrast — Different depths. — Know the BUN and creatinine (contrast can be nephrotoxic); watch for reaction; warn about the warm flush; fluids after.","MRI — — — Not with metal implants or pregnancy.","HIDA scan — Nuclear medicine; done when gallstones are suspected but the ultrasound didn't show them. Shows patency of the common bile duct. — —","ERCP — Scope through the mouth to the duodenum, then backward into the pancreatic, hepatic and common bile ducts. — NPO, consent, sedation. After: vital signs for perforation or infection; most common complication is pancreatitis; nothing b…","Liver biopsy — Very vascular organ. — Before: coags (PT/INR), type and crossmatch, consent, baseline vitals; patient holds breath after expiration for the needle. After: frequent vitals (rising HR first with bleeding); lie on the right side…","Hepatitis","Often no symptoms: 30% of hepatitis B and as much as 80% of hepatitis C. Acute phase lasts 1 to 4 months: vague malaise, flu-like symptoms (myalgia, arthralgia), nausea and vomiting, RUQ discomfort, hepatomegaly, jaundice (icteric), itching…","Transmission — Fecal-oral — Infected blood, high-risk behaviors, perinatal — Similar to B","Symptoms — Mild, self-limiting — Asymptomatic to fulminant (severe liver failure) — Many have none","Chronic? — Never — A small number — Tends to linger","Vaccine — Yes: all children from 12 months, high-risk adults — Yes: three doses, all infants — None","Post-exposure — Vaccine or immune globulin (about 2 months' protection) within 2 weeks — Within 24 hours; no benefit after 7 days — —","Also — Typically no drug therapy — \"Leading cause of cancer\" (as stated). Drugs only after genotyping, for severe liver failure. — Leading indication for liver transplant. Drugs if chronic (most are); genotyped.","Jaundice","Yellow sclera, skin or hard palate; always too much bilirubin; usually present when bilirubin is greater than 2.5. A symptom, not a disease.","Hemolytic: RBC breakdown (wrong blood; check urine for hematuria).","Obstructive: intrahepatic or extrahepatic (a gallstone in the common bile duct). Bilirubin can't reach the gut: clay-colored stools and dark urine.","Care and teaching","No specific treatment: rest, managed at home.","No special diet: well balanced, adequate calories; low fat if fat isn't tolerated; B complex and vitamin K.","Itching: diphenhydramine.","Stop alcohol; avoid hepatotoxic drugs: acetaminophen and isoniazid.","Hygiene; treated or bottled water abroad; don't share linens, utensils, glasses, needles, razors, nail clippers, toothbrushes; condoms; cover cuts. Hep C: never donate blood, organs or tissue. Notify contacts.","Cirrhosis","Early (insidious): weight loss, weakness, GI upset, bowel changes, nausea and vomiting. Late: jaundice, low albumin, prolonged PT, and everything driven by portal hypertension.","Ascites — Portal hypertension plus low albumin (lost \"pulling power\"): fluid third-spaces into the peritoneum.","Bleeding risk — Lack of prothrombin: longer bleeding times.","Splenomegaly — Congestion from portal hypertension (spleen is LUQ; liver mostly RUQ).","Spider angiomas, caput medusae — Engorged vessels from portal hypertension.","Esophageal and anorectal varices — Backed-up, engorged veins; esophageal varices are very dangerous if they bleed.","Hepatic encephalopathy — Too much serum ammonia (worth memorizing): not converted to urea; crosses the blood-brain barrier; sedative. Reversible. Confusion, slowed responses, personality change, ataxia, up to hepatic coma.","Asterixis (\"liver flap\") — Wrists flexed, hands flap involuntarily: elevated ammonia.","Managing ascites and fluid","Rest first.","Fluid and sodium restriction (water follows salt).","Diuretics: spironolactone (potassium sparing) and furosemide (potassium losing), often both.","Albumin to raise oncotic pressure.","Abdominal girth with skin markings, but daily weight is more accurate.","Paracentesis and TIPS","Paracentesis when ascites impairs breathing. Consent, baseline vitals, have the patient void (avoid puncturing a full bladder), supine or high Fowler's. Often repeated in cirrhosis.","TIPS (transjugular intrahepatic portosystemic shunt): a non-surgical stent that lowers portal hypertension, controlling ascites and variceal bleeding.","Nutrition","High calorie, high carbohydrate, moderate to low fat.","Restrict protein only with severe encephalopathy; otherwise protein's benefits outweigh the ammonia risk.","Low sodium with fluid excess and ascites.","Bleeding Precautions","Assess","Coags; oral cavity (gums bleed first); ecchymosis, purpura, petechiae.","Fall prevention: a fall risks head injury and subarachnoid hemorrhage.","Avoid","Aspirin; alcohol, spicy and bulky foods; injections.","Vigorous nose blowing and straining at stool (raises pressure and can rupture varices): stool softeners.","Hard toothbrushes, rectal temps, enemas. Hold pressure longer after an IM shot.","Bleeding esophageal varices are an emergency. Endoscopic sclerotherapy (injected sclerosing agent) or variceal banding (rubber bands around the base). For an active bleed: balloon tamponade (Blakemore tube) applies direct pressure; the pati…","Hepatic Encephalopathy Care","Goal: get the ammonia down.","Control GI bleeding: blood in the gut is nitrogen-rich and raises ammonia.","Avoid constipation. Lactulose titrated to about 2 to 4 stools a day to remove ammonia. Teach why, even if they've already had a BM today.","Monitor level of consciousness and GCS (EMV); safety precautions for confusion.","Cholelithiasis & Cholecystitis","Cholelithiasis = gallstones in the gallbladder; cholecystitis = inflamed gallbladder (acute or chronic). Risks: middle-aged female, fair skin, overweight, high-fat diet, oral contraceptives.","Manifestations","Episodic, vague upper abdominal pain radiating to the right shoulder, triggered by high-fat or large meals.","Nausea, vomiting, indigestion, belching, gas; fever with inflammation.","Jaundice, clay-colored stools, dark urine, steatorrhea: most common with chronic cholecystitis (a stone blocking the common bile duct).","Treatment","Non-surgical: avoid fatty foods; NPO during a flare; opioids, antiemetics, antispasmodics for biliary pain.","Rarely lithotripsy; uncommonly ERCP with sphincterotomy.","Most common: laparoscopic cholecystectomy.","After a lap chole","Remove the puncture-site bandages the day after surgery; may shower.","Call for redness, swelling, purulent or bile-colored drainage, increasing pain, nausea, vomiting, fever, chills.","Gradually increase activity; back to work in about a week.","Low-fat diet tolerated better for the first few weeks; then a regular diet.","Open cholecystectomy and T-tube","Subcostal incision healing by primary intention.","A T-tube keeps the common bile duct patent while inflammation goes down.","Rarely goes home with it: teach emptying the bile bag.","Pancreatitis","Acute: premature activation of pancreatic enzymes that autodigest the pancreas. Gallstones and alcohol are the two most common causes. CT is the best imaging for pancreatitis and its complications.","Severe pain; nausea and vomiting that vomiting doesn't relieve — Distension and peritoneal irritation (visceral pain).","Low-grade temp, high WBC — Inflammation.","Jaundice — Hepatobiliary obstruction.","Paralytic ileus — Peritoneal irritation: NG to low wall suction until it resolves.","Cullen's and Turner's signs — Bluish discoloration from enzyme leak into subcutaneous tissue: Cullen's around the umbilicus, Turner's the other location. Severe disease, poor prognosis.","Hypovolemia, tachycardia — Inflammatory mediators increase vascular permeability.","High amylase and lipase — The pancreatic enzymes.","High triglycerides; hypocalcemia — Fat necrosis (and deposits).","Complications","Pseudocyst: cavity of necrotic products outside the pancreas; often resolves; worst case it perforates into the peritoneum (severe peritonitis).","Abscess: within the pancreas; often needs surgical drainage.","Acute care","Pain relief first (can cause hemodynamic compromise): IV opioids (morphine); knees bent / fetal position.","Labile vitals: monitor often; fluid balance; aggressive IV hydration.","NPO with NG to low wall suction during a flare to rest the pancreas.","Monitor stools for steatorrhea and blood sugar for hyperglycemia (endocrine function).","Quit alcohol. Diet after NPO: carbohydrates encouraged (less stimulating), fat restricted.","Chronic Pancreatitis","Progressive, destructive inflammation and fibrosis with remissions and flares. Intense pain (less tenderness than acute); a mass is likely a pseudocyst or abscess; ascites with possible respiratory compromise; steatorrhea (oily, floating, g…","Opioids given more cautiously (some can increase pain through spasms).","Weight loss can be significant: 4,000 to 6,000 calories a day PO, so TPN may be considered.","Prevent flares: avoid alcohol, caffeine, nicotine, spices; bland, low-fat, high-protein, high-carbohydrate, small high-calorie meals; rest after eating.","Pancreatic enzyme replacement therapy (PERT): pancrelipase","Standard of care to prevent malnutrition, malabsorption and weight loss. Contains amylase, lipase and protease.","Take with the first bite of every meal or snack. Sometimes ordered with an antacid or H2 blocker (pH).","Swallow without chewing; wipe the lips. Powder in applesauce or juice; not mixed with protein foods. Don't crush enteric-coated forms.","Record the number and consistency of stools: goal is fewer, less fatty stools."]},{"s":"Self-Test Flashcards▾","a":"self-test-flashcards","k":["Self-Test Flashcards▾","Mixed across all seven Med Surg I weeks, built around facts that link one week to another. Click a card to flip it."]}]}];
